window.LUMEN_LOAD({"id": "graph-theory", "lessons": {"graphs-intro": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-mathematicians-draw-dots-and-lines\" data-h=\"3\">Why mathematicians draw dots and lines</h3>\n<p>Look at almost anything that involves connections and you will find the same picture underneath. A map of cities joined by roads. A group of friends where some pairs know each other. Web pages linking to other web pages. Atoms bonded in a molecule. Tasks that must happen before other tasks. In every case there are <em>things</em>, and there are <em>relationships between pairs of things</em>. A <strong>graph</strong> is the mathematical object that keeps exactly that information and throws away everything else.</p>\n<p>That act of throwing away is the whole point. When you ask \"can I drive from Pune to Delhi?\", the colour of the road signs and the exact curve of each highway do not matter. All that matters is which cities are directly connected. Once you strip the problem down to dots and lines, one single theory and one single set of algorithms solves the road problem, the friendship problem and the web problem at the same time.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-small-friendship-network\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a small friendship network</h3>\n<p>Imagine five students: Asha, Ben, Chen, Dev and Esi. Asha is friends with Ben and Chen. Ben is also friends with Chen and Dev. Dev is friends with Esi. Nobody else is friends. You can draw this by putting a dot for each person and a line between each pair of friends. You get 5 dots and 5 lines. From the picture you can instantly see things the list hides: Esi is only connected to the group through Dev, and Asha, Ben and Chen form a little triangle where everyone knows everyone.</p>\n<p>That drawing is a graph. The dots are called <strong>vertices</strong> (one vertex, many vertices; some books say <em>nodes</em>) and the lines are called <strong>edges</strong> (some books say <em>links</em> or <em>arcs</em>).</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Graph</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A graph <span class=\"m\">G = (V, E)</span> is a set <span class=\"m\">V</span> of vertices together with a set <span class=\"m\">E</span> of edges, where each edge joins a pair of vertices. We write <span class=\"m\">n = |V|</span> for the number of vertices and <span class=\"m\">m = |E|</span> for the number of edges.</p></div></div>\n<p>In our example <span class=\"m\">V = \\{A, B, C, D, E\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">E = \\{AB, AC, BC, BD, DE\\}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">n = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">m = 5</span>. The edge <span class=\"m\">AB</span> is the same as the edge <span class=\"m\">BA</span>: friendship goes both ways. When two vertices share an edge we call them <strong>adjacent</strong> or <strong>neighbours</strong>, and we say the edge is <strong>incident</strong> to each of its two endpoints.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-drawing-is-not-the-graph\" data-h=\"3\">The drawing is not the graph</h3>\n<p>A very important idea for beginners: the graph is the <em>set of connections</em>, not the picture. You could place Asha on the left or on the right, draw the lines straight or curved, and as long as the same pairs are joined it is the same graph. Two drawings that represent the same connections (possibly after renaming the vertices) are called <strong>isomorphic</strong>. This is why a graph question never depends on lengths or angles in the drawing, unless the problem explicitly adds that information.</p>\n<h3 id=\"simple-graphs-multigraphs-and-loops\" data-h=\"3\">Simple graphs, multigraphs and loops</h3>\n<p>The most common kind of graph is a <strong>simple graph</strong>: no edge from a vertex to itself (a <strong>loop</strong>), and at most one edge between any two vertices. Sometimes you need more. A road map might have two different roads between the same two towns; a graph that allows repeated edges is a <strong>multigraph</strong>. A state machine might have a transition from a state back to itself, which is a loop. Unless a lesson says otherwise, \"graph\" in this course means simple graph.</p>\n<p>How many edges can a simple graph on <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices have? Each edge is a choice of 2 vertices out of <span class=\"m\">n</span>, so the maximum is <span class=\"m\">\\binom n2 = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}</span>. With 5 vertices that is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{5 \\cdot 4}{2} = 10</span> edges. The graph that has every possible edge is the <strong>complete graph</strong> <span class=\"m\">K_n</span>. Our friendship graph uses 5 of the 10 possible edges.</p>\n<h3 id=\"adding-information-weights-and-directions\" data-h=\"3\">Adding information: weights and directions</h3>\n<p>Two common upgrades turn a plain graph into a richer model. In a <strong>weighted graph</strong> each edge carries a number, such as the length of a road in kilometres, the cost of a flight, or the strength of a connection. In a <strong>directed graph</strong> (or <em>digraph</em>) each edge has a direction, drawn as an arrow from <span class=\"m\">u</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span>. Directed edges model one-way relationships: \"page <span class=\"m\">u</span> links to page <span class=\"m\">v</span>\", \"task <span class=\"m\">u</span> must happen before task <span class=\"m\">v</span>\", \"person <span class=\"m\">u</span> follows person <span class=\"m\">v</span>\". On a directed graph <span class=\"m\">u \\to v</span> and <span class=\"m\">v \\to u</span> are different edges. We will study directed graphs in detail in their own unit.</p>\n<h3 id=\"storing-a-graph-in-a-computer\" data-h=\"3\">Storing a graph in a computer</h3>\n<p>A computer cannot look at a drawing, so we need a data structure. There are two standard choices.</p>\n<p>An <strong>adjacency list</strong> stores, for each vertex, a list of its neighbours. For the friendship graph: A: [B, C]; B: [A, C, D]; C: [A, B]; D: [B, E]; E: [D]. The total length of all lists is <span class=\"m\">2m = 10</span>, because each edge appears once in each endpoint's list. Memory use is proportional to <span class=\"m\">n + m</span>, which is small for <strong>sparse</strong> graphs (graphs with far fewer edges than the maximum).</p>\n<p>An <strong>adjacency matrix</strong> is an <span class=\"m\">n \\times n</span> table <span class=\"m\">A</span> where <span class=\"m\">A_{ij} = 1</span> if vertices <span class=\"m\">i</span> and <span class=\"m\">j</span> are adjacent and <span class=\"m\">0</span> otherwise. Numbering A to E as 1 to 5, row 2 (Ben) reads <span class=\"m\">[1, 0, 1, 1, 0]</span>. For an undirected graph the matrix is <strong>symmetric</strong>: <span class=\"m\">A_{ij} = A_{ji}</span>. The diagonal is all zeros in a simple graph because there are no loops. The matrix uses <span class=\"m\">n^2</span> memory no matter how few edges there are, but it answers \"are <span class=\"m\">i</span> and <span class=\"m\">j</span> adjacent?\" in a single lookup. It also lets you use linear algebra on graphs, which turns out to be extremely powerful (see the last unit).</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;envelope&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;degree&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>A small graph. Each label shows how many edges touch that vertex.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The first mistake is counting an undirected edge twice when you count edges. The edge <span class=\"m\">AB</span> is one edge even though it shows up in both A's and B's adjacency lists. The second mistake is thinking two drawings are different graphs because they look different; always compare the edge sets. The third is forgetting that a graph may have vertices with no edges at all (isolated vertices). They still belong to <span class=\"m\">V</span> and still count in <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A graph is a set of vertices plus a set of edges joining pairs of vertices. It captures connections and nothing else, so the same mathematics applies to roads, friendships, web links and molecules. Simple graphs have no loops or repeated edges and at most <span class=\"m\">\\binom n2</span> edges. Weights add numbers to edges, directions add arrows. Computers store graphs as adjacency lists (memory about <span class=\"m\">n + m</span>) or adjacency matrices (memory <span class=\"m\">n^2</span>, instant edge lookup).</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;k4&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;degree&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Switch graphs, then use \"Edit edges\" to add and remove connections.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose the graph <code>k4</code>. Count its edges and check that it is <span class=\"m\">\\binom42 = 6</span>.\n2. Switch to \"Edit edges\", click vertex 0 then vertex 2 to delete that edge, and watch the edge count drop.\n3. Click on empty space to add a new isolated vertex. Notice that <span class=\"m\">n</span> grows but <span class=\"m\">m</span> does not.\n4. Go back to \"Explore\" and drag vertices around. The degrees and edge count never change.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> moving vertices changes the drawing but not the graph, exactly as the article said. Only adding or removing edges changes the graph. The complete graph is the one where every pair is already joined, so in edit mode you can only remove edges from it.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">G = (V, E), \\qquad n = |V|, \\qquad m = |E|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{simple graph: } 0 \\le m \\le \\binom n2 = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A_{ij} = \\begin{cases}1 &amp; ij \\in E\\\\ 0 &amp; \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}, \\qquad A = A^\\top \\text{ for undirected graphs}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Number of edges of <span class=\"m\">K_n</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The complete graph <span class=\"m\">K_n</span> has exactly <span class=\"m\">\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}</span> edges, and no simple graph on <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices has more.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An edge of a simple graph is determined by an unordered pair of distinct vertices, and different edges use different pairs. There are <span class=\"m\">\\binom n2 = \\frac{n(n-1)}2</span> unordered pairs, so <span class=\"m\">m \\le \\frac{n(n-1)}2</span>, and <span class=\"m\">K_n</span> uses all of them.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Write the adjacency list and matrix for <span class=\"m\">V = \\{1,2,3,4\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">E = \\{12, 13, 34\\}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Neighbours: 1 is joined to 2 and 3; 2 to 1; 3 to 1 and 4; 4 to 3.</li>\n<li>Adjacency list: 1: [2, 3]; 2: [1]; 3: [1, 4]; 4: [3]. Total length <span class=\"m\">6 = 2 \\times 3</span> edges.</li>\n<li>Matrix rows: row 1 <span class=\"m\">= [0,1,1,0]</span>, row 2 <span class=\"m\">= [1,0,0,0]</span>, row 3 <span class=\"m\">= [1,0,0,1]</span>, row 4 <span class=\"m\">= [0,0,1,0]</span>.</li>\n<li>Check: the matrix is symmetric, has zeros on the diagonal, and contains <span class=\"m\">2m = 6</span> ones.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How full is a graph with 20 vertices and 38 edges?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Maximum possible edges: <span class=\"m\">\\binom{20}2 = \\frac{20 \\cdot 19}{2} = 190</span>.</li>\n<li>Fraction present (the <strong>density</strong>): <span class=\"m\">\\frac{38}{190} = 0.2</span>, so 20% of possible edges exist.</li>\n<li>Adjacency matrix: <span class=\"m\">20^2 = 400</span> entries. Adjacency list: about <span class=\"m\">n + 2m = 20 + 76 = 96</span> entries. The list is smaller.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A simple graph has 8 vertices. (a) What is the largest possible number of edges? (b) If the graph has 12 edges, how many 1s are in its adjacency matrix? (c) How many entries of the matrix are 0?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">\\binom82 = 28</span>. (b) Each edge gives two 1s (<span class=\"m\">A_{ij}</span> and <span class=\"m\">A_{ji}</span>), so <span class=\"m\">2 \\times 12 = 24</span>. (c) The matrix has <span class=\"m\">8^2 = 64</span> entries, so <span class=\"m\">64 - 24 = 40</span> are 0 (including the 8 diagonal entries).</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Almost every large system stores graphs as adjacency lists because real networks are sparse: a social network may have a billion users but each user has only a few hundred friends. Road-routing services, package managers (dependency graphs), compilers (control-flow graphs) and databases (query plans) are all graph programs underneath.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Graph neural networks, knowledge graphs and recommendation systems all start from an adjacency matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span>. Many ML formulas are written with <span class=\"m\">A</span> directly, for example a graph convolution layer computes <span class=\"m\">H' = \\sigma(\\hat A H W)</span>, where <span class=\"m\">\\hat A</span> is a normalised adjacency matrix.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A metro map is a graph: stations are vertices and track segments are edges. The map designer deliberately distorts distances and angles (the drawing) but keeps the connections (the graph) exact, because the connections are all a traveller needs.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">A simple graph has 6 vertices. What is the maximum number of edges it can have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"15\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\binom62 = \\frac{6\\cdot5}{2} = 15</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">In an undirected graph's adjacency matrix, which property always holds?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">every row sums to 1</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the matrix is symmetric</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the diagonal is all 1s</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it has exactly <span class=\"m\">n</span> ones</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Edge <span class=\"m\">ij</span> is the same as edge <span class=\"m\">ji</span>, so <span class=\"m\">A_{ij} = A_{ji}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Two drawings have the same vertices and the same pairs joined, but one uses curved lines. Are they the same graph?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A graph is its vertex and edge sets. The drawing style is irrelevant.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">A graph has 10 vertices and 9 edges. What is the total length of all its adjacency lists?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"18\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each edge appears in two lists, so the total is <span class=\"m\">2m = 18</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which model fits \"task <span class=\"m\">u</span> must be finished before task <span class=\"m\">v</span>\"?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">an undirected graph</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a directed graph</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a complete graph</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The relationship has a direction, so the edge is an arrow <span class=\"m\">u \\to v</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "degree-handshake": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"counting-connections-at-one-vertex\" data-h=\"3\">Counting connections at one vertex</h3>\n<p>Once you have a graph, the most natural question about a single vertex is \"how connected is it?\". In a friendship network, that is \"how many friends does this person have?\". In a road network, \"how many roads leave this junction?\". This number is called the <strong>degree</strong> of the vertex. It is the simplest statistic in graph theory and, surprisingly, it already leads to a real theorem with a clever proof.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Degree</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The degree <span class=\"m\">\\deg(v)</span> of a vertex <span class=\"m\">v</span> is the number of edges incident to it. In a simple graph this equals the number of neighbours of <span class=\"m\">v</span>. A vertex of degree 0 is <strong>isolated</strong>; a vertex of degree 1 is a <strong>leaf</strong> (or pendant vertex).</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-room-full-of-handshakes\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a room full of handshakes</h3>\n<p>Imagine a party where some pairs of guests shake hands. Afterwards, every guest writes down how many hands they shook, and you add up all those numbers. Here is the surprise: the total is always even. In fact it is exactly twice the number of handshakes. Why? Every single handshake involves two hands. When you add up everybody's count, each handshake gets counted once by each of its two participants. So the total is <span class=\"m\">2 \\times</span> (number of handshakes).</p>\n<p>Translate guests into vertices and handshakes into edges and you get the <strong>handshake lemma</strong>: the sum of all degrees equals twice the number of edges.\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_{v \\in V} \\deg(v) = 2m</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"checking-it-on-a-small-example\" data-h=\"3\">Checking it on a small example</h3>\n<p>Take the friendship graph from the previous lesson: edges <span class=\"m\">AB, AC, BC, BD, DE</span>. The degrees are <span class=\"m\">\\deg A = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\deg B = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\deg C = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\deg D = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\deg E = 1</span>. Their sum is <span class=\"m\">2 + 3 + 2 + 2 + 1 = 10</span>, and <span class=\"m\">2m = 2 \\times 5 = 10</span>. It works.</p>\n<p>The proof idea, counting the same thing in two ways, is called <strong>double counting</strong>. We counted pairs (vertex, edge touching it). Counting by vertices gives the sum of degrees. Counting by edges gives 2 per edge, because each edge touches exactly two vertices. Two correct counts of the same set must agree.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-consequence-that-feels-like-magic\" data-h=\"3\">A consequence that feels like magic</h3>\n<p>Because the degree sum is even, the number of vertices with an <strong>odd</strong> degree must itself be even. Here is why. Split the sum into the odd degrees and the even degrees. The even degrees add to something even. The total is even. So the odd degrees must also add to something even, and a sum of odd numbers is even only when there is an even number of them.</p>\n<p>This lets you answer questions instantly. Can 7 people at a party each shake exactly 3 hands? That would be 7 vertices of odd degree 3, an odd count of odd vertices, which is impossible. Equivalently, the degree sum would be <span class=\"m\">7 \\times 3 = 21</span>, which is odd, but it must equal <span class=\"m\">2m</span>. So no such party exists.</p>\n<h3 id=\"degree-sequences\" data-h=\"3\">Degree sequences</h3>\n<p>If you list all the degrees, usually from largest to smallest, you get the <strong>degree sequence</strong>. For the friendship graph it is <span class=\"m\">(3, 2, 2, 2, 1)</span>. Not every list of numbers is a degree sequence. A list is called <strong>graphical</strong> if some simple graph has exactly those degrees. Two quick tests rule many lists out: the sum must be even, and in a simple graph on <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices no degree can exceed <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> (you can be adjacent to at most every other vertex). For example <span class=\"m\">(4, 4, 1, 1)</span> fails the second test: with 4 vertices the largest possible degree is 3. A full test (the Havel–Hakimi algorithm) appears in the worked examples.</p>\n<p>We also name the extremes: <span class=\"m\">\\Delta(G)</span> is the <strong>maximum degree</strong> and <span class=\"m\">\\delta(G)</span> is the <strong>minimum degree</strong>. Since the average degree is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{2m}{n}</span>, we always have <span class=\"m\">\\delta \\le \\frac{2m}{n} \\le \\Delta</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-gallery-of-special-graphs\" data-h=\"3\">A gallery of special graphs</h3>\n<p>A few graphs appear so often that they have names.</p>\n<p>The <strong>complete graph</strong> <span class=\"m\">K_n</span> has every possible edge. Each vertex is adjacent to the other <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span>, so every degree is <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span>. Handshake check: <span class=\"m\">n(n-1) = 2m</span>, so <span class=\"m\">m = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}</span>, matching the previous lesson.</p>\n<p>The <strong>cycle</strong> <span class=\"m\">C_n</span> (for <span class=\"m\">n \\ge 3</span>) is <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices arranged in a ring, each joined to the next and the last joined back to the first. Every degree is 2 and <span class=\"m\">m = n</span>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>path</strong> <span class=\"m\">P_n</span> is <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices in a line. The two ends have degree 1 and the inside vertices have degree 2, so the degree sum is <span class=\"m\">2 + 2(n-2) = 2n - 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">m = n - 1</span>.</p>\n<p>A graph where every vertex has the same degree <span class=\"m\">k</span> is called <strong><span class=\"m\">k</span>-regular</strong>. <span class=\"m\">K_n</span> is <span class=\"m\">(n-1)</span>-regular and <span class=\"m\">C_n</span> is 2-regular. The famous <strong>Petersen graph</strong> is 3-regular with 10 vertices. For a <span class=\"m\">k</span>-regular graph the handshake lemma gives <span class=\"m\">nk = 2m</span>, so <span class=\"m\">m = \\frac{nk}{2}</span>, and <span class=\"m\">nk</span> must be even.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;petersen&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;degree&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The Petersen graph: 10 vertices, every degree is 3, so it has 15 edges.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"degrees-in-directed-graphs\" data-h=\"3\">Degrees in directed graphs</h3>\n<p>In a directed graph each vertex has an <strong>in-degree</strong> (arrows coming in) and an <strong>out-degree</strong> (arrows going out). Each arrow adds 1 to exactly one out-degree and 1 to exactly one in-degree, so\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_v \\deg^-(v) = \\sum_v \\deg^+(v) = m.</span>\nOn Twitter-style networks, your in-degree is your follower count and your out-degree is how many accounts you follow.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Students often forget the factor of 2 and write <span class=\"m\">\\sum \\deg(v) = m</span>. Remember the handshake: two hands per shake. Another mistake is assuming a list with an even sum must be graphical; <span class=\"m\">(3, 3, 0, 0)</span> has an even sum but two vertices cannot each have 3 neighbours when only one other vertex is available to them. Finally, in multigraphs a loop adds 2 to the degree of its vertex, not 1, which keeps the handshake lemma true.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The degree of a vertex is how many edges touch it. Adding all degrees counts every edge twice, so <span class=\"m\">\\sum \\deg(v) = 2m</span>, and consequently the number of odd-degree vertices is even. Degree sequences summarise a graph, but not every list is achievable. Regular graphs, complete graphs, cycles and paths have simple degree patterns that make edge counts immediate.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;envelope&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;degree&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The readout checks the handshake lemma live. Odd-degree vertices are ringed.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the <code>envelope</code> graph, check the readout: the degree sum equals twice the edge count.\n2. Switch to \"Edit edges\" and add an edge between vertices 0 and 4. Watch two degrees change by 1 each and the sum change by 2.\n3. Try to make the number of ringed (odd) vertices equal to 1 or 3. You cannot: every edge change flips the parity of exactly two vertices.\n4. Load <code>petersen</code>, <code>cube</code> and <code>c6</code> and read off their degree sequences. Which ones are regular?</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> every edge you add or delete changes the degree sum by exactly 2. That is the handshake lemma in action, and it is also why odd vertices always come in pairs.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\sum_{v\\in V}\\deg(v) = 2m \\qquad \\text{(handshake lemma)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\{v : \\deg(v) \\text{ odd}\\} \\text{ is even}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\delta(G) \\le \\frac{2m}{n} \\le \\Delta(G)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">k\\text{-regular: } m = \\frac{nk}{2}; \\quad K_n: m = \\frac{n(n-1)}2; \\quad C_n: m = n; \\quad P_n: m = n-1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{directed: } \\sum_v \\deg^+(v) = \\sum_v \\deg^-(v) = m</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Handshake lemma</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In every graph, <span class=\"m\">\\sum_{v \\in V} \\deg(v) = 2|E|</span>. Hence every graph has an even number of vertices of odd degree.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Count the pairs <span class=\"m\">(v, e)</span> with <span class=\"m\">v</span> an endpoint of edge <span class=\"m\">e</span>. Grouping by vertex gives <span class=\"m\">\\sum_v \\deg(v)</span>. Grouping by edge gives exactly 2 per edge, so <span class=\"m\">2|E|</span>. For the corollary, write the sum as (sum of odd degrees) + (sum of even degrees). The total and the even part are even, so the odd part is even, which requires an even number of odd terms.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Is <span class=\"m\">(3, 3, 2, 2, 2)</span> a degree sequence? (Havel–Hakimi)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>The sum is <span class=\"m\">12</span>, which is even, and no entry exceeds <span class=\"m\">n - 1 = 4</span>. Keep going.</li>\n<li>Remove the first entry (3) and subtract 1 from the next 3 entries: <span class=\"m\">(3, 2, 2, 2) \\to (2, 1, 1, 2)</span>. Re-sort: <span class=\"m\">(2, 2, 1, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li>Remove 2 and subtract 1 from the next 2: <span class=\"m\">(2, 1, 1) \\to (1, 0, 1)</span>. Re-sort: <span class=\"m\">(1, 1, 0)</span>.</li>\n<li>Remove 1 and subtract 1 from the next 1: <span class=\"m\">(1, 0) \\to (0, 0)</span>. All zeros, so the original list is graphical.</li>\n<li>A graph realising it: a 5-cycle <span class=\"m\">1\\text{-}2\\text{-}3\\text{-}4\\text{-}5\\text{-}1</span> plus the chord <span class=\"m\">1\\text{-}3</span>, giving degrees <span class=\"m\">3,2,3,2,2</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A 4-regular graph has 15 vertices. How many edges?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Handshake: sum of degrees <span class=\"m\">= 15 \\times 4 = 60</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">2m = 60</span>, so <span class=\"m\">m = 30</span>.</li>\n<li>Sanity check: a 3-regular graph on 15 vertices would need <span class=\"m\">15 \\times 3 = 45 = 2m</span>, which is odd, so it cannot exist.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A graph has 9 vertices. Four of them have degree 3, three have degree 4 and the rest have degree 2. (a) How many edges does it have? (b) Could one more vertex of degree 1 be added to it (by joining it to an existing vertex) without anything else changing? Explain.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) The remaining <span class=\"m\">9 - 4 - 3 = 2</span> vertices have degree 2. Sum <span class=\"m\">= 4(3) + 3(4) + 2(2) = 12 + 12 + 4 = 28 = 2m</span>, so <span class=\"m\">m = 14</span>. (b) No. Joining a new leaf to an existing vertex also raises that vertex's degree by 1, so \"nothing else changing\" is impossible: one edge always changes two degrees. (The sum would go from 28 to 30, still even.)</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Degree is the first thing computed in network analysis. \"Degree centrality\" ranks users, servers or airports by how many direct connections they have, and the degree distribution of the web or a social network (very heavy-tailed: a few hubs with enormous degree) drives how caches, load balancers and crawlers are designed.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In graph neural networks the degree matrix <span class=\"m\">D = \\mathrm{diag}(\\deg(v_1), \\dots, \\deg(v_n))</span> normalises messages: the common GCN layer uses <span class=\"m\">D^{-1/2} A D^{-1/2}</span> so that high-degree nodes do not overwhelm their neighbours. Node degree is also one of the simplest and most useful node features.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Airline route maps are graphs where airports are vertices. Hub airports like Dubai or Atlanta have huge degree. By the handshake lemma, if you add up the number of routes listed at every airport you get exactly twice the number of routes, since each route is listed at both ends.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A graph has degree sequence <span class=\"m\">(4, 3, 3, 2, 2)</span>. How many edges does it have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The degree sum is <span class=\"m\">14 = 2m</span>, so <span class=\"m\">m = 7</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Can a graph have exactly 5 vertices of odd degree?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The number of odd-degree vertices is always even.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many edges does a 3-regular graph on 12 vertices have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"18\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">m = \\frac{nk}2 = \\frac{12\\cdot3}2 = 18</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is every vertex's degree in <span class=\"m\">K_7</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each vertex is adjacent to all <span class=\"m\">n - 1 = 6</span> others.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which list can NOT be the degree sequence of a simple graph?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(2, 2, 2)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(1, 1, 1, 1)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">(3, 1, 1)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(2, 1, 1)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>With only 3 vertices, no vertex can have degree 3: the maximum is <span class=\"m\">n - 1 = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In a directed graph with 20 arrows, what is the sum of all in-degrees?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"20\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each arrow contributes exactly 1 to some in-degree.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "paths-connectivity": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"getting-from-here-to-there\" data-h=\"3\">Getting from here to there</h3>\n<p>The reason we draw graphs is usually to travel on them. A delivery driver wants to get from the depot to a customer. A rumour spreads from one friend to the next. A data packet hops from router to router across the internet. All of these are journeys along edges, and graph theory has precise words for different kinds of journeys. Getting those words right matters, because many theorems are true for one kind of journey and false for another.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-walking-around-a-town\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine walking around a town</h3>\n<p>Imagine a small town drawn as a graph: junctions are vertices, streets are edges. You leave your house and wander: house, bakery, park, bakery, school. That sequence is a <strong>walk</strong>. You are allowed to revisit places and even walk the same street twice. A walk is simply a sequence of vertices where each consecutive pair is joined by an edge. Its <strong>length</strong> is the number of edges used, so house, bakery, park, bakery, school has length 4.</p>\n<p>Now add restrictions. If you never walk down the same street twice (but you may pass through the same junction again) the walk is a <strong>trail</strong>. If you never visit the same junction twice, it is a <strong>path</strong>. A path is the most efficient kind of journey, because it never comes back on itself. Every path is a trail and every trail is a walk, but not the other way round.</p>\n<p>A walk that ends where it started is <strong>closed</strong>. A closed trail with at least one edge is a <strong>circuit</strong>, and a closed walk that repeats no vertex except the start/end (and has length at least 3 in a simple graph) is a <strong>cycle</strong>. In the town, house, bakery, park, house would be a cycle of length 3, also called a triangle.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Path</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A walk <span class=\"m\">v_0, v_1, \\dots, v_k</span> in which all vertices are distinct. Its length is <span class=\"m\">k</span>, the number of edges.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cycle</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A closed walk <span class=\"m\">v_0, v_1, \\dots, v_k = v_0</span> with <span class=\"m\">k \\ge 3</span> in which <span class=\"m\">v_0, \\dots, v_{k-1}</span> are all distinct.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"walks-always-contain-paths\" data-h=\"3\">Walks always contain paths</h3>\n<p>Here is a small but very useful fact: if there is any walk from <span class=\"m\">u</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span>, there is a path from <span class=\"m\">u</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span>. The idea is to cut out detours. Suppose the walk visits some vertex <span class=\"m\">x</span> twice: <span class=\"m\">u, \\dots, x, \\dots, x, \\dots, v</span>. Delete everything between the two visits to <span class=\"m\">x</span>. The result is still a walk from <span class=\"m\">u</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span>, but shorter. Repeat until no vertex repeats, and you have a path. This is why, when we ask \"can I get from <span class=\"m\">u</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span>?\", it does not matter whether we mean walk or path.</p>\n<h3 id=\"distance\" data-h=\"3\">Distance</h3>\n<p>The <strong>distance</strong> <span class=\"m\">d(u, v)</span> is the length of a shortest path from <span class=\"m\">u</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span>. If no path exists we say <span class=\"m\">d(u,v) = \\infty</span>. Distance behaves like distance on a map: <span class=\"m\">d(u,u) = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">d(u,v) = d(v,u)</span> in an undirected graph, and the <strong>triangle inequality</strong> holds: <span class=\"m\">d(u,w) \\le d(u,v) + d(v,w)</span>, because you can always reach <span class=\"m\">w</span> by going through <span class=\"m\">v</span>. The largest distance between any two vertices is the <strong>diameter</strong> of the graph. Facebook measured its friendship graph and found the average distance between two users was about 4.7, the famous \"six degrees of separation\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"connected-graphs-and-components\" data-h=\"3\">Connected graphs and components</h3>\n<p>A graph is <strong>connected</strong> if every pair of vertices has a path between them. If not, the graph falls into separate pieces. Being joined by a path is an equivalence relation (reflexive, symmetric, transitive), so the vertices split into equivalence classes called <strong>connected components</strong>. Each component is a maximal connected piece: you can travel freely inside it but never leave it.</p>\n<p>How few edges can a connected graph have? Think of building the graph by adding edges one at a time to <span class=\"m\">n</span> isolated vertices. At the start there are <span class=\"m\">n</span> components. Each new edge can join at most two components into one, so it reduces the count by at most 1. To get down to 1 component you need at least <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges. So <strong>a connected graph on <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices has at least <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges</strong>. A path <span class=\"m\">P_n</span> shows this is the best possible. More generally, a graph with <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices, <span class=\"m\">m</span> edges and <span class=\"m\">c</span> components satisfies <span class=\"m\">m \\ge n - c</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"weak-spots-bridges-and-cut-vertices\" data-h=\"3\">Weak spots: bridges and cut vertices</h3>\n<p>Some edges are more important than others. A <strong>bridge</strong> is an edge whose removal increases the number of components. A <strong>cut vertex</strong> (or articulation point) is a vertex whose removal, together with its edges, increases the number of components. In a computer network these are single points of failure: if that one cable or that one router dies, part of the network is cut off.</p>\n<p>There is a neat characterisation: an edge is a bridge exactly when it lies on no cycle. If an edge <span class=\"m\">uv</span> is on a cycle, then after deleting it you can still get from <span class=\"m\">u</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span> the long way around the cycle, so nothing disconnects. If it is on no cycle, there is no other route, so deleting it separates <span class=\"m\">u</span> from <span class=\"m\">v</span>.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;bridges&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;components&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Two triangles joined by a bridge, plus a pendant edge. Red dashed edges are bridges; ringed vertices are cut vertices.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"counting-walks-with-matrix-powers\" data-h=\"3\">Counting walks with matrix powers</h3>\n<p>The adjacency matrix has a beautiful hidden property. The entry <span class=\"m\">(A^2)_{ij} = \\sum_k A_{ik}A_{kj}</span> adds 1 for every middle vertex <span class=\"m\">k</span> that is adjacent to both <span class=\"m\">i</span> and <span class=\"m\">j</span>. That is exactly the number of walks of length 2 from <span class=\"m\">i</span> to <span class=\"m\">j</span>. The same argument repeated shows that <span class=\"m\">(A^k)_{ij}</span> counts walks of length <span class=\"m\">k</span> from <span class=\"m\">i</span> to <span class=\"m\">j</span>. For example, on the diagonal, <span class=\"m\">(A^2)_{ii} = \\deg(i)</span>, because a walk of length 2 from <span class=\"m\">i</span> back to <span class=\"m\">i</span> goes out along an edge and straight back. And <span class=\"m\">(A^3)_{ii}</span> is twice the number of triangles containing <span class=\"m\">i</span> (each triangle can be walked in two directions). So <span class=\"m\">d(i,j)</span> is the smallest <span class=\"m\">k</span> with <span class=\"m\">(A^k)_{ij} &gt; 0</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Do not confuse path and walk: \"the number of paths of length 3\" and \"the number of walks of length 3\" are different numbers, and only walks are counted by <span class=\"m\">A^3</span>. Remember the length of a path counts edges, not vertices: a path through 4 vertices has length 3. Finally, a graph with <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges need not be connected (a triangle plus an isolated vertex has 3 edges and 4 vertices); the theorem only says connected graphs need at least that many.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Walks may repeat anything, trails may not repeat edges, and paths may not repeat vertices. Any walk can be shortened to a path. Distance is the length of a shortest path and obeys the triangle inequality. Connectivity splits a graph into components, a connected graph needs at least <span class=\"m\">n-1</span> edges, and bridges (edges on no cycle) and cut vertices are its weak points. Powers of the adjacency matrix count walks.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;bridges&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;components&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Edit the graph and watch bridges and cut vertices appear and disappear.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <code>bridges</code> loaded, read the bridges (2–3 and 5–6) and cut vertices (2, 3, 5).\n2. Switch to \"Edit edges\" and add the edge 6–4. The pendant edge 5–6 is now on the cycle 4–5–6, so it stops being a bridge.\n3. Add an edge 1–4. Now the edge 2–3 lies on a cycle too, and the graph has no bridges at all.\n4. Change \"Ask\" to \"Counting walks\", set <span class=\"m\">k = 2</span> and click vertex 3. The label on vertex 3 itself equals <span class=\"m\">\\deg(3)</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> a bridge disappears exactly when you create a second route around it, which is the \"bridge = edge on no cycle\" theorem. In walk mode, the diagonal entry of <span class=\"m\">A^2</span> is always the degree, and the first <span class=\"m\">k</span> that makes a label non-zero is the distance.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{walk} \\supseteq \\text{trail} \\supseteq \\text{path}; \\qquad \\text{length} = \\text{number of edges}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">d(u,w) \\le d(u,v) + d(v,w)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{connected} \\Rightarrow m \\ge n - 1; \\qquad \\text{in general } m \\ge n - c</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(A^k)_{ij} = \\#\\{\\text{walks of length } k \\text{ from } i \\text{ to } j\\}, \\qquad (A^2)_{ii} = \\deg(i), \\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(A^3) = 6 \\cdot \\#\\text{triangles}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Walks contain paths</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If a graph has a walk from <span class=\"m\">u</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span>, then it has a path from <span class=\"m\">u</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span>, of length at most the walk's length.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Take a shortest walk <span class=\"m\">W</span> from <span class=\"m\">u</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span>. If some vertex <span class=\"m\">x</span> appeared twice in <span class=\"m\">W</span>, deleting the closed section between the two copies of <span class=\"m\">x</span> would give a strictly shorter walk from <span class=\"m\">u</span> to <span class=\"m\">v</span>, contradicting minimality. So no vertex repeats and <span class=\"m\">W</span> is a path.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Bridges</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An edge <span class=\"m\">e</span> of a graph is a bridge if and only if <span class=\"m\">e</span> lies on no cycle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">e = uv</span>. If <span class=\"m\">e</span> lies on a cycle, the rest of the cycle is a <span class=\"m\">u</span>–<span class=\"m\">v</span> path avoiding <span class=\"m\">e</span>, so any walk using <span class=\"m\">e</span> can be rerouted and removing <span class=\"m\">e</span> disconnects nothing. Conversely, if removing <span class=\"m\">e</span> leaves <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span> connected by some path <span class=\"m\">Q</span>, then <span class=\"m\">Q</span> plus <span class=\"m\">e</span> is a cycle through <span class=\"m\">e</span>. So <span class=\"m\">e</span> is not a bridge exactly when it lies on a cycle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Walk counting</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For every <span class=\"m\">k \\ge 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">(A^k)_{ij}</span> equals the number of walks of length <span class=\"m\">k</span> from <span class=\"m\">i</span> to <span class=\"m\">j</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Induction on <span class=\"m\">k</span>. For <span class=\"m\">k = 1</span> it is the definition of <span class=\"m\">A</span>. If it holds for <span class=\"m\">k</span>, then <span class=\"m\">(A^{k+1})_{ij} = \\sum_t (A^k)_{it} A_{tj}</span>. Each walk of length <span class=\"m\">k+1</span> from <span class=\"m\">i</span> to <span class=\"m\">j</span> is a walk of length <span class=\"m\">k</span> from <span class=\"m\">i</span> to some <span class=\"m\">t</span> followed by an edge <span class=\"m\">tj</span>, and the sum counts exactly these.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Count walks of length 2 and 3 in the triangle <span class=\"m\">K_3</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0&amp;1\\\\1&amp;1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A^2 = \\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;1&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}</span>: the diagonal is each degree (2), and there is 1 walk of length 2 between different vertices (through the third vertex).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A^3 = A^2 A = \\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;3&amp;3\\\\3&amp;2&amp;3\\\\3&amp;3&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}</span>. The diagonal 2 counts the triangle walked in two directions.</li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(A^3) = 6 = 6 \\times 1</span> triangle.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A graph has 12 vertices, 15 edges and 4 components. What is the smallest number of extra edges that makes it connected?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Each new edge can merge at most two components.</li>\n<li>Going from 4 components to 1 needs at least <span class=\"m\">4 - 1 = 3</span> merges.</li>\n<li>3 edges suffice: join one vertex of component 1 to each of components 2, 3 and 4.</li>\n<li>Answer: 3.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">G</span> be the path <span class=\"m\">1 - 2 - 3 - 4</span>. (a) Write <span class=\"m\">A</span>. (b) Compute the number of walks of length 3 from vertex 1 to vertex 4, and from 1 to 2. (c) Which edges are bridges?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Rows: <span class=\"m\">[0,1,0,0], [1,0,1,0], [0,1,0,1], [0,0,1,0]</span>. (b) From 1 to 4, the only walk of length 3 is <span class=\"m\">1,2,3,4</span>, so 1. From 1 to 2: walks <span class=\"m\">1,2,1,2</span> and <span class=\"m\">1,2,3,2</span>, so 2. (You can check with <span class=\"m\">(A^3)_{14} = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">(A^3)_{12} = 2</span>.) (c) Every edge is a bridge, because a path has no cycles.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Finding connected components is a basic step in image processing (labelling blobs of pixels), in garbage collection (which objects are reachable from the program's roots), and in network reliability. Tarjan's algorithm finds all bridges and cut vertices in <span class=\"m\">O(n + m)</span> time using depth-first search.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Many graph features used in ML are walk counts: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(A^3)/6</span> counts triangles, a key measure of how clustered a social network is. Graph neural networks with <span class=\"m\">k</span> layers combine information from walks of length up to <span class=\"m\">k</span>, which is why a 2-layer GNN can only \"see\" a vertex's 2-hop neighbourhood.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Power grids and airline networks are analysed for bridges and cut vertices: a substation that is a cut vertex is a single point of failure for every town on the far side. Engineers add redundant lines precisely to put every important edge on a cycle.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which journey may repeat vertices but not edges?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a path</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a trail</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a cycle</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Trails forbid repeated edges only. Paths forbid repeated vertices.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">A connected graph has 25 vertices. What is the smallest number of edges it can have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"24\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A connected graph needs at least <span class=\"m\">n - 1 = 24</span> edges.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">An edge lies on a cycle. Can it be a bridge?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Bridges are exactly the edges on no cycle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">In any graph, what does <span class=\"m\">(A^2)_{ii}</span> equal?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">0</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\deg(i)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the number of triangles at <span class=\"m\">i</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A walk of length 2 from <span class=\"m\">i</span> back to <span class=\"m\">i</span> goes along an edge and returns, one for each neighbour.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">A path visits 7 vertices. What is its length?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Length counts edges, and a path through 7 vertices uses 6 edges.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "trees": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-simplest-connected-graphs\" data-h=\"3\">The simplest connected graphs</h3>\n<p>Some graphs are connected but have no wasted edges at all. Take away any single edge and they fall apart. Add any single edge and a cycle appears. These graphs are called <strong>trees</strong>, and they are probably the most useful graphs in all of computer science. Your computer's folders form a tree. A family tree is (roughly) a tree. The way a sorting algorithm splits its work, the way a decision is made by a sequence of yes/no questions, the way a compiler represents <code>3 * (x + 2)</code>: trees, all of them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-wiring-up-some-villages\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine wiring up some villages</h3>\n<p>Imagine you must connect 6 villages with cables so that every village can reach every other one, and cable is expensive. You lay cables one at a time. If you ever lay a cable between two villages that can already reach each other, it is wasted, since it closes a loop and adds no new connectivity. A careful engineer never does that. When the network finally connects all 6 villages without a single loop, you will find you laid exactly 5 cables. Try it with any arrangement: it is always 5. That network is a tree, and \"always <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges\" is the central fact about trees.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Tree</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A tree is a connected graph with no cycles. A graph with no cycles (connected or not) is a <strong>forest</strong>; its components are trees.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Leaf</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A vertex of degree 1 in a tree.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-every-tree-has-leaves\" data-h=\"3\">Why every tree has leaves</h3>\n<p>Every tree with at least 2 vertices has at least 2 leaves. Here is a slick argument. Take a longest path in the tree, <span class=\"m\">v_0, v_1, \\dots, v_k</span>. Look at its end <span class=\"m\">v_0</span>. If <span class=\"m\">v_0</span> had a neighbour other than <span class=\"m\">v_1</span>, either that neighbour is already on the path (which would create a cycle) or it is not (which would make a longer path). Both are impossible, so <span class=\"m\">\\deg(v_0) = 1</span>. The same holds for <span class=\"m\">v_k</span>. So there are at least two leaves.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-a-tree-has-exactly-mathph11x-edges\" data-h=\"3\">Why a tree has exactly <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges</h3>\n<p>Leaves give an easy induction. A tree with 1 vertex has 0 edges. For a tree with <span class=\"m\">n \\ge 2</span> vertices, remove a leaf and its single edge. What remains is still connected (nothing passed <em>through</em> a leaf) and still has no cycles, so it is a tree on <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> vertices, which by induction has <span class=\"m\">n - 2</span> edges. Put the leaf back: <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges.</p>\n<p>Combined with the last lesson (connected needs at least <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges), this tells us trees are the <em>minimally connected</em> graphs. They are also <em>maximally acyclic</em>: adding any edge <span class=\"m\">uv</span> creates exactly one cycle, formed by the edge plus the unique path between <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"many-ways-to-say-tree\" data-h=\"3\">Many ways to say \"tree\"</h3>\n<p>For a graph <span class=\"m\">G</span> with <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices, the following are all equivalent. Any one of them can serve as the definition.\n1. <span class=\"m\">G</span> is connected and has no cycles.\n2. <span class=\"m\">G</span> is connected and has <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges.\n3. <span class=\"m\">G</span> has no cycles and has <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges.\n4. Between any two vertices there is exactly one path.\n5. <span class=\"m\">G</span> is connected, and removing any edge disconnects it (every edge is a bridge).\n6. <span class=\"m\">G</span> has no cycles, and adding any edge creates exactly one cycle.</p>\n<p>Statement 4 is the one people use most in practice. In a folder tree, there is exactly one way to get from the root folder to any file, which is why a file has a single, well-defined path like <code>/home/asha/notes.txt</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rooted-trees-and-their-vocabulary\" data-h=\"3\">Rooted trees and their vocabulary</h3>\n<p>Often we pick one vertex as the <strong>root</strong> and hang the tree from it. Then every other vertex has a <strong>parent</strong> (the next vertex on its path to the root) and possibly <strong>children</strong>. Vertices with no children are leaves. The <strong>depth</strong> of a vertex is its distance from the root, and the <strong>height</strong> of the tree is the largest depth. Vertices sharing a parent are siblings, and everything below a vertex forms its <strong>subtree</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>binary tree</strong> is a rooted tree where each vertex has at most 2 children, called left and right. A binary tree of height <span class=\"m\">h</span> has at most <span class=\"m\">1 + 2 + 4 + \\dots + 2^h = 2^{h+1} - 1</span> vertices, because the number of vertices can at most double at each level. Turning that around, a binary tree with <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices has height at least <span class=\"m\">\\log_2(n+1) - 1</span>, which is roughly <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 n</span>. This one inequality explains why balanced search trees, heaps and binary search all run in logarithmic time, and it is the key to the sorting lower bound in the algorithms course.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;tree&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;degree&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>A tree on 8 vertices: 7 edges, and the leaves are the vertices of degree 1.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"forests-and-counting-trees\" data-h=\"3\">Forests and counting trees</h3>\n<p>A forest with <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices and <span class=\"m\">c</span> components (each a tree) has exactly <span class=\"m\">n - c</span> edges, since each tree with <span class=\"m\">n_i</span> vertices has <span class=\"m\">n_i - 1</span> edges and the <span class=\"m\">n_i</span> add up to <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p>\n<p>How many different trees can you build on <span class=\"m\">n</span> labelled vertices? For <span class=\"m\">n = 3</span> there are 3 (choose which vertex is in the middle). For <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span> there are 16. The general answer is <strong>Cayley's formula</strong>: <span class=\"m\">n^{n-2}</span>. For <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">4^2 = 16</span>. For <span class=\"m\">n = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">5^3 = 125</span>. These are the possible spanning trees of the complete graph <span class=\"m\">K_n</span>, a topic we will return to.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>A graph with <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges is not automatically a tree: you also need connectivity or acyclicity (a triangle plus an isolated vertex has 3 edges on 4 vertices). Height is counted in edges, not levels: a single root has height 0. And \"binary tree\" in CS usually means at most two children, not exactly two; a tree where every internal vertex has exactly two children is called a <strong>full</strong> binary tree.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A tree is a connected acyclic graph. Trees with <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices have exactly <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges, at least two leaves (if <span class=\"m\">n \\ge 2</span>), and a unique path between any two vertices. They are minimally connected and maximally acyclic. Rooted trees add parent/child structure, binary trees of height <span class=\"m\">h</span> hold at most <span class=\"m\">2^{h+1} - 1</span> vertices, forests with <span class=\"m\">c</span> components have <span class=\"m\">n - c</span> edges, and there are <span class=\"m\">n^{n-2}</span> labelled trees on <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;tree&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;components&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Add one edge to the tree and watch a cycle appear and every bridge on it disappear.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <code>tree</code> loaded and \"Components, bridges, cut vertices\" selected, notice every edge is a red dashed bridge. That is property 5.\n2. Switch to \"Edit edges\" and add the edge 4–5. Exactly the edges on the cycle 4–1–0–2–5–4 stop being bridges.\n3. Remove the edge 0–2 instead. The tree splits into a forest of 2 components with <span class=\"m\">8 - 2 = 6</span> edges.\n4. Switch \"Ask\" to \"Degrees\" and count the leaves (degree 1). There are always at least 2.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> in a tree every edge is essential, and one extra edge creates exactly one cycle. Deleting an edge from a tree always produces exactly two components.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{tree on } n \\text{ vertices: } m = n - 1; \\qquad \\text{forest with } c \\text{ trees: } m = n - c</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{binary tree of height } h: \\; n \\le 2^{h+1} - 1 \\iff h \\ge \\log_2(n+1) - 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Cayley: } \\#\\{\\text{labelled trees on } n \\text{ vertices}\\} = n^{n-2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{leaves in a tree: } \\#\\text{leaves} = 2 + \\sum_{\\deg v \\ge 3} (\\deg v - 2)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Edges of a tree</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every tree with <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices has exactly <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Induction on <span class=\"m\">n</span>. For <span class=\"m\">n = 1</span> there are 0 edges. For <span class=\"m\">n \\ge 2</span>, a longest path ends at a leaf <span class=\"m\">\\ell</span> (as shown in the article). Deleting <span class=\"m\">\\ell</span> and its edge leaves a connected acyclic graph on <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> vertices, which by induction has <span class=\"m\">n - 2</span> edges. So the original has <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Unique paths</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A graph is a tree if and only if every pair of vertices is joined by exactly one path.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If two different <span class=\"m\">u</span>–<span class=\"m\">v</span> paths existed in a tree, follow them from <span class=\"m\">u</span> until they first split, and then until they first meet again; the two pieces between those points form a cycle, a contradiction. Conversely, if every pair has exactly one path the graph is connected, and a cycle would give two different paths between any two of its vertices, so there is no cycle.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A tree has 4 vertices of degree 3, 2 vertices of degree 2, and the rest are leaves. How many leaves?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Let <span class=\"m\">L</span> be the number of leaves. Then <span class=\"m\">n = 4 + 2 + L = 6 + L</span> and <span class=\"m\">m = n - 1 = 5 + L</span>.</li>\n<li>Handshake: <span class=\"m\">4(3) + 2(2) + L(1) = 2m = 10 + 2L</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">16 + L = 10 + 2L</span>, so <span class=\"m\">L = 6</span>.</li>\n<li>Check with the leaf formula: <span class=\"m\">2 + 4 \\times (3 - 2) = 6</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">What is the minimum height of a binary tree with 100 vertices?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Height <span class=\"m\">h</span> allows at most <span class=\"m\">2^{h+1} - 1</span> vertices.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">h = 5</span>: <span class=\"m\">2^6 - 1 = 63 &lt; 100</span>. Not enough.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">h = 6</span>: <span class=\"m\">2^7 - 1 = 127 \\ge 100</span>. Enough.</li>\n<li>So the minimum height is 6, and a balanced tree achieves it.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(a) A forest has 40 vertices and 33 edges. How many trees does it contain? (b) Prove that adding one edge to a tree creates exactly one cycle.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">m = n - c</span>, so <span class=\"m\">33 = 40 - c</span> and <span class=\"m\">c = 7</span>. (b) Add edge <span class=\"m\">uv</span>. Any cycle in the new graph must use <span class=\"m\">uv</span> (the tree itself has none), and removing <span class=\"m\">uv</span> from such a cycle leaves a <span class=\"m\">u</span>–<span class=\"m\">v</span> path in the tree. There is exactly one such path, so there is exactly one cycle: the unique path plus the new edge.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>File systems, the HTML DOM, syntax trees in compilers, binary search trees, heaps, tries and B-trees in databases are all rooted trees. The bound <span class=\"m\">h \\ge \\log_2(n+1) - 1</span> is why a balanced tree holding a billion keys needs only about 30 levels.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Decision trees, random forests and gradient-boosted trees (XGBoost, LightGBM) are literally rooted trees: each internal vertex asks a yes/no question about a feature and each leaf holds a prediction. Hierarchical clustering produces a tree of merges called a dendrogram.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A knockout tournament with 64 players is a binary tree: each match is an internal vertex and each player is a leaf. Since every match eliminates exactly one player, there are always <span class=\"m\">64 - 1 = 63</span> matches, the same \"<span class=\"m\">n - 1</span>\" count as the edges of a tree.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A tree has 30 vertices. How many edges does it have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"29\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Every tree has <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A connected graph has 10 vertices and 9 edges. Must it be a tree?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Connected with <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges is one of the equivalent definitions of a tree.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many labelled trees are there on 5 vertices?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"125\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Cayley's formula: <span class=\"m\">5^{5-2} = 125</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A forest has 20 vertices and 4 components. How many edges?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"16\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">m = n - c = 20 - 4 = 16</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the maximum number of vertices in a binary tree of height 3?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"15\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2^{3+1} - 1 = 15</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Removing one edge from a tree produces how many components?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">1</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">2</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it depends</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Every edge of a tree is a bridge, and removing a single bridge splits one component into exactly two.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "bfs-dfs": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-need-a-systematic-way-to-explore\" data-h=\"3\">Why we need a systematic way to explore</h3>\n<p>Suppose you are dropped into a maze, or a computer is handed a social network with a billion users, and the question is \"what can I reach from here?\". Wandering randomly might work eventually, but it can miss things and it can loop forever. We need a method that visits every reachable vertex exactly once and never gets lost. There are two classic methods, and nearly every graph algorithm you will ever meet is built on top of one of them: <strong>breadth-first search</strong> (BFS) and <strong>depth-first search</strong> (DFS).</p>\n<p>Both methods share one idea: keep a record of which vertices you have already <strong>discovered</strong>, and never process a vertex twice. They differ only in the <em>order</em> in which they process the vertices they have discovered but not yet explored, which we call the <strong>frontier</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-ripples-on-a-pond\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine ripples on a pond</h3>\n<p>Drop a stone in a pond and ripples spread out in rings. First the water right next to the stone moves, then the ring just outside that, then the next ring. BFS explores a graph the same way. It starts at a source vertex <span class=\"m\">s</span>, then visits all neighbours of <span class=\"m\">s</span> (distance 1), then all their undiscovered neighbours (distance 2), and so on, layer by layer.</p>\n<p>To get this ring-by-ring order, BFS keeps the frontier in a <strong>queue</strong>: first in, first out, like a line at a ticket counter. The algorithm is short:\n1. Mark <span class=\"m\">s</span> discovered, set <span class=\"m\">d(s) = 0</span>, and put <span class=\"m\">s</span> in the queue.\n2. While the queue is not empty, remove the vertex <span class=\"m\">u</span> at the front.\n3. For each neighbour <span class=\"m\">v</span> of <span class=\"m\">u</span> that is not yet discovered, mark it, set <span class=\"m\">d(v) = d(u) + 1</span>, record <span class=\"m\">u</span> as its <strong>parent</strong>, and add <span class=\"m\">v</span> to the back of the queue.</p>\n<p>Let us run it on a small graph. Vertices 0 to 4, edges <span class=\"m\">01, 02, 13, 23, 34</span>. Start at 0. Queue: [0]. Remove 0, discover 1 and 2 at distance 1. Queue: [1, 2]. Remove 1, discover 3 at distance 2 (2 is already discovered). Queue: [2, 3]. Remove 2: its neighbours 0 and 3 are both discovered. Queue: [3]. Remove 3, discover 4 at distance 3. Remove 4. Done. The order of visits was 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, and the distances 0, 1, 1, 2, 3 are exactly the shortest-path distances.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-bfs-finds-shortest-paths\" data-h=\"3\">Why BFS finds shortest paths</h3>\n<p>That last claim is the most important property of BFS: in an unweighted graph, the value <span class=\"m\">d(v)</span> it computes is the true distance from <span class=\"m\">s</span>. The reason is the queue. Vertices enter the queue in order of non-decreasing <span class=\"m\">d</span> value: first all of layer 0, then all of layer 1, then layer 2. A vertex at true distance <span class=\"m\">k</span> has a neighbour at distance <span class=\"m\">k - 1</span>, which gets processed before any vertex of layer <span class=\"m\">k</span> or beyond, and that neighbour discovers it with <span class=\"m\">d = k</span>. It cannot be discovered earlier with a smaller value, because nothing at distance less than <span class=\"m\">k - 1</span> is adjacent to it. Following the parent pointers backwards from <span class=\"m\">v</span> traces an actual shortest path. The parent edges together form the <strong>BFS tree</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-exploring-a-cave\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine exploring a cave</h3>\n<p>Now imagine exploring a cave with a ball of string. You follow one tunnel as deep as it goes. When you hit a dead end, you walk back along your string to the most recent junction with an unexplored tunnel, and dive down that one. This is <strong>depth-first search</strong>. DFS keeps the frontier on a <strong>stack</strong> (last in, first out), or more naturally uses recursion, where the computer's call stack plays the role of the string:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Mark <span class=\"m\">u</span> as discovered.</li>\n<li>For each neighbour <span class=\"m\">v</span> of <span class=\"m\">u</span>: if <span class=\"m\">v</span> is not discovered, record <span class=\"m\">u</span> as its parent and run DFS from <span class=\"m\">v</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>On the same graph from 0 (neighbours in increasing order): visit 0, go to 1, from 1 go to 3, from 3 go to 2 (2 is undiscovered), 2's neighbours are all discovered so back up to 3, go to 4, back up all the way. Visit order: 0, 1, 3, 2, 4. Notice DFS reached 2 through a long route; DFS does <strong>not</strong> find shortest paths.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-dfs-is-good-for\" data-h=\"3\">What DFS is good for</h3>\n<p>DFS is good at questions about structure. In an undirected graph, if DFS ever looks along an edge to an already-discovered vertex that is not its parent, it has found a <strong>cycle</strong>. Running DFS (or BFS) from each undiscovered vertex in turn labels the <strong>connected components</strong>. On directed graphs, the order in which DFS <em>finishes</em> vertices gives a topological sort, and DFS is the engine of Tarjan's algorithms for bridges, cut vertices and strongly connected components.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"graph\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;tree&quot;, &quot;alg&quot;: &quot;dfs&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>DFS on a tree: it dives down the leftmost branch first, then backs up.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"how-fast-are-they\" data-h=\"3\">How fast are they?</h3>\n<p>Each vertex is added to the queue or stack at most once, because we mark it when it is discovered. When a vertex is processed we scan its adjacency list once. The total length of all adjacency lists is <span class=\"m\">2m</span> (the handshake lemma again). So both BFS and DFS do <span class=\"m\">O(n + m)</span> work with adjacency lists. That is <strong>linear time</strong>: proportional to the size of the input, which is as fast as you can hope for, since you must at least read the graph. With an adjacency matrix, scanning a vertex's neighbours costs <span class=\"m\">n</span>, so the total becomes <span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The classic BFS bug is marking a vertex as visited when it is <em>removed</em> from the queue rather than when it is <em>added</em>. Then the same vertex can be added many times, wasting time and sometimes breaking the distance labels. Another mistake is using BFS distances on a weighted graph: BFS counts edges, not total weight, so it is wrong as soon as edges have different lengths (that is Dijkstra's job, next lesson). Finally, a single BFS or DFS only reaches the source's component; to cover a disconnected graph, restart from every undiscovered vertex.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>BFS explores in layers using a queue and computes shortest-path distances in unweighted graphs, with parent pointers forming a shortest-path tree. DFS dives deep using a stack or recursion and reveals structure: cycles, components, and orderings. Both run in <span class=\"m\">O(n + m)</span> time with adjacency lists because each vertex is handled once and each edge is looked at twice.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"graph\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;sample&quot;, &quot;alg&quot;: &quot;bfs&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Press Play for BFS, then switch to DFS and compare the visit orders.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run BFS from vertex 0. The pink numbers above vertices are BFS distances: vertices 1 and 2 get 1, vertices 3 and 5 get 2, vertices 4 and 6 get 3.\n2. Read the queue in the readout at each step. New vertices always join at the back.\n3. Switch to DFS and play again. The visit order changes, and DFS goes deep along one branch before returning.\n4. Click a different vertex to make it the source and rerun both searches.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> BFS finishes everything at distance 1 before touching anything at distance 2, which is exactly the \"ripples\" picture and the reason BFS distances are correct. DFS's order depends on which neighbour it tries first, and it can reach a vertex by a long route.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{BFS: } d(v) = d(\\text{parent}(v)) + 1, \\quad d(s) = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{time with adjacency lists: } O(n) + O\\Big(\\sum_v \\deg v\\Big) = O(n + 2m) = O(n + m)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{time with adjacency matrix: } O(n^2)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">BFS computes distances</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In an unweighted graph, BFS from <span class=\"m\">s</span> sets <span class=\"m\">d(v) = \\operatorname{dist}(s, v)</span> for every vertex <span class=\"m\">v</span> reachable from <span class=\"m\">s</span>, and the parent pointers give a shortest path from <span class=\"m\">s</span> to each <span class=\"m\">v</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>First, <span class=\"m\">d(v) \\ge \\operatorname{dist}(s,v)</span> always, since <span class=\"m\">d(v)</span> is the length of a real path (the chain of parents). Next, BFS removes vertices from the queue in non-decreasing order of <span class=\"m\">d</span>. Suppose some <span class=\"m\">v</span> has <span class=\"m\">d(v) &gt; \\operatorname{dist}(s,v) = k</span>, and take such a <span class=\"m\">v</span> with smallest <span class=\"m\">k</span>. Let <span class=\"m\">u</span> be the previous vertex on a shortest path, so <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{dist}(s,u) = k-1</span> and <span class=\"m\">d(u) = k-1</span> by minimality. When <span class=\"m\">u</span> was removed from the queue, <span class=\"m\">v</span> was either undiscovered (then it gets <span class=\"m\">d(v) = k</span>) or already discovered by a vertex removed no later than <span class=\"m\">u</span>, with <span class=\"m\">d</span> value at most <span class=\"m\">k - 1</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">d(v) \\le k</span>. Either way <span class=\"m\">d(v) \\le k</span>, a contradiction.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Run BFS from A on edges <span class=\"m\">AB, AC, BD, CD, CE, DF, EF</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Queue [A], <span class=\"m\">d(A) = 0</span>. Remove A: discover B, C with <span class=\"m\">d = 1</span>. Queue [B, C].</li>\n<li>Remove B: discover D with <span class=\"m\">d = 2</span>. Queue [C, D].</li>\n<li>Remove C: D is already discovered; discover E with <span class=\"m\">d = 2</span>. Queue [D, E].</li>\n<li>Remove D: discover F with <span class=\"m\">d = 3</span>. Queue [E, F]. Remove E (F already found), remove F.</li>\n<li>Distances: A 0, B 1, C 1, D 2, E 2, F 3. Shortest path to F via parents: F ← D ← B ← A.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Use DFS to count the components of a graph with vertices 1–7 and edges <span class=\"m\">12, 23, 45, 67</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Start DFS at 1: reach 2, then 3. Component 1 = {1, 2, 3}.</li>\n<li>Next undiscovered vertex is 4: reach 5. Component 2 = {4, 5}.</li>\n<li>Next undiscovered is 6: reach 7. Component 3 = {6, 7}.</li>\n<li>Answer: 3 components, found in <span class=\"m\">O(n + m)</span> total time.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A graph has 1000 vertices and 4000 edges, stored as adjacency lists. (a) Roughly how many adjacency-list entries does BFS scan in total? (b) If the graph were stored as an adjacency matrix, roughly how many entries would BFS scan? (c) Which search would you use to find the fewest flights between two cities, and why?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Each edge appears twice, so <span class=\"m\">2m = 8000</span> entries, plus <span class=\"m\">n = 1000</span> vertex operations, about 9000 steps. (b) Each of the 1000 processed vertices scans a full row of 1000 entries: <span class=\"m\">10^6</span> entries. (c) BFS, because \"fewest flights\" is an unweighted shortest path (count of edges), which BFS computes exactly; DFS may find a much longer route.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Web crawlers explore the web graph breadth-first from seed pages. Garbage collectors use DFS or BFS from the program's roots to find live objects. Puzzle solvers (Rubik's cube, sliding puzzles) use BFS to find solutions with the fewest moves, and maze generators use randomised DFS.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>GraphSAGE and other GNNs sample a node's neighbourhood with a BFS-like expansion (1-hop, then 2-hop). Node2vec builds node embeddings from random walks that are deliberately biased to behave either more like BFS (exploring locally) or more like DFS (exploring far away).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>\"How many degrees of separation are there between you and a famous actor?\" is a BFS question. The \"Six Degrees of Kevin Bacon\" game runs BFS on the graph of actors joined by shared films.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which data structure does BFS use for its frontier?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a queue</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a stack</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a priority queue</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>First in, first out gives the layer-by-layer order.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">On an unweighted graph, which search guarantees shortest paths from the source?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">BFS</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">DFS</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">both</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>BFS processes vertices in order of distance. DFS may reach a vertex by a long route.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">With adjacency lists, BFS on a graph with 50 vertices and 200 edges does work proportional to about:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">50^2</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">50 + 200</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">50 \\times 200</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>BFS is <span class=\"m\">O(n + m)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">In undirected DFS, you look along an edge and find an already discovered vertex that is not your parent. What have you found?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a bridge</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a cycle</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a leaf</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The tree path between the two vertices plus this edge forms a cycle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">BFS from <span class=\"m\">s</span> reaches <span class=\"m\">v</span> with <span class=\"m\">d(v) = 4</span>. How many edges are on a shortest <span class=\"m\">s</span>–<span class=\"m\">v</span> path?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>BFS distances are exact shortest-path lengths in unweighted graphs.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "shortest-paths": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"when-edges-have-lengths\" data-h=\"3\">When edges have lengths</h3>\n<p>BFS counts edges. But real journeys have lengths: a 2 km road and a 40 km road are not the same. Flights have prices. Network links have delays. Once edges carry <strong>weights</strong>, the shortest path is the one whose <em>total weight</em> is smallest, and that may use more edges. A route with three 5-minute hops (15 minutes) beats a single 40-minute hop. We need a new algorithm.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Weighted distance</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In a graph with edge weights <span class=\"m\">w(u,v)</span>, the length of a path is the sum of its edge weights, and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{dist}(s, v)</span> is the minimum length over all <span class=\"m\">s</span>–<span class=\"m\">v</span> paths.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"imagine-water-flowing-through-pipes\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine water flowing through pipes</h3>\n<p>Imagine pouring water into the source vertex of a network of pipes, where each pipe takes <span class=\"m\">w</span> seconds to traverse. Water spreads outward, and the moment water first reaches a vertex is exactly its shortest distance from the source. Water reaches the closest vertex first, then the next closest, and so on. Dijkstra's algorithm simulates this: it settles vertices in order of their distance from the source.</p>\n<h3 id=\"relaxation-the-basic-move\" data-h=\"3\">Relaxation: the basic move</h3>\n<p>Every shortest-path algorithm keeps a tentative distance <span class=\"m\">d(v)</span> for each vertex, starting at <span class=\"m\">d(s) = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">d(v) = \\infty</span> otherwise. The basic operation is <strong>relaxing</strong> an edge <span class=\"m\">(u, v)</span>: if going through <span class=\"m\">u</span> gives a shorter route, update.\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{if } d(u) + w(u,v) &lt; d(v): \\quad d(v) \\leftarrow d(u) + w(u,v), \\;\\; \\text{parent}(v) \\leftarrow u</span>\nRelaxation never makes <span class=\"m\">d(v)</span> wrong in the dangerous direction: <span class=\"m\">d(v)</span> is always the length of some real path, so it is always at least the true distance. The algorithms differ in which edges they relax and in what order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"dijkstra-s-algorithm\" data-h=\"3\">Dijkstra's algorithm</h3>\n<p>Dijkstra's algorithm works when all weights are non-negative.\n1. Set <span class=\"m\">d(s) = 0</span> and all other <span class=\"m\">d(v) = \\infty</span>. Every vertex is unfinished.\n2. Pick the unfinished vertex <span class=\"m\">u</span> with the smallest <span class=\"m\">d(u)</span> and mark it <strong>finished</strong>.\n3. Relax every edge leaving <span class=\"m\">u</span>.\n4. Repeat until every reachable vertex is finished.</p>\n<p>A small example: vertices <span class=\"m\">s, a, b, t</span> with edges <span class=\"m\">sa = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">sb = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">ba = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">at = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">bt = 5</span>. Start: <span class=\"m\">d = (s{:}0, a{:}\\infty, b{:}\\infty, t{:}\\infty)</span>. Finish <span class=\"m\">s</span>, relax: <span class=\"m\">a = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 1</span>. Smallest unfinished is <span class=\"m\">b</span> (1). Finish <span class=\"m\">b</span>, relax: <span class=\"m\">a = \\min(4, 1 + 2) = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">t = 1 + 5 = 6</span>. Finish <span class=\"m\">a</span> (3), relax: <span class=\"m\">t = \\min(6, 3 + 1) = 4</span>. Finish <span class=\"m\">t</span> (4). The shortest path to <span class=\"m\">t</span> is <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to a \\to t</span> with length 4, using three edges even though <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to t</span> uses only two.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-the-greedy-choice-is-safe\" data-h=\"3\">Why the greedy choice is safe</h3>\n<p>Why can we declare the smallest tentative vertex finished forever? Suppose <span class=\"m\">u</span> has the smallest <span class=\"m\">d(u)</span> among unfinished vertices. Any other route to <span class=\"m\">u</span> must leave the finished region at some unfinished vertex <span class=\"m\">x</span>, and <span class=\"m\">d(x) \\ge d(u)</span> already. Continuing from <span class=\"m\">x</span> to <span class=\"m\">u</span> only adds non-negative weights, so that route is at least <span class=\"m\">d(u)</span>. Nothing can beat <span class=\"m\">d(u)</span>. This argument is exactly where <strong>non-negative weights</strong> are needed: a negative edge later on could make a detour cheaper, and Dijkstra would have already finalised the wrong answer.</p>\n<h3 id=\"speed\" data-h=\"3\">Speed</h3>\n<p>With a <strong>binary heap</strong> (priority queue) holding the tentative distances, each \"pick the smallest\" costs <span class=\"m\">O(\\log n)</span> and each relaxation that lowers a distance costs <span class=\"m\">O(\\log n)</span>. There are <span class=\"m\">n</span> picks and at most <span class=\"m\">m</span> successful relaxations, so Dijkstra runs in <span class=\"m\">O((n + m)\\log n)</span>. With a plain array, picking the minimum costs <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span> each time, giving <span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span>, which is actually better for very dense graphs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"negative-weights-bellman-ford\" data-h=\"3\">Negative weights: Bellman–Ford</h3>\n<p>Negative weights do occur: in currency exchange, a \"cost\" can be a gain. <strong>Bellman–Ford</strong> handles them by brute patience: relax <em>every</em> edge, and repeat that <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> times. Why <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span>? A shortest path (with no cycles) has at most <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges, and after round <span class=\"m\">k</span> every vertex whose shortest path uses at most <span class=\"m\">k</span> edges has the correct distance. Total time <span class=\"m\">O(nm)</span>.</p>\n<p>If a cycle has negative total weight, you could loop around it forever and make the \"shortest\" path as small as you like, so shortest distances are undefined. Bellman–Ford detects this: if a <span class=\"m\">n</span>-th round still improves some distance, a negative cycle exists.</p>\n<h3 id=\"all-pairs-floyd-warshall\" data-h=\"3\">All pairs: Floyd–Warshall</h3>\n<p>To get distances between <em>every</em> pair, the <strong>Floyd–Warshall</strong> algorithm uses dynamic programming. Number the vertices <span class=\"m\">1</span> to <span class=\"m\">n</span> and let <span class=\"m\">d_k(i,j)</span> be the shortest <span class=\"m\">i</span>–<span class=\"m\">j</span> distance using only vertices <span class=\"m\">1..k</span> as intermediate stops. Then\n<span class=\"mb\">d_k(i,j) = \\min\\big(d_{k-1}(i,j),\\; d_{k-1}(i,k) + d_{k-1}(k,j)\\big),</span>\nbecause the best path either avoids <span class=\"m\">k</span> or goes through it once. Three nested loops give <span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span>.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"graph\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;sample&quot;, &quot;alg&quot;: &quot;dijkstra&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Dijkstra from vertex 0. Pink labels are the current tentative distances.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"reading-the-answer-the-shortest-path-tree\" data-h=\"3\">Reading the answer: the shortest-path tree</h3>\n<p>Dijkstra gives you more than numbers. Every time a relaxation succeeds it records a parent, and when the algorithm ends each vertex's parent is the previous stop on one of its shortest routes. To recover the route to <span class=\"m\">t</span>, start at <span class=\"m\">t</span> and follow parents back to <span class=\"m\">s</span>, then reverse the list. In the small example above, <span class=\"m\">t</span>'s parent is <span class=\"m\">a</span>, <span class=\"m\">a</span>'s parent is <span class=\"m\">b</span> and <span class=\"m\">b</span>'s parent is <span class=\"m\">s</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to a \\to t</span>. The parent edges of all vertices together form a tree rooted at <span class=\"m\">s</span> called the <strong>shortest-path tree</strong>. It has a useful property: any piece of a shortest path is itself a shortest path. If the best route from <span class=\"m\">s</span> to <span class=\"m\">t</span> passes through <span class=\"m\">a</span>, then its first part must be a best route from <span class=\"m\">s</span> to <span class=\"m\">a</span>, otherwise you could swap in a better first part and beat the best route. This \"optimal substructure\" is the reason all these algorithms can build long answers out of short ones, and it is the same idea that powers dynamic programming in the algorithms course.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Do not use Dijkstra with negative edges; it can return wrong answers without any warning. Do not confuse the shortest path with the path with fewest edges; that is BFS's question. And remember that a shortest-path tree is not a minimum spanning tree: the first minimises distances from one source, the second minimises total edge weight (next lesson).</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>With weights, distance is minimum total weight. All algorithms work by relaxation. Dijkstra finalises vertices in order of distance and is correct for non-negative weights, running in <span class=\"m\">O((n+m)\\log n)</span> with a heap. Bellman–Ford handles negative weights in <span class=\"m\">O(nm)</span> and detects negative cycles. Floyd–Warshall computes all pairs in <span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"graph\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;sample&quot;, &quot;alg&quot;: &quot;dijkstra&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Step through Dijkstra and watch each vertex get finalised.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Press Step repeatedly from source 0. The readout shows which vertex is finalised and the current distances.\n2. Check vertex 1: it first gets distance 4 (direct edge) but improves to 3 via vertex 2 (<span class=\"m\">2 + 1</span>).\n3. Note the final distances: 0, 3, 2, 8, 10, 12, 13 for vertices 0 to 6.\n4. Click vertex 6 to make it the source and run again.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> vertices are finalised in increasing order of distance (0, 2, 1, 3, 4, 5, 6 from source 0), just like water reaching the nearest points first. A tentative distance can drop several times, but once a vertex is finalised it never changes.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{relax}(u,v): \\; d(v) \\leftarrow \\min\\big(d(v),\\; d(u) + w(u,v)\\big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{dist}(s,v) = \\min_{u:\\,(u,v)\\in E}\\big(\\operatorname{dist}(s,u) + w(u,v)\\big) \\quad \\text{(Bellman equation)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Dijkstra (heap): } O((n+m)\\log n); \\quad \\text{Bellman–Ford: } O(nm); \\quad \\text{Floyd–Warshall: } O(n^3)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">d_k(i,j) = \\min\\big(d_{k-1}(i,j),\\, d_{k-1}(i,k) + d_{k-1}(k,j)\\big)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Correctness of Dijkstra</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If all edge weights are non-negative, then when Dijkstra's algorithm finalises a vertex <span class=\"m\">u</span>, <span class=\"m\">d(u) = \\operatorname{dist}(s,u)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Suppose not, and let <span class=\"m\">u</span> be the first vertex finalised with <span class=\"m\">d(u) &gt; \\operatorname{dist}(s,u)</span>. Take a true shortest path <span class=\"m\">P</span> from <span class=\"m\">s</span> to <span class=\"m\">u</span> and let <span class=\"m\">y</span> be the first unfinished vertex on it, with predecessor <span class=\"m\">x</span> (finished, so <span class=\"m\">d(x)</span> is correct). When <span class=\"m\">x</span> was finished, edge <span class=\"m\">xy</span> was relaxed, so <span class=\"m\">d(y) \\le \\operatorname{dist}(s,x) + w(x,y) = \\operatorname{dist}(s,y) \\le \\operatorname{dist}(s,u)</span>, using non-negative weights for the last step. Hence <span class=\"m\">d(y) \\le \\operatorname{dist}(s,u) &lt; d(u)</span>, so Dijkstra would have picked <span class=\"m\">y</span> before <span class=\"m\">u</span>. Contradiction.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Dijkstra from A: <span class=\"m\">AB = 7</span>, <span class=\"m\">AC = 9</span>, <span class=\"m\">AF = 14</span>, <span class=\"m\">BC = 10</span>, <span class=\"m\">BD = 15</span>, <span class=\"m\">CD = 11</span>, <span class=\"m\">CF = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">DE = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">EF = 9</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Finish A (0). Relax: B 7, C 9, F 14.</li>\n<li>Finish B (7). Relax: C <span class=\"m\">\\min(9, 17) = 9</span>, D 22.</li>\n<li>Finish C (9). Relax: D <span class=\"m\">\\min(22, 20) = 20</span>, F <span class=\"m\">\\min(14, 11) = 11</span>.</li>\n<li>Finish F (11). Relax: E <span class=\"m\">11 + 9 = 20</span>.</li>\n<li>Finish D (20) and E (20; <span class=\"m\">20 + 6 = 26</span> does not improve). Final: A 0, B 7, C 9, D 20, E 20, F 11.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Why Dijkstra fails with a negative edge: <span class=\"m\">s \\to a = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">s \\to b = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">b \\to a = -2</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Finish <span class=\"m\">s</span>. Tentative: <span class=\"m\">a = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 3</span>.</li>\n<li>Finish <span class=\"m\">a</span> with <span class=\"m\">d(a) = 2</span> (smallest).</li>\n<li>Finish <span class=\"m\">b</span> (3) and relax <span class=\"m\">b \\to a</span>: <span class=\"m\">3 - 2 = 1 &lt; 2</span>. But <span class=\"m\">a</span> is already finalised.</li>\n<li>The true distance to <span class=\"m\">a</span> is 1, so Dijkstra's answer 2 is wrong. Bellman–Ford would get 1.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A directed graph has edges <span class=\"m\">s\\to a</span> (5), <span class=\"m\">s \\to b</span> (2), <span class=\"m\">b \\to a</span> (1), <span class=\"m\">a \\to t</span> (3), <span class=\"m\">b \\to t</span> (7). (a) Run Dijkstra from <span class=\"m\">s</span> and give <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{dist}(s,t)</span> and the path. (b) How many rounds does Bellman–Ford need in the worst case here?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Finish <span class=\"m\">s</span>: <span class=\"m\">a = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 2</span>. Finish <span class=\"m\">b</span> (2): <span class=\"m\">a = \\min(5, 3) = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">t = 9</span>. Finish <span class=\"m\">a</span> (3): <span class=\"m\">t = \\min(9, 6) = 6</span>. Finish <span class=\"m\">t</span> (6). Path <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to a \\to t</span>, length 6. (b) <span class=\"m\">n - 1 = 3</span> rounds (4 vertices).</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>GPS navigation, network routing protocols (OSPF uses Dijkstra; RIP uses Bellman–Ford style updates) and game AI pathfinding (A*, which is Dijkstra plus a distance-to-goal estimate) are shortest-path algorithms at their core.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The Bellman equation <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{dist}(v) = \\min_u (\\operatorname{dist}(u) + w(u,v))</span> is the same equation that drives value iteration in reinforcement learning, where an agent learns the cheapest (or most rewarding) way to reach goals. Isomap and other manifold-learning methods compute shortest-path distances on a nearest-neighbour graph to estimate distances along curved data.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Currency traders look for negative cycles: if converting rupees to dollars to euros and back to rupees gives more than you started with, then with weights <span class=\"m\">-\\log(\\text{rate})</span> that loop has negative total weight. Bellman–Ford's negative-cycle check is an arbitrage detector.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Dijkstra's algorithm requires which condition?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the graph is a tree</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">all edge weights are non-negative</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the graph is unweighted</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The greedy finalisation argument breaks when a later negative edge can shorten a path.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Tentative distances are <span class=\"m\">d(u) = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">d(v) = 9</span>, and <span class=\"m\">w(u,v) = 3</span>. After relaxing <span class=\"m\">(u,v)</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">d(v)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\min(9, 4 + 3) = 7</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many rounds of relaxing every edge does Bellman–Ford need on a graph with 10 vertices?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"9\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> rounds suffice because a shortest simple path has at most <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">What does it mean if Bellman–Ford still improves a distance in round <span class=\"m\">n</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">there is a negative cycle reachable from the source</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the graph is disconnected</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Dijkstra would also fail to terminate</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Without a negative cycle, all distances are final after <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> rounds.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the running time of Floyd–Warshall on <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">O(n \\log n)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Three nested loops over <span class=\"m\">k</span>, <span class=\"m\">i</span>, <span class=\"m\">j</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "spanning-trees": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"connecting-towns-as-cheaply-as-possible\" data-h=\"3\">Connecting towns as cheaply as possible</h3>\n<p>A government wants to connect seven villages with fibre-optic cable. Laying cable between any two villages has a known cost, and every village must be able to reach every other one, directly or through other villages. Which cables should be laid to make the total cost as small as possible? You never want a cycle of cables, because one cable of the cycle could be removed and everything would still be connected, saving money. So the cheapest network is a <strong>tree</strong> that touches every village. This is the <strong>minimum spanning tree</strong> problem, one of the oldest problems in algorithms (it was first solved in 1926 for an electricity network in Moravia).</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Spanning tree</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A spanning tree of a connected graph <span class=\"m\">G</span> is a subgraph that includes every vertex of <span class=\"m\">G</span> and is a tree. It has exactly <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Minimum spanning tree (MST)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In a connected weighted graph, a minimum spanning tree is a spanning tree whose total edge weight is as small as possible.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"spanning-trees-exist-and-there-are-many-of-them\" data-h=\"3\">Spanning trees exist and there are many of them</h3>\n<p>Every connected graph has a spanning tree. One way to see it: while the graph has a cycle, delete any edge of that cycle. Deleting a cycle edge never disconnects the graph, because the rest of the cycle still joins its two ends. When no cycles remain, what is left is connected and acyclic, which is a tree. Another way: the BFS tree or DFS tree from the last lessons is a spanning tree.</p>\n<p>A graph usually has a huge number of spanning trees. A 4-cycle has 4 (remove any one of its 4 edges). The complete graph <span class=\"m\">K_n</span> has <span class=\"m\">n^{n-2}</span> by Cayley's formula, so <span class=\"m\">K_5</span> already has 125. Checking them all is hopeless for big graphs, so we need a cleverer idea.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-cut-property\" data-h=\"3\">The cut property</h3>\n<p>A <strong>cut</strong> splits the vertices into two non-empty groups <span class=\"m\">S</span> and <span class=\"m\">V \\setminus S</span>. An edge <strong>crosses</strong> the cut if it has one end in each group. Here is the key fact, called the <strong>cut property</strong>: for any cut, a lightest edge crossing it belongs to some minimum spanning tree (and if that lightest edge is unique, it belongs to every MST).</p>\n<p>Why? Take any MST <span class=\"m\">T</span>. If it already contains the lightest crossing edge <span class=\"m\">e</span>, we are done. Otherwise, add <span class=\"m\">e</span> to <span class=\"m\">T</span>. This creates exactly one cycle. That cycle goes from one side of the cut to the other along <span class=\"m\">e</span>, so it must come back across the cut by some other edge <span class=\"m\">f</span> of <span class=\"m\">T</span>. Now remove <span class=\"m\">f</span>. We get a spanning tree again, and its weight is <span class=\"m\">w(T) + w(e) - w(f) \\le w(T)</span> because <span class=\"m\">e</span> was the lightest crossing edge. So this new tree is also minimum, and it contains <span class=\"m\">e</span>. This \"exchange argument\" is the template for many greedy proofs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"kruskal-s-algorithm\" data-h=\"3\">Kruskal's algorithm</h3>\n<p>Kruskal's idea is the most natural greedy strategy: sort all edges from lightest to heaviest, then go through them in order, adding an edge whenever it does not create a cycle with the edges already chosen. Stop when you have <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges.</p>\n<p>Small example: vertices A, B, C, D with weights <span class=\"m\">AB = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">BC = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">AC = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">CD = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">BD = 5</span>. Take <span class=\"m\">AB</span> (1). Take <span class=\"m\">BC</span> (2). <span class=\"m\">AC</span> (3) would close the cycle A–B–C, so skip it. Take <span class=\"m\">CD</span> (4). We now have 3 edges for 4 vertices, so stop. MST weight <span class=\"m\">1 + 2 + 4 = 7</span>.</p>\n<p>Each edge Kruskal accepts is the lightest edge crossing the cut between \"the component containing one of its ends\" and everything else, so the cut property says it is safe. To test \"does this edge make a cycle?\" quickly, Kruskal uses a <strong>union–find</strong> structure that tracks which component each vertex is in; the total time is <span class=\"m\">O(m \\log m)</span>, dominated by sorting.</p>\n<h3 id=\"prim-s-algorithm\" data-h=\"3\">Prim's algorithm</h3>\n<p>Prim's algorithm grows a single tree from a starting vertex. At each step it looks at all edges leaving the current tree and adds the lightest one, together with its new vertex. On the same example starting from A: edges leaving {A} are <span class=\"m\">AB = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">AC = 3</span>; take <span class=\"m\">AB</span>. Leaving {A, B}: <span class=\"m\">BC = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">AC = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">BD = 5</span>; take <span class=\"m\">BC</span>. Leaving {A, B, C}: <span class=\"m\">CD = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">BD = 5</span>; take <span class=\"m\">CD</span>. Same tree, weight 7. Each step is the cut property applied to the cut \"tree versus rest\". With a heap Prim runs in <span class=\"m\">O(m \\log n)</span>, very much like Dijkstra, except that a vertex's key is the weight of its cheapest connecting edge rather than its distance from the start.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-cycle-property\" data-h=\"3\">The cycle property</h3>\n<p>There is a mirror-image fact. The <strong>cycle property</strong>: if an edge is the strictly heaviest edge on some cycle, it is in no MST. If it were in an MST, removing it would split the tree into two parts, and the cycle would contain another, lighter edge joining those parts; swapping it in would give a lighter spanning tree. This is why Kruskal can safely skip edges that close a cycle: at that moment they are the heaviest edge of that cycle.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>An MST is not a shortest-path tree. The MST minimises the <em>total</em> weight of the network, but the route between two particular vertices inside the MST may be much longer than their true shortest path. Another mistake is adding a cheap edge that closes a cycle in Kruskal; always check that the two ends are in different components. And MSTs are only for <strong>undirected</strong> graphs; the directed version (arborescences) needs a different algorithm.</p>\n<p>If all edge weights are distinct the MST is unique. With ties there can be several MSTs, but they all have the same total weight.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A spanning tree connects all <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices with <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges and no cycles. An MST does this with least total weight. The cut property (the lightest edge across any cut is safe) justifies both Kruskal's algorithm (global lightest safe edge, using union–find) and Prim's algorithm (grow one tree by its lightest outgoing edge). Both run in <span class=\"m\">O(m \\log n)</span> time.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"graph\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;sample&quot;, &quot;alg&quot;: &quot;kruskal&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Kruskal on the sample graph after all steps: highlighted edges form the MST.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"graph\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;sample&quot;, &quot;alg&quot;: &quot;kruskal&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Step through Kruskal, then switch to Prim and compare.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run Kruskal step by step. The edges are tried in order of weight: 1–2 (1), 5–6 (1), 0–2 (2), 3–4 (2), 4–5 (2), then 4–6 (3) is rejected because 4, 5, 6 are already connected.\n2. Keep stepping: 0–1 (4) is rejected, and 1–3 (5) joins the two halves.\n3. Check the final total: <span class=\"m\">1 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 5 = 13</span>, with 6 edges for 7 vertices.\n4. Switch to Prim and run it. The order of additions differs but the total is again 13.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> every rejected edge would close a cycle, and is the heaviest edge on that cycle (cycle property). Every accepted edge is the lightest one crossing some cut (cut property). Two very different greedy orders end at the same weight.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">|E(T)| = n - 1 \\text{ for any spanning tree } T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">w(T) = \\sum_{e \\in T} w(e)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{exchange: } w(T + e - f) = w(T) + w(e) - w(f)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Kruskal: } O(m \\log m), \\qquad \\text{Prim (heap): } O(m \\log n)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cut property</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">S</span> be a non-empty proper subset of vertices of a connected weighted graph, and let <span class=\"m\">e</span> be an edge of minimum weight among the edges with one end in <span class=\"m\">S</span> and one end outside <span class=\"m\">S</span>. Then some minimum spanning tree contains <span class=\"m\">e</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">T</span> be an MST. If <span class=\"m\">e \\in T</span>, done. Otherwise <span class=\"m\">T + e</span> contains a unique cycle <span class=\"m\">C</span>, which uses <span class=\"m\">e</span> to cross from <span class=\"m\">S</span> to <span class=\"m\">V \\setminus S</span>, so <span class=\"m\">C</span> must contain another edge <span class=\"m\">f</span> of <span class=\"m\">T</span> crossing the cut. <span class=\"m\">T' = T + e - f</span> is connected (the cycle still links <span class=\"m\">f</span>'s ends) and has <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges, so it is a spanning tree, with <span class=\"m\">w(T') = w(T) + w(e) - w(f) \\le w(T)</span> since <span class=\"m\">w(e) \\le w(f)</span>. So <span class=\"m\">T'</span> is an MST containing <span class=\"m\">e</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Run Kruskal on edges <span class=\"m\">PQ = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">PR = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">QR = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">QS = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">RS = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">ST = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">RT = 4</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Sorted: <span class=\"m\">PR</span> 1, <span class=\"m\">ST</span> 2, <span class=\"m\">PQ</span> 3, <span class=\"m\">QR</span> 3, <span class=\"m\">RT</span> 4, <span class=\"m\">RS</span> 5, <span class=\"m\">QS</span> 6.</li>\n<li>Take <span class=\"m\">PR</span> (1) and <span class=\"m\">ST</span> (2). Components {P, R}, {S, T}, {Q}.</li>\n<li>Take <span class=\"m\">PQ</span> (3). Skip <span class=\"m\">QR</span> (3): Q and R are both in {P, Q, R}.</li>\n<li>Take <span class=\"m\">RT</span> (4), which joins {P, Q, R} with {S, T}. We have 4 edges for 5 vertices: stop.</li>\n<li>MST weight <span class=\"m\">1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Run Prim from P on the same graph</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Tree {P}. Cheapest leaving edge: <span class=\"m\">PR</span> (1). Tree {P, R}.</li>\n<li>Leaving edges: <span class=\"m\">PQ</span> 3, <span class=\"m\">QR</span> 3, <span class=\"m\">RT</span> 4, <span class=\"m\">RS</span> 5. Take <span class=\"m\">PQ</span> (3) (a tie with <span class=\"m\">QR</span>; either works). Tree {P, Q, R}.</li>\n<li>Leaving edges: <span class=\"m\">RT</span> 4, <span class=\"m\">RS</span> 5, <span class=\"m\">QS</span> 6. Take <span class=\"m\">RT</span> (4). Tree {P, Q, R, T}.</li>\n<li>Leaving edges: <span class=\"m\">ST</span> 2, <span class=\"m\">RS</span> 5, <span class=\"m\">QS</span> 6. Take <span class=\"m\">ST</span> (2). All vertices reached.</li>\n<li>Total <span class=\"m\">1 + 3 + 4 + 2 = 10</span>, matching Kruskal.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A connected graph has 8 vertices and 12 edges, all with distinct weights. (a) How many edges does its MST have? (b) How many edges does Kruskal reject before finishing, at most? (c) Edge <span class=\"m\">e</span> is the heaviest edge of the whole graph and lies on a cycle. Can <span class=\"m\">e</span> be in the MST?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">n - 1 = 7</span>. (b) At most <span class=\"m\">12 - 7 = 5</span> edges are rejected (Kruskal may stop before even looking at some of them). (c) No. <span class=\"m\">e</span> is the strictly heaviest edge on that cycle (weights are distinct), so by the cycle property it is in no MST.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Network design (cables, pipelines, road maintenance), approximation algorithms (the MST gives a 2-approximation for the travelling salesman problem with metric distances) and image segmentation (Felzenszwalb's method merges pixel regions in Kruskal order) all use minimum spanning trees.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Single-linkage hierarchical clustering is exactly Kruskal's algorithm: merging the two closest clusters again and again builds the MST of the data points, and cutting the <span class=\"m\">k - 1</span> heaviest MST edges gives <span class=\"m\">k</span> clusters. HDBSCAN builds an MST on \"mutual reachability\" distances for density-based clustering.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Electricity grids, water pipes and early telephone networks were planned by connecting all customers at minimum total cost. The first MST algorithm, by Otakar Borůvka in 1926, was designed for the electrical network of Moravia.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many edges does a spanning tree of a connected graph with 20 vertices have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"19\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Any tree on <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices has <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Kruskal considers edges in which order?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">increasing weight</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">decreasing weight</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">BFS order from a root</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It greedily takes the lightest edge that does not create a cycle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A 4-cycle with edge weights 1, 2, 3, 4. What is the weight of its MST?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Remove the heaviest edge (4): <span class=\"m\">1 + 2 + 3 = 6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which fact justifies adding the lightest edge leaving the current tree in Prim's algorithm?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the cut property</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the handshake lemma</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the cycle property applied to the lightest edge</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The edge is the lightest crossing the cut between the tree and the rest.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If all edge weights are distinct, the MST is:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">unique</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">not necessarily unique</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">always a path</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>With distinct weights the cut property's lightest edge is unique for every cut, which forces the tree.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is the MST path between two vertices always a shortest path between them?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">no</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The MST minimises total weight, not individual distances.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "bipartite": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"two-kinds-of-things\" data-h=\"3\">Two kinds of things</h3>\n<p>Many networks connect two different <em>kinds</em> of object. Students are linked to the courses they take. Customers are linked to the films they rated. Doctors are linked to the shifts they can work. Words are linked to the documents that contain them. In each case an edge never joins two students or two courses; it always joins one of each kind. Graphs with this shape are called <strong>bipartite</strong>, and they are so common that they deserve a lesson of their own.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Bipartite graph</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A graph is bipartite if its vertex set can be split into two disjoint sets <span class=\"m\">L</span> and <span class=\"m\">R</span> (the two sides, or parts) so that every edge has one end in <span class=\"m\">L</span> and the other in <span class=\"m\">R</span>. Such a split is called a bipartition.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Complete bipartite graph <span class=\"m\">K_{a,b}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The bipartite graph with <span class=\"m\">a</span> vertices on one side, <span class=\"m\">b</span> on the other, and every one of the <span class=\"m\">ab</span> possible cross edges.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"seeing-it-as-a-colouring\" data-h=\"3\">Seeing it as a colouring</h3>\n<p>An equivalent way to say \"bipartite\" is: you can colour every vertex either red or blue so that no edge joins two vertices of the same colour. Red is one side and blue is the other. This viewpoint makes it easy to test. Take a path <span class=\"m\">1 - 2 - 3 - 4</span>. Colour 1 red. Its neighbour 2 must be blue. Then 3 must be red, and 4 blue. No conflict, so the path is bipartite with sides <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 3\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\{2, 4\\}</span>. Now take a triangle <span class=\"m\">1 - 2 - 3 - 1</span>. Colour 1 red, so 2 is blue, so 3 is red, but 3 is adjacent to 1, which is also red. Conflict. The triangle is not bipartite, and no clever choice could fix it: once one vertex's colour is chosen, every other colour is forced.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-odd-cycle-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">The odd cycle theorem</h3>\n<p>The triangle failed because it has odd length. Walk around any cycle in a bipartite graph and the colours alternate red, blue, red, blue. To come back to the starting colour you need an even number of steps. So <strong>a bipartite graph cannot contain an odd cycle</strong>. A 4-cycle, 6-cycle or the cube graph are fine; a 5-cycle or any graph containing a triangle is not.</p>\n<p>The remarkable thing is that the converse is also true: <strong>if a graph has no odd cycle, it is bipartite</strong>. Here is the idea. In each connected component pick a root <span class=\"m\">r</span> and colour each vertex by the parity of its distance from <span class=\"m\">r</span>: even distance red, odd distance blue. If some edge <span class=\"m\">uv</span> joined two vertices of the same colour, then <span class=\"m\">d(u)</span> and <span class=\"m\">d(v)</span> would have the same parity, and the path from <span class=\"m\">r</span> to <span class=\"m\">u</span>, the edge <span class=\"m\">uv</span>, and the path from <span class=\"m\">v</span> back to <span class=\"m\">r</span> would form a closed walk of odd length <span class=\"m\">d(u) + 1 + d(v)</span>. Every closed walk of odd length contains an odd cycle, which we assumed did not exist. So the colouring is proper.</p>\n<h3 id=\"testing-in-linear-time-with-bfs\" data-h=\"3\">Testing in linear time with BFS</h3>\n<p>The proof is also an algorithm. Run BFS from any vertex, colour vertices by the parity of their BFS layer, and check every edge. If all edges cross between colours, the graph is bipartite and you have the two sides. If some edge joins two vertices in layers of the same parity, follow both of their parent pointers back until they meet: the two paths plus the bad edge form an odd cycle, which is a certificate that the graph is not bipartite. All of this takes <span class=\"m\">O(n + m)</span> time.</p>\n<p>For example, in a 6-cycle <span class=\"m\">0 - 1 - 2 - 3 - 4 - 5 - 0</span>, BFS from 0 puts 1 and 5 in layer 1, then 2 and 4 in layer 2, then 3 in layer 3. Even layers: <span class=\"m\">\\{0, 2, 4\\}</span>; odd layers: <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 3, 5\\}</span>. Every edge goes between them. In a 5-cycle, BFS from 0 puts 1 and 4 in layer 1 and 2 and 3 in layer 2, and the edge <span class=\"m\">23</span> joins two layer-2 vertices: odd cycle <span class=\"m\">0 - 1 - 2 - 3 - 4 - 0</span> found.</p>\n<h3 id=\"counting-edges-in-bipartite-graphs\" data-h=\"3\">Counting edges in bipartite graphs</h3>\n<p>If the sides have sizes <span class=\"m\">a</span> and <span class=\"m\">b</span>, every edge picks one vertex from each side, so there are at most <span class=\"m\">ab</span> edges, achieved by <span class=\"m\">K_{a,b}</span>. For a fixed total <span class=\"m\">n = a + b</span>, the product <span class=\"m\">ab</span> is largest when the sides are as equal as possible, giving at most <span class=\"m\">\\lfloor n^2/4 \\rfloor</span> edges. Compare that with <span class=\"m\">n(n-1)/2</span> edges in <span class=\"m\">K_n</span>: a bipartite graph can have about half of all possible edges. The degree sums on the two sides are equal, because each edge contributes exactly one to each side: <span class=\"m\">\\sum_{v \\in L} \\deg v = \\sum_{v \\in R} \\deg v = m</span>.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;cube&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;bipartite&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The cube graph is bipartite: BFS colours the corners in two classes and every edge crosses.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"bipartite-graphs-hiding-in-plain-sight\" data-h=\"3\">Bipartite graphs hiding in plain sight</h3>\n<p>Once you start looking, bipartite graphs appear everywhere, sometimes in disguise. A chessboard's squares form a bipartite graph if you join squares a knight's move apart: every knight move goes from a light square to a dark square, so the colours of the board are the bipartition. This is why a knight returns to its starting square only after an even number of moves. A grid of streets is bipartite: colour each intersection by whether (row + column) is even or odd, and every block changes the parity by one. The hypercube graph used in parallel computers joins binary strings that differ in one bit, and it is bipartite by the parity of the number of 1s. Whenever there is a quantity that changes by exactly one along every edge, its parity gives a 2-colouring.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>\"Bipartite\" is about the <em>possibility</em> of a split, not about how a picture is drawn. The cube graph drawn as two nested squares does not look like two columns, but it is bipartite. Also, a graph with no triangles is not automatically bipartite: the 5-cycle has no triangle but has an odd cycle. Finally, for disconnected graphs, check every component; the graph is bipartite exactly when every component is.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A graph is bipartite when its vertices split into two sides with all edges crossing, equivalently when it can be properly 2-coloured. The odd cycle theorem says this happens exactly when there is no cycle of odd length. BFS layer parity gives a linear-time test that either produces the bipartition or exhibits an odd cycle. Bipartite graphs model all \"two kinds of thing\" relationships and are the setting for matching, the next lesson.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;c6&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;bipartite&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Run the 2-colouring test on different graphs.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Start with the 6-cycle. The readout shows the two sides {0, 2, 4} and {1, 3, 5} found from BFS layer parity.\n2. Switch the graph to the 5-cycle. The test fails and highlights an odd cycle, the certificate from the article.\n3. Try <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span> and the cube: both bipartite. Try <span class=\"m\">K_4</span>, the wheel and the Petersen graph: all contain odd cycles.\n4. Click a vertex to start the BFS from a different place. The sides are the same (up to swapping colours) for a connected graph.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> whenever the test fails, the highlighted cycle has odd length, and whenever it succeeds every edge crosses between colours. There is no third outcome, which is exactly the odd cycle theorem.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">G \\text{ bipartite} \\iff G \\text{ has a proper 2-colouring} \\iff G \\text{ has no odd cycle}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">|E(K_{a,b})| = ab, \\qquad |E| \\le \\left\\lfloor \\tfrac{n^2}{4} \\right\\rfloor \\text{ for bipartite } G \\text{ on } n \\text{ vertices}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_{v \\in L} \\deg v = \\sum_{v \\in R} \\deg v = m</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Odd cycle theorem (König, 1936)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A graph is bipartite if and only if it contains no cycle of odd length.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(<span class=\"m\">\\Rightarrow</span>) Along any cycle the sides alternate, so returning to the start takes an even number of edges. (<span class=\"m\">\\Leftarrow</span>) Work in one component; fix a root <span class=\"m\">r</span> and let <span class=\"m\">L</span> be the vertices at even distance, <span class=\"m\">R</span> those at odd distance. Suppose an edge <span class=\"m\">uv</span> had both ends in the same set. Then <span class=\"m\">d(u) + d(v) + 1</span> is odd, and the shortest paths <span class=\"m\">r \\to u</span>, edge <span class=\"m\">uv</span>, and <span class=\"m\">v \\to r</span> form a closed walk of odd length. A closed walk of odd length always contains an odd cycle (if it repeats a vertex, split it into two shorter closed walks, one of which has odd length, and repeat). This contradicts the assumption, so every edge crosses and the graph is bipartite.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Is the graph with edges <span class=\"m\">12, 23, 34, 41, 35, 56, 61</span> bipartite?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>BFS from 1: layer 0 {1}, layer 1 {2, 4, 6}, layer 2 {3, 5}.</li>\n<li>Check edges: <span class=\"m\">12, 41, 61</span> cross layers 0–1; <span class=\"m\">23, 34, 56</span> cross 1–2.</li>\n<li>Edge <span class=\"m\">35</span> joins two layer-2 vertices: conflict.</li>\n<li>Odd cycle: <span class=\"m\">3 - 5 - 6 - 1 - 2 - 3</span> has 5 edges. Not bipartite.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">What is the largest number of edges in a bipartite graph on 9 vertices?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Sides of sizes <span class=\"m\">a</span> and <span class=\"m\">9 - a</span> allow at most <span class=\"m\">a(9 - a)</span> edges.</li>\n<li>Try values: <span class=\"m\">a = 4</span> gives 20, <span class=\"m\">a = 3</span> gives 18, <span class=\"m\">a = 5</span> gives 20.</li>\n<li>Maximum <span class=\"m\">= \\lfloor 81/4 \\rfloor = 20</span>, achieved by <span class=\"m\">K_{4,5}</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(a) For which <span class=\"m\">n \\ge 3</span> is the cycle <span class=\"m\">C_n</span> bipartite? (b) Is the 3-dimensional cube graph (8 corners, 12 edges) bipartite? Give the sides. (c) A bipartite graph has sides of size 6 and 10 and every vertex on the size-6 side has degree 5. What is the average degree on the size-10 side?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Exactly when <span class=\"m\">n</span> is even. (b) Yes: label corners by binary strings <span class=\"m\">xyz</span>; each edge flips one bit, so it changes the parity of the number of 1s. Sides: even weight <span class=\"m\">\\{000, 011, 101, 110\\}</span> and odd weight <span class=\"m\">\\{001, 010, 100, 111\\}</span>. (c) <span class=\"m\">m = 6 \\times 5 = 30</span>, so the other side's degrees also sum to 30, average <span class=\"m\">30/10 = 3</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Recommender systems store user–item interactions as a bipartite graph. Matrix factorisation (user and item embeddings) and graph-based recommenders like PinSage and LightGCN pass messages back and forth across the two sides. Restricted Boltzmann machines are called \"restricted\" precisely because their visible and hidden units form a bipartite graph, which makes sampling one side given the other easy.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Bipartiteness checking appears in scheduling (two-shift assignments), in 2-SAT style constraint problems, and in compilers. Every \"is this conflict graph 2-colourable?\" question is a bipartite test.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A dance class wants to pair people so that partners always come from two different groups. If \"must not be in the same group\" relations contain an odd cycle (A–B, B–C, C–A), no assignment into two groups exists.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which graph is NOT bipartite?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a 4-cycle</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a tree</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a 7-cycle</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A cycle of odd length is never bipartite.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many edges does <span class=\"m\">K_{3,5}</span> have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"15\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3 \\times 5 = 15</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">BFS finds an edge joining two vertices in the same layer. What can you conclude?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the graph has an odd cycle, so it is not bipartite</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the graph is bipartite</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the graph is disconnected</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Same-layer edges close an odd cycle with the BFS paths.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the maximum number of edges in a bipartite graph on 10 vertices?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"25\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\lfloor 100/4 \\rfloor = 25</span>, from <span class=\"m\">K_{5,5}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Every tree is bipartite because:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">it has no cycles at all, so in particular no odd cycles</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it has <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span> edges</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">its leaves form one side</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>No cycles means no odd cycles.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "matching": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"assigning-people-to-jobs\" data-h=\"3\">Assigning people to jobs</h3>\n<p>A small company has four workers and four jobs. Each worker is qualified for only some of the jobs. Can every worker get a job they are qualified for, with no job given to two workers? If not, what is the largest number of workers who can be placed? This is the <strong>matching problem</strong>. Draw a bipartite graph with workers on the left, jobs on the right, and an edge for each qualification. A valid assignment is a set of edges with no shared endpoints.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Matching</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A matching in a graph is a set of edges no two of which share a vertex. A vertex touched by a matching edge is matched; otherwise it is free (unmatched).</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Maximum and perfect matching</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A maximum matching has the largest possible number of edges. A perfect matching matches every vertex, so it has <span class=\"m\">n/2</span> edges.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"maximal-is-not-maximum\" data-h=\"3\">Maximal is not maximum</h3>\n<p>The obvious greedy approach is to keep adding any edge whose ends are both free. When you get stuck, you have a <strong>maximal</strong> matching: no edge can be added. But maximal is not the same as maximum. Take the path <span class=\"m\">a - b - c - d</span>. If greedy picks the middle edge <span class=\"m\">bc</span> first, both other edges are blocked, so you are stuck at size 1. Yet <span class=\"m\">\\{ab, cd\\}</span> has size 2. A greedy maximal matching is always at least half the size of a maximum one, but it can be that bad.</p>\n<h3 id=\"augmenting-paths\" data-h=\"3\">Augmenting paths</h3>\n<p>How do you escape from the stuck matching <span class=\"m\">\\{bc\\}</span>? Look at the path <span class=\"m\">a - b - c - d</span>. It starts at a free vertex <span class=\"m\">a</span>, goes along a non-matching edge <span class=\"m\">ab</span>, then a matching edge <span class=\"m\">bc</span>, then a non-matching edge <span class=\"m\">cd</span>, ending at a free vertex <span class=\"m\">d</span>. Its edges alternate between \"not in the matching\" and \"in the matching\", and both ends are free. Such a path is called an <strong>augmenting path</strong>. Flip every edge on it: matched edges become unmatched and vice versa. The path had one more non-matching edge than matching edges, so the matching grows by one: <span class=\"m\">\\{bc\\}</span> becomes <span class=\"m\">\\{ab, cd\\}</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Berge's theorem</strong> says this is the whole story: a matching is maximum if and only if there is no augmenting path. So an algorithm can start from the empty matching and repeatedly search for an augmenting path, flipping it each time. In bipartite graphs the search is a BFS or DFS that alternates between non-matching and matching edges. Each search costs <span class=\"m\">O(m)</span> and there are at most <span class=\"m\">n/2</span> augmentations, giving <span class=\"m\">O(nm)</span>. The Hopcroft–Karp algorithm finds many augmenting paths at once and runs in <span class=\"m\">O(m\\sqrt{n})</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-is-a-perfect-matching-impossible-hall-s-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">When is a perfect matching impossible? Hall's theorem</h3>\n<p>Suppose workers Asha and Ben are both qualified only for job 1. Then whatever you do, one of them gets nothing. In general, if some group <span class=\"m\">S</span> of workers is qualified, all together, for fewer than <span class=\"m\">|S|</span> jobs, they cannot all be placed. Write <span class=\"m\">N(S)</span> for the set of jobs adjacent to at least one worker in <span class=\"m\">S</span>. The obstruction is <span class=\"m\">|N(S)| &lt; |S|</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hall's marriage theorem</strong> says that this obvious obstruction is the <em>only</em> one. In a bipartite graph with left side <span class=\"m\">L</span>, there is a matching that covers every vertex of <span class=\"m\">L</span> if and only if\n<span class=\"mb\">|N(S)| \\ge |S| \\quad \\text{for every subset } S \\subseteq L.</span>\nThis is surprising: to show a matching exists you don't need to construct it, just check a counting condition. And if no such matching exists, a violating set <span class=\"m\">S</span> is a short proof of impossibility.</p>\n<h3 id=\"k-nig-s-theorem-matchings-and-covers\" data-h=\"3\">König's theorem: matchings and covers</h3>\n<p>A <strong>vertex cover</strong> is a set of vertices touching every edge. Think of it as placing guards on vertices so that every edge has a guard at one end. Each matching edge needs its own guard, since no vertex covers two matching edges. So for any graph, (size of any matching) <span class=\"m\">\\le</span> (size of any vertex cover). In bipartite graphs something much stronger holds. <strong>König's theorem</strong>: the size of a maximum matching <em>equals</em> the size of a minimum vertex cover. For example, in the jobs graph where Asha and Ben only like job 1, the maximum matching has size 3 and the three vertices {job 1, Chen, Dev} touch every edge, proving that 3 is optimal. In non-bipartite graphs the equality can fail: the triangle has maximum matching 1 but needs 2 vertices to cover its 3 edges.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;jobs&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;matching&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>A perfect matching of four workers (left) to four jobs (right).</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-the-two-theorems-fit-together\" data-h=\"3\">Why the two theorems fit together</h3>\n<p>Hall's theorem and König's theorem are really two views of the same fact. Suppose the left side cannot be fully matched and the maximum matching has size <span class=\"m\">k &lt; |L|</span>. König gives a vertex cover <span class=\"m\">C</span> with <span class=\"m\">|C| = k</span>. Let <span class=\"m\">S</span> be the left vertices that are <em>not</em> in <span class=\"m\">C</span>. Every edge from <span class=\"m\">S</span> must be covered at its right end, so <span class=\"m\">N(S)</span> lies inside the right part of <span class=\"m\">C</span>. Counting, <span class=\"m\">|N(S)| \\le |C| - (|L| - |S|) = k - |L| + |S| &lt; |S|</span>, which is exactly a violation of Hall's condition. In the same way, the augmenting-path algorithm, when it finishes, hands you both a maximum matching and a matching-sized cover, so you always get a certificate of optimality along with the answer. This idea of a \"dual\" object proving optimality reappears in network flows, where a minimum cut proves a flow is maximum.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Do not stop at a maximal matching and call it maximum. Check for augmenting paths. When applying Hall's condition, you must check <em>every</em> subset <span class=\"m\">S</span> of the left side, not just single vertices: each worker may individually have two options while a group of three shares only two jobs. And remember that Hall's theorem guarantees a matching that saturates one side; a perfect matching additionally needs <span class=\"m\">|L| = |R|</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A matching is a set of disjoint edges. Maximal (cannot add an edge) is weaker than maximum (largest size). Augmenting paths, alternating paths between two free vertices, grow a matching by one, and Berge's theorem says a matching is maximum exactly when none exists. In bipartite graphs, Hall's condition <span class=\"m\">|N(S)| \\ge |S|</span> characterises when one side can be fully matched, and König's theorem says maximum matching equals minimum vertex cover.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;jobs&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;matching&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Compare a graph with a perfect matching to one that violates Hall's condition.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the jobs graph, read the maximum matching and the ringed minimum vertex cover. Both have size 4, and the matching is perfect.\n2. Switch to the \"jobs-hall\" graph. Workers 0 and 1 are both adjacent only to job 4, so <span class=\"m\">S = \\{0, 1\\}</span> has <span class=\"m\">|N(S)| = 1 &lt; 2</span>.\n3. Read the new maximum matching size, 3, and check that the cover also has size 3, as König's theorem promises.\n4. Switch to the 5-cycle or <span class=\"m\">K_4</span>: not bipartite. Compare matching size and cover size.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> in bipartite graphs the thick matching edges and the ringed cover vertices always come in equal numbers, so each certifies the other is optimal. In the 5-cycle, matching size is 2 but the cover needs 3.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">|M| \\le |C| \\text{ for any matching } M \\text{ and vertex cover } C</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{bipartite: } \\max |M| = \\min |C| \\quad \\text{(König)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Hall: } L \\text{ can be fully matched} \\iff |N(S)| \\ge |S| \\;\\; \\forall S \\subseteq L</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{augmenting path with } k \\text{ matched edges has } k+1 \\text{ unmatched edges: } |M \\oplus P| = |M| + 1</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Berge's theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A matching <span class=\"m\">M</span> is maximum if and only if there is no <span class=\"m\">M</span>-augmenting path.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If an augmenting path <span class=\"m\">P</span> exists, <span class=\"m\">M \\oplus P</span> (flip the edges of <span class=\"m\">P</span>) is a matching with one more edge, so <span class=\"m\">M</span> is not maximum. Conversely, suppose <span class=\"m\">M'</span> is a bigger matching. In the symmetric difference <span class=\"m\">M \\oplus M'</span>, every vertex has degree at most 2, so it splits into paths and even cycles whose edges alternate between <span class=\"m\">M</span> and <span class=\"m\">M'</span>. Cycles have equally many edges of each, and since <span class=\"m\">|M'| &gt; |M|</span> some path has more <span class=\"m\">M'</span> edges than <span class=\"m\">M</span> edges. That path starts and ends with <span class=\"m\">M'</span> edges at vertices free in <span class=\"m\">M</span>, so it is an <span class=\"m\">M</span>-augmenting path.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Hall's marriage theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A bipartite graph with sides <span class=\"m\">L</span> and <span class=\"m\">R</span> has a matching covering all of <span class=\"m\">L</span> if and only if <span class=\"m\">|N(S)| \\ge |S|</span> for every <span class=\"m\">S \\subseteq L</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find a maximum matching: <span class=\"m\">L = \\{1, 2, 3\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">R = \\{a, b, c\\}</span>, edges <span class=\"m\">1a, 1b, 2a, 3b, 3c</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Greedy: take <span class=\"m\">1a</span>, then <span class=\"m\">3b</span>. Vertex 2's only neighbour <span class=\"m\">a</span> is taken: matching <span class=\"m\">\\{1a, 3b\\}</span>, size 2.</li>\n<li>Search for an augmenting path from free vertex 2: <span class=\"m\">2 - a</span> (unmatched), <span class=\"m\">a - 1</span> (matched), <span class=\"m\">1 - b</span> (unmatched), <span class=\"m\">b - 3</span> (matched), <span class=\"m\">3 - c</span> (unmatched), and <span class=\"m\">c</span> is free.</li>\n<li>Flip: remove <span class=\"m\">1a, 3b</span>; add <span class=\"m\">2a, 1b, 3c</span>.</li>\n<li>New matching <span class=\"m\">\\{2a, 1b, 3c\\}</span> has size 3: perfect.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show there is no perfect matching: <span class=\"m\">L = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">R = \\{a, b, c, d\\}</span>, edges <span class=\"m\">1a, 2a, 2b, 3a, 3b, 4c, 4d</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Take <span class=\"m\">S = \\{1, 2, 3\\}</span>. Their neighbours: <span class=\"m\">N(S) = \\{a, b\\}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">|N(S)| = 2 &lt; 3 = |S|</span>: Hall's condition fails.</li>\n<li>So at most 2 of vertices 1, 2, 3 can be matched, and the maximum matching has size at most 3 (e.g. <span class=\"m\">1a, 2b, 4c</span>).</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Five students each choose some of five project topics: P1 likes {A, B}, P2 likes {A}, P3 likes {B, C}, P4 likes {C, D, E}, P5 likes {D}. (a) Find a perfect matching. (b) If P3 changes to like only {A}, prove no perfect matching exists. (c) In case (b), what is the maximum matching size?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) P2–A, P1–B, P3–C, P5–D, P4–E. (b) <span class=\"m\">S = \\{P1, P2, P3\\}</span> now has <span class=\"m\">N(S) = \\{A, B\\}</span>, and <span class=\"m\">2 &lt; 3</span>, so Hall fails. (c) 4: for example P2–A, P1–B, P4–C, P5–D. It cannot be 5 by part (b).</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Matching assigns tasks to servers, students to schools (the stable-matching algorithm behind school admissions and the US medical residency match), and ride requests to drivers. In computer vision, the Hungarian algorithm (a weighted bipartite matching method) matches detected objects across video frames.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>DETR, a transformer-based object detector, trains by computing a minimum-cost bipartite matching between predicted boxes and ground-truth boxes with the Hungarian algorithm, then applying the loss to matched pairs. Optimal transport between two discrete distributions with equal weights reduces to an assignment (perfect matching) problem.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Kidney exchange programmes pair incompatible donor–patient couples with each other so that more transplants can happen; finding the best set of swaps is a matching problem on a large compatibility graph.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the path <span class=\"m\">a - b - c - d</span>, which matching is maximal but not maximum?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\{bc\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\{ab, cd\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\{ab\\}</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\{bc\\}</span> blocks both other edges but <span class=\"m\">\\{ab, cd\\}</span> is larger. <span class=\"m\">\\{ab\\}</span> is not maximal since <span class=\"m\">cd</span> can be added.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">An augmenting path contains 3 matched edges. How many unmatched edges does it have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It alternates and starts and ends with unmatched edges, so it has one more unmatched edge.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Three left vertices have combined neighbourhood of size 2. What does Hall's theorem say?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">no matching covers the whole left side</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">there is a perfect matching</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the graph is not bipartite</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">|N(S)| &lt; |S|</span> is exactly Hall's obstruction.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In a bipartite graph the maximum matching has size 7. What is the size of a minimum vertex cover?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>König's theorem: they are equal in bipartite graphs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the maximum matching size of a triangle?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Any two edges of a triangle share a vertex.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many edges does a perfect matching on 12 vertices have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each edge matches two vertices: <span class=\"m\">12/2 = 6</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "euler-hamilton": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-seven-bridges-of-k-nigsberg\" data-h=\"3\">The seven bridges of Königsberg</h3>\n<p>In the 1700s the city of Königsberg (now Kaliningrad) was built around a river with two islands, joined to the banks and to each other by seven bridges. Residents wondered whether they could take a Sunday walk that crossed every bridge exactly once. Nobody could find such a walk, but nobody could prove it was impossible either. In 1736 Leonhard Euler settled the question, and in doing so he invented graph theory. He replaced each land mass with a dot and each bridge with a line, and noticed that the shape of the land did not matter at all: only which land masses each bridge joined.</p>\n<p>The Königsberg graph has 4 vertices (two banks, two islands) and 7 edges. Their degrees are 5, 3, 3 and 3. Euler's insight was about what happens <em>in the middle</em> of a walk.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Euler trail and Euler circuit</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An Euler trail is a walk that uses every edge of the graph exactly once. An Euler circuit is an Euler trail that starts and ends at the same vertex.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-parity-argument\" data-h=\"3\">The parity argument</h3>\n<p>Follow a walker through a vertex <span class=\"m\">v</span> that is neither the start nor the end. Each time they arrive on one edge, they must leave on another. So the edges at <span class=\"m\">v</span> get used up in pairs: one in, one out. If the walk uses every edge exactly once, <span class=\"m\">\\deg v</span> must be even. Only the start and end vertices can have odd degree, because the walk leaves the start once without arriving and arrives at the end once without leaving. If the walk is a circuit, start and end are the same vertex, and its degree is even too.</p>\n<p>So: an Euler circuit requires every vertex to have even degree, and an Euler trail requires either zero or exactly two odd-degree vertices. Königsberg has four odd vertices, so the walk is impossible. That is the whole proof.</p>\n<h3 id=\"and-the-condition-is-enough\" data-h=\"3\">And the condition is enough</h3>\n<p>The surprising part is the converse. If the graph is connected (ignoring isolated vertices) and every degree is even, an Euler circuit always exists. If exactly two vertices are odd, an Euler trail exists, starting at one odd vertex and ending at the other. Here is why. Start anywhere and walk along unused edges. Because every degree is even, whenever you enter a vertex other than the start there is an unused edge to leave by, so you can only get stuck back at the start. You now have a closed circuit, though it might miss some edges. If it does, some vertex on your circuit still has unused edges (the graph is connected). Walk a new closed loop from there using only unused edges, and splice it into the first circuit at that vertex. Repeat until no edges remain. This splicing method is <strong>Hierholzer's algorithm</strong>, and it runs in <span class=\"m\">O(m)</span> time.</p>\n<p>For two odd vertices <span class=\"m\">a</span> and <span class=\"m\">b</span>, add a temporary extra edge <span class=\"m\">ab</span>. Now all degrees are even, so there is an Euler circuit; delete the temporary edge from it and you get an Euler trail from <span class=\"m\">a</span> to <span class=\"m\">b</span>.</p>\n<p>A familiar puzzle: draw the \"house\" or envelope shape without lifting your pen and without redrawing a line. The envelope (a square with both diagonals plus a roof) has two vertices of degree 3 at the bottom and all others even, so it can be done, but only if you start at one bottom corner and finish at the other.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;envelope&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;euler&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The envelope has exactly two odd vertices, so an Euler trail exists between them.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"hamilton-every-vertex-instead-of-every-edge\" data-h=\"3\">Hamilton: every vertex instead of every edge</h3>\n<p>A century later, William Rowan Hamilton sold a puzzle called the Icosian game: find a route along the edges of a dodecahedron that visits every corner exactly once and returns to the start. The question looks like Euler's with \"edge\" replaced by \"vertex\", but it behaves completely differently.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Hamilton path and Hamilton cycle</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A Hamilton path visits every vertex exactly once. A Hamilton cycle is a Hamilton path whose last vertex is adjacent to its first, closing a cycle through all <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices. A graph with a Hamilton cycle is called Hamiltonian.</p></div></div>\n<p>No simple test like \"check the degrees\" is known for Hamilton cycles. Deciding whether a graph has one is <strong>NP-complete</strong>, which means that a fast general algorithm would give fast algorithms for thousands of other hard problems, and most computer scientists believe none exists. What we do have are <strong>sufficient</strong> conditions, which guarantee a Hamilton cycle when they hold but say nothing when they fail, and <strong>obstacles</strong> that rule one out.</p>\n<p>The best-known sufficient condition is <strong>Dirac's theorem</strong>: if <span class=\"m\">n \\ge 3</span> and every vertex has degree at least <span class=\"m\">n/2</span>, the graph has a Hamilton cycle. For example <span class=\"m\">K_n</span> (degree <span class=\"m\">n - 1</span>) and <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span> (degree <span class=\"m\">3 = 6/2</span>) qualify. Ore's theorem is a refinement: it is enough that <span class=\"m\">\\deg u + \\deg v \\ge n</span> for every pair of non-adjacent vertices <span class=\"m\">u, v</span>.</p>\n<p>Useful obstacles: a vertex of degree 1 rules out a Hamilton cycle (you could enter it but never leave on a fresh edge). A cut vertex rules it out too, because a cycle through both sides would have to pass through the cut vertex twice. A bipartite graph with sides of unequal size has no Hamilton cycle, because the cycle alternates sides and so uses equally many vertices from each. And the Petersen graph, 10 vertices of degree 3, has a Hamilton path but no Hamilton cycle, a classic small counterexample to many hopeful conjectures.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Do not mix the two problems up: Euler is about <strong>edges</strong> and is easy; Hamilton is about <strong>vertices</strong> and is hard. Dirac's theorem is one-way: a cycle <span class=\"m\">C_n</span> has every degree 2, which is less than <span class=\"m\">n/2</span> for <span class=\"m\">n \\ge 5</span>, yet it is itself a Hamilton cycle. When applying Euler's test, remember connectivity: two separate triangles have all degrees even but no single circuit can cover both.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>An Euler circuit exists exactly when the graph is connected and all degrees are even; an Euler trail exists exactly when the graph is connected and has zero or two odd vertices. Hierholzer's splicing algorithm finds one in linear time. Hamilton cycles, visiting every vertex once, have no known efficient test; Dirac's and Ore's theorems give sufficient conditions, while degree-1 vertices, cut vertices and unbalanced bipartite sides are obstacles.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;envelope&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;euler&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Test Euler's condition, then switch to Hamilton mode on the same graphs.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. On the envelope, read the Euler trail. It starts at vertex 0 and ends at vertex 1, the two odd-degree vertices. Press Animate to watch the edge order.\n2. Switch to <span class=\"m\">K_5</span> (all degrees 4): an Euler circuit. Switch to <span class=\"m\">K_4</span> or the cube (all degrees 3): no Euler trail, and the odd vertices are ringed.\n3. Change the mode to Hamilton. <span class=\"m\">K_4</span>, <span class=\"m\">K_5</span> and <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span> satisfy Dirac's condition; the readout confirms it.\n4. Choose the Petersen graph in Hamilton mode: only a Hamilton path is found. Then try the tree: no Hamilton path at all.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the Euler answer can always be predicted from degrees alone, exactly as the parity argument says. The Hamilton answer cannot: the cube has every degree 3, less than <span class=\"m\">n/2 = 4</span>, and still has a Hamilton cycle, while the Petersen graph, also 3-regular, has none.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Euler circuit} \\iff \\text{connected and every } \\deg v \\text{ even}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Euler trail} \\iff \\text{connected and exactly 0 or 2 vertices of odd degree}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Dirac: } n \\ge 3,\\; \\delta(G) \\ge \\tfrac{n}{2} \\Rightarrow \\text{Hamiltonian}; \\qquad \\text{Ore: } \\deg u + \\deg v \\ge n \\text{ for all non-adjacent } u,v</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\text{Hamilton cycles in } K_n = \\frac{(n-1)!}{2}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Euler's theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A connected graph has an Euler circuit if and only if every vertex has even degree.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If an Euler circuit exists, each visit to a vertex uses one edge in and one edge out, so degrees are even. Conversely, use induction on the number of edges. Since all degrees are even and the graph has an edge, every vertex of positive degree has degree at least 2, so there is a cycle <span class=\"m\">C</span>. Removing the edges of <span class=\"m\">C</span> leaves all degrees even. Each component of what remains has fewer edges and so, by induction, an Euler circuit; each such component shares a vertex with <span class=\"m\">C</span> (the original graph is connected). Traverse <span class=\"m\">C</span>, and on reaching each component's shared vertex for the first time, detour around that component's Euler circuit. This uses every edge exactly once.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Can you draw the graph with edges <span class=\"m\">AB, BC, CD, DA, AC, BD, AE, EB</span> in one stroke?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Degrees: A: B, D, C, E → 4. B: A, C, D, E → 4. C: B, D, A → 3. D: C, A, B → 3. E: A, B → 2.</li>\n<li>Exactly two odd vertices (C and D), and the graph is connected.</li>\n<li>So an Euler trail exists from C to D (but no Euler circuit).</li>\n<li>One trail: C–A–B–C–D–A–E–B–D. It uses all 8 edges once.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How many distinct Hamilton cycles does <span class=\"m\">K_5</span> have?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Fix a starting vertex (rotations give the same cycle): arrange the other 4 in order, <span class=\"m\">4! = 24</span> ways.</li>\n<li>Each cycle is counted twice, once in each direction.</li>\n<li>Answer <span class=\"m\">24/2 = 12</span>, matching <span class=\"m\">(n-1)!/2</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(a) Königsberg has land masses with degrees 5, 3, 3, 3. What is the minimum number of new bridges needed so that a walk crossing every bridge exactly once exists (it need not return to the start)? (b) A bipartite graph has sides of size 4 and 5. Can it have a Hamilton cycle? (c) A graph on 10 vertices has minimum degree 5. What does Dirac's theorem say?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) An Euler trail needs at most 2 odd vertices. Adding one bridge between two odd land masses makes both even, leaving 2 odd vertices. So 1 new bridge suffices. (b) No: a Hamilton cycle alternates sides and would need equal sides. (c) <span class=\"m\">5 \\ge 10/2</span>, so the graph has a Hamilton cycle.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Euler trails assemble DNA: in de Bruijn graph genome assembly, each short sequencing read becomes an edge, and the genome is an Euler trail through them. Snow ploughs and postal routes use the Chinese Postman problem, which adds the cheapest duplicate edges to make all degrees even. Hamilton cycles appear in the travelling salesman problem, which asks for the cheapest Hamilton cycle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Routing and tour-planning models trained with reinforcement learning or attention (the \"attention model\" for the travelling salesman problem) are evaluated on how close their tours come to optimal Hamilton cycles, because exact solutions are intractable for large inputs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Drawing a figure \"without lifting your pen\" is an Euler trail puzzle, and the answer is always decided by counting odd corners: zero means you can start anywhere, two means you must start at one of them, more than two means it is impossible.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A connected graph has degrees 2, 4, 4, 3, 3, 2. Does it have an Euler circuit, an Euler trail, or neither?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Euler circuit</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Euler trail only</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">neither</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Exactly two odd vertices, so a trail between them but no circuit.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many odd-degree vertices did the Königsberg graph have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Degrees 5, 3, 3, 3: all four are odd.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which problem is NP-complete in general?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">deciding if a graph has an Euler circuit</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">deciding if a graph has a Hamilton cycle</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">finding the degree of every vertex</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Euler is decided by degrees in linear time; Hamilton has no known fast test.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many Hamilton cycles does <span class=\"m\">K_4</span> have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(4-1)!/2 = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A graph has 12 vertices and minimum degree 6. Is it guaranteed to be Hamiltonian?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">yes, by Dirac's theorem</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">no</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">only if it is bipartite</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">6 \\ge 12/2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A graph has a vertex of degree 1. Can it have a Hamilton cycle?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">no</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A cycle needs two edges at every vertex.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "planarity": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-utilities-puzzle\" data-h=\"3\">The three utilities puzzle</h3>\n<p>Here is an old puzzle. Three houses each need to be connected to three utilities: water, gas and electricity. Can you draw all nine pipes on a flat sheet of paper so that no two pipes cross? People have tried for over a century, and it always fails: you can draw eight pipes without crossings, but the ninth always has to cross something. The graph in this puzzle is <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span>, and this lesson explains why it can never be drawn flat.</p>\n<p>The same question matters for real engineering. On a printed circuit board, wires on one layer cannot cross. A road map cannot have roads crossing without a junction or bridge. In graph drawing software, crossings make diagrams hard to read. All of these ask: when can a graph be drawn in the plane with no edges crossing?</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Planar graph</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A graph is planar if it can be drawn in the plane so that edges meet only at shared endpoints (no crossings). Such a crossing-free drawing is called a plane graph or a planar embedding.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Face</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In a plane drawing, the faces are the regions the edges divide the plane into, including the single unbounded outer region.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"planar-is-about-the-graph-not-the-picture\" data-h=\"3\">Planar is about the graph, not the picture</h3>\n<p>A graph might be drawn with crossings and still be planar. <span class=\"m\">K_4</span> is usually drawn as a square with both diagonals, which has one crossing. But you can move one vertex into the middle of the triangle formed by the other three, and all six edges become crossing-free. So \"planar\" means \"there exists a crossing-free drawing\", and to prove a graph is planar you just exhibit one. Proving a graph is <em>not</em> planar is harder, because you must rule out every possible drawing. That is where Euler's formula comes in.</p>\n<h3 id=\"euler-s-formula\" data-h=\"3\">Euler's formula</h3>\n<p>Count vertices <span class=\"m\">V</span>, edges <span class=\"m\">E</span> and faces <span class=\"m\">F</span> in any connected plane drawing. A triangle: <span class=\"m\">V = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">E = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">F = 2</span> (inside and outside). A square with one diagonal: <span class=\"m\">V = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">E = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">F = 3</span>. A cube drawn as two nested squares: <span class=\"m\">V = 8</span>, <span class=\"m\">E = 12</span>, <span class=\"m\">F = 6</span>. In every case\n<span class=\"mb\">V - E + F = 2.</span>\nThis is <strong>Euler's formula</strong>, and it holds for every connected plane graph, however it is drawn.</p>\n<p>Here is why. Start with a spanning tree of the graph. A tree has <span class=\"m\">E = V - 1</span> and one face (it encloses nothing), so <span class=\"m\">V - E + F = V - (V - 1) + 1 = 2</span>. Now add the remaining edges one at a time. Each added edge joins two vertices already connected in the tree, so it closes a cycle and splits one face into two. That adds 1 to <span class=\"m\">E</span> and 1 to <span class=\"m\">F</span>, leaving <span class=\"m\">V - E + F</span> unchanged. When all edges are back, the value is still 2.</p>\n<h3 id=\"counting-faces-gives-an-edge-limit\" data-h=\"3\">Counting faces gives an edge limit</h3>\n<p>Euler's formula turns into a powerful test. In a simple plane graph with at least 3 vertices, every face is bounded by at least 3 edges (no loops or double edges, so no face with 1 or 2 sides). If we go round every face and count its edges, we count each edge at most twice, once from each side. So <span class=\"m\">3F \\le 2E</span>. Substitute <span class=\"m\">F = 2 - V + E</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">3(2 - V + E) \\le 2E \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad E \\le 3V - 6.</span>\n<strong>A simple planar graph with <span class=\"m\">V \\ge 3</span> has at most <span class=\"m\">3V - 6</span> edges.</strong> If the graph has no triangles, every face has at least 4 sides, so <span class=\"m\">4F \\le 2E</span>, which gives <span class=\"m\">E \\le 2V - 4</span>.</p>\n<p>Now test the famous examples. <span class=\"m\">K_5</span> has <span class=\"m\">V = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">E = 10</span>, but <span class=\"m\">3V - 6 = 9</span>. Too many edges: <strong><span class=\"m\">K_5</span> is not planar</strong>. <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span> has <span class=\"m\">V = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">E = 9</span>, and <span class=\"m\">3V - 6 = 12</span>, so the first test passes. But <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span> is bipartite, so it has no triangles, and <span class=\"m\">2V - 4 = 8 &lt; 9</span>. <strong><span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span> is not planar</strong>, which solves the utilities puzzle.</p>\n<p>The same counting shows every planar graph has a vertex of degree at most 5. If every degree were at least 6, the handshake lemma would give <span class=\"m\">2E \\ge 6V</span>, so <span class=\"m\">E \\ge 3V</span>, contradicting <span class=\"m\">E \\le 3V - 6</span>. This small fact is the key step in proving that planar maps can be coloured with few colours (next lesson).</p>\n<h3 id=\"kuratowski-s-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">Kuratowski's theorem</h3>\n<p>The edge bounds are only one-way tests: a graph can pass them and still be non-planar. The Petersen graph has <span class=\"m\">V = 10</span>, <span class=\"m\">E = 15</span>, no triangles, and <span class=\"m\">2V - 4 = 16 \\ge 15</span>, so both tests pass, yet it is not planar. (Its shortest cycle has length 5, and the sharper bound <span class=\"m\">5F \\le 2E</span> gives <span class=\"m\">E \\le \\tfrac{5}{3}(V - 2) \\approx 13.3</span>, which it fails.)</p>\n<p>The complete answer is <strong>Kuratowski's theorem</strong> (1930): a graph is planar if and only if it does not contain a <strong>subdivision</strong> of <span class=\"m\">K_5</span> or <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span>, that is, a copy of one of them in which edges may be replaced by paths. In other words, <span class=\"m\">K_5</span> and <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span> are the only two fundamental obstacles; every non-planar graph is hiding one of them. Linear-time algorithms (Hopcroft–Tarjan, 1974) can test planarity and produce a drawing.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;cube&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;planar&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The cube drawn as nested squares: no crossings, and V − E + F = 8 − 12 + 6 = 2.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Passing <span class=\"m\">E \\le 3V - 6</span> does not prove planarity; it only fails to disprove it. Use <span class=\"m\">E \\le 2V - 4</span> only for triangle-free graphs. Remember to count the outer face in Euler's formula. And for a plane graph with <span class=\"m\">c</span> components, the formula becomes <span class=\"m\">V - E + F = 1 + c</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A graph is planar if some drawing has no crossings. Euler's formula <span class=\"m\">V - E + F = 2</span> holds for every connected plane graph. Combined with \"each face has at least 3 edges\", it gives <span class=\"m\">E \\le 3V - 6</span> (and <span class=\"m\">E \\le 2V - 4</span> without triangles), which proves <span class=\"m\">K_5</span> and <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span> are non-planar and that planar graphs always have a vertex of degree at most 5. Kuratowski's theorem says those two graphs, in subdivided form, are the only obstructions.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;k4&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;planar&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag vertices to remove crossings, and watch the readout check Euler's formula.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. <span class=\"m\">K_4</span> starts with 1 crossing (marked ×). Drag one vertex into the middle of the triangle formed by the others until the crossing count is 0. The readout now confirms <span class=\"m\">V - E + F = 4 - 6 + 4 = 2</span>.\n2. Switch to the cube and the wheel: already crossing-free. Check <span class=\"m\">F</span> in each.\n3. Switch to <span class=\"m\">K_5</span>: the readout shows <span class=\"m\">E = 10 &gt; 3V - 6 = 9</span>, so no amount of dragging can remove every crossing. Try anyway.\n4. Switch to <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span>: the first test passes but the triangle-free test <span class=\"m\">E \\le 2V - 4 = 8</span> fails.\n5. Try the Petersen graph: both tests pass, yet you will not manage to remove all crossings.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> whenever a drawing has no crossings, <span class=\"m\">V - E + F</span> is exactly 2 (or <span class=\"m\">1 +</span> the number of components). The edge tests can prove non-planarity, but passing them, as Petersen shows, proves nothing.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">V - E + F = 2 \\quad \\text{(connected plane graph)}, \\qquad V - E + F = 1 + c \\quad (c \\text{ components})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_{\\text{faces}} \\deg(f) = 2E</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E \\le 3V - 6 \\;\\; (V \\ge 3), \\qquad E \\le 2V - 4 \\;\\; \\text{(no triangles)}, \\qquad E \\le \\tfrac{g}{g-2}(V - 2) \\;\\; \\text{(shortest cycle } g)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{planar} \\Rightarrow \\exists v: \\deg v \\le 5</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Euler's formula</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For every connected plane graph, <span class=\"m\">V - E + F = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Induction on the number of edges outside a spanning tree <span class=\"m\">T</span>. For <span class=\"m\">T</span> itself, <span class=\"m\">E = V - 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">F = 1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">V - E + F = 2</span>. Each remaining edge <span class=\"m\">e</span> joins two vertices already connected, so in the drawing it lies on a cycle and separates one existing face into two (by the Jordan curve theorem). Adding <span class=\"m\">e</span> increases <span class=\"m\">E</span> by 1 and <span class=\"m\">F</span> by 1, keeping <span class=\"m\">V - E + F = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Kuratowski's theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A graph is planar if and only if it contains no subgraph that is a subdivision of <span class=\"m\">K_5</span> or of <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A connected plane graph has 12 vertices, each of degree 3. How many faces does it have?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Handshake: <span class=\"m\">2E = 12 \\times 3 = 36</span>, so <span class=\"m\">E = 18</span>.</li>\n<li>Euler: <span class=\"m\">F = 2 - V + E = 2 - 12 + 18 = 8</span>.</li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"m\">E = 18 \\le 3V - 6 = 30</span>. (The truncated tetrahedron is such a graph.)</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show that a simple graph with 8 vertices and 19 edges is not planar</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>If planar, <span class=\"m\">E \\le 3V - 6 = 3(8) - 6 = 18</span>.</li>\n<li>But <span class=\"m\">E = 19 &gt; 18</span>.</li>\n<li>So no crossing-free drawing exists.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(a) A connected planar graph has 10 vertices and 15 edges. How many faces does any plane drawing have? (b) What is the largest number of edges a simple planar graph on 10 vertices can have? (c) A triangle-free planar graph has 10 vertices. At most how many edges can it have?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">F = 2 - 10 + 15 = 7</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">3(10) - 6 = 24</span>. (c) <span class=\"m\">2(10) - 4 = 16</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Circuit board and chip layout (VLSI) software minimises crossings, since each crossing needs an extra layer or via. Graph drawing libraries (such as Graphviz) and map rendering use planarity tests and planar embeddings. Many hard problems become easier on planar graphs: for example, planar graphs always have small \"separators\" (about <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{n}</span> vertices), which gives fast divide-and-conquer algorithms.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Meshes in 3D graphics and geometry processing are planar-like graphs (triangulated surfaces satisfy a version of Euler's formula, <span class=\"m\">V - E + F = 2</span> for a sphere-like surface). Mesh-based graph neural networks for physics simulation use these vertex–edge–face relationships.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>There are exactly five Platonic solids (tetrahedron, cube, octahedron, dodecahedron, icosahedron). Their skeletons are planar graphs, and Euler's formula, together with the requirement that every face and every corner look the same, leaves only five possibilities.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">A connected plane graph has 7 vertices and 10 edges. How many faces?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">F = 2 - 7 + 10 = 5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which test shows <span class=\"m\">K_{3,3}</span> is not planar?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">E \\le 3V - 6</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">E \\le 2V - 4</span>, since it has no triangles</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Euler's formula alone, without bounds</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">9 &gt; 2(6) - 4 = 8</span>, while <span class=\"m\">9 \\le 12</span> passes the first test.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the maximum number of edges in a simple planar graph with 6 vertices?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"12\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3(6) - 6 = 12</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Every simple planar graph has a vertex of degree at most:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">3</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">4</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">5</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>If all degrees were at least 6, then <span class=\"m\">E \\ge 3V &gt; 3V - 6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">A graph passes the test <span class=\"m\">E \\le 3V - 6</span>. What can you conclude?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it is planar</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">nothing: it may or may not be planar</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it is not planar</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The bound is necessary, not sufficient (Petersen passes and is non-planar).</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "colouring": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"scheduling-exams-without-clashes\" data-h=\"3\">Scheduling exams without clashes</h3>\n<p>A university must schedule exams. Some pairs of courses share a student, so their exams cannot happen at the same time. What is the smallest number of time slots needed? Draw a graph with one vertex per course and an edge between any two courses that share a student. Assigning time slots is then the same as giving each vertex a \"colour\" so that no edge joins two vertices of the same colour. The fewest slots is the fewest colours. This is <strong>graph colouring</strong>, and the same model fits radio frequencies (nearby transmitters need different frequencies), compiler register allocation (variables alive at the same time need different registers), and Sudoku (cells in the same row, column or box need different digits).</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proper colouring</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A proper <span class=\"m\">k</span>-colouring assigns each vertex one of <span class=\"m\">k</span> colours so that the two ends of every edge have different colours.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Chromatic number <span class=\"m\">\\chi(G)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The smallest <span class=\"m\">k</span> for which <span class=\"m\">G</span> has a proper <span class=\"m\">k</span>-colouring.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"small-examples\" data-h=\"3\">Small examples</h3>\n<p>A graph with no edges needs 1 colour. Any graph with at least one edge needs at least 2. The bipartite graphs are exactly those with <span class=\"m\">\\chi \\le 2</span>, so trees, even cycles, grids and the cube all have <span class=\"m\">\\chi = 2</span>. An odd cycle needs 3: going around, the colours would have to alternate, and an odd number of alternations cannot close up. So <span class=\"m\">\\chi(C_5) = 3</span>. The complete graph <span class=\"m\">K_n</span> needs <span class=\"m\">n</span> colours, since every pair of vertices is adjacent. The wheel with an odd rim (a 5-cycle plus a hub joined to everything) needs 4: the rim needs 3 and the hub must differ from all of them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lower-bounds-cliques\" data-h=\"3\">Lower bounds: cliques</h3>\n<p>A <strong>clique</strong> is a set of vertices that are all adjacent to each other. If a graph contains a clique of size <span class=\"m\">\\omega</span>, those vertices need <span class=\"m\">\\omega</span> different colours, so <span class=\"m\">\\chi(G) \\ge \\omega(G)</span>. In the exam example, if four courses all share students pairwise, at least four slots are needed. Another lower bound: each colour class is an <strong>independent set</strong> (no edges inside it), so if the largest independent set has <span class=\"m\">\\alpha</span> vertices, you need at least <span class=\"m\">n/\\alpha</span> colours. Neither bound is always tight: the 5-cycle has <span class=\"m\">\\omega = 2</span> but <span class=\"m\">\\chi = 3</span>, and there are triangle-free graphs with chromatic number as large as you like.</p>\n<h3 id=\"upper-bounds-the-greedy-algorithm\" data-h=\"3\">Upper bounds: the greedy algorithm</h3>\n<p>The simplest way to colour is <strong>greedy</strong>: put the vertices in some order, and give each vertex the smallest colour (1, 2, 3, ...) not already used by its coloured neighbours. When we reach a vertex <span class=\"m\">v</span>, at most <span class=\"m\">\\deg v</span> of its neighbours have colours, so at most <span class=\"m\">\\deg v</span> colours are blocked, and one of the colours <span class=\"m\">1, 2, \\ldots, \\deg v + 1</span> is free. So greedy uses at most <span class=\"m\">\\Delta + 1</span> colours, where <span class=\"m\">\\Delta</span> is the maximum degree:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\chi(G) \\le \\Delta(G) + 1.</span></p>\n<p>But the <em>order</em> matters a great deal. Take the path <span class=\"m\">a - b - c - d</span>. In order <span class=\"m\">a, b, c, d</span>, greedy alternates 1, 2, 1, 2: two colours, which is optimal. In the order <span class=\"m\">a, d, b, c</span>: <span class=\"m\">a</span> gets 1; <span class=\"m\">d</span> has no coloured neighbour, so it also gets 1; <span class=\"m\">b</span> is next to <span class=\"m\">a</span> and gets 2; <span class=\"m\">c</span> is next to <span class=\"m\">b</span> (colour 2) and <span class=\"m\">d</span> (colour 1), so it needs colour 3. Three colours on a graph that only needs two. A popular heuristic, the <strong>Welsh–Powell</strong> rule, colours vertices in order of decreasing degree, which handles the most constrained vertices first. There is always <em>some</em> order for which greedy is optimal (colour the vertices class by class from an optimal colouring), but finding it is as hard as the original problem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"brooks-theorem-and-the-four-colour-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">Brooks' theorem and the four colour theorem</h3>\n<p>The bound <span class=\"m\">\\Delta + 1</span> is reached by complete graphs (<span class=\"m\">\\chi(K_n) = n = \\Delta + 1</span>) and odd cycles (<span class=\"m\">\\chi = 3 = \\Delta + 1</span>). <strong>Brooks' theorem</strong> (1941) says these are the only connected graphs that need <span class=\"m\">\\Delta + 1</span> colours; every other connected graph can be coloured with <span class=\"m\">\\Delta</span> colours.</p>\n<p>For planar graphs, the fact from the last lesson, that some vertex has degree at most 5, gives an easy proof that 6 colours always suffice: remove that vertex, colour the rest by induction, then put it back; it has at most 5 neighbours, so one of 6 colours is free. With more care the same idea gives 5 colours. The famous <strong>four colour theorem</strong> says that 4 colours are always enough for planar graphs, so every map can be coloured with four colours so that neighbouring countries differ. It was conjectured in 1852 and proved only in 1976 by Appel and Haken, using a computer to check nearly 2000 configurations, the first major theorem proved with essential computer help.</p>\n<h3 id=\"hardness\" data-h=\"3\">Hardness</h3>\n<p>Computing <span class=\"m\">\\chi(G)</span> exactly is NP-hard, and even deciding whether a graph is 3-colourable is NP-complete. Deciding 2-colourability, on the other hand, is just the BFS bipartite test. In practice, large colouring problems are solved with heuristics like greedy with good orderings, or with exact solvers on small instances.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;wheel&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;optimal&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The wheel with a 5-cycle rim needs 4 colours: 3 for the odd rim and 1 more for the hub.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Showing a colouring with <span class=\"m\">k</span> colours proves only <span class=\"m\">\\chi \\le k</span>. To prove <span class=\"m\">\\chi = k</span> you also need a lower-bound argument, like a clique of size <span class=\"m\">k</span> or an odd cycle when <span class=\"m\">k = 3</span>. Do not assume \"no triangle\" means \"3 colours suffice for everything\" or \"2 colours suffice\": the 5-cycle has no triangle and needs 3. And remember that greedy's answer depends on the order, so a greedy result is an upper bound, not the chromatic number.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A proper colouring gives adjacent vertices different colours, and <span class=\"m\">\\chi(G)</span> is the fewest colours possible. Cliques and independent sets give lower bounds; greedy colouring gives the upper bound <span class=\"m\">\\Delta + 1</span>, which Brooks' theorem improves to <span class=\"m\">\\Delta</span> except for complete graphs and odd cycles. Planar graphs need at most 4 colours. Computing <span class=\"m\">\\chi</span> is hard in general, but colouring models scheduling, frequency assignment and register allocation everywhere.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;petersen&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;greedy&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Compare greedy colourings with the optimal number of colours.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. On the Petersen graph, greedy in number order uses 3 colours. Switch the mode to \"optimal\": <span class=\"m\">\\chi = 3</span>, since the outer 5-cycle is odd.\n2. Switch to the wheel: 4 colours, the odd rim plus the hub. Its <span class=\"m\">\\Delta + 1 = 6</span>, so the greedy bound is far from tight here.\n3. Try <span class=\"m\">K_5</span> (5 colours, <span class=\"m\">\\Delta + 1 = 5</span>, one of Brooks' exceptions) and <span class=\"m\">C_5</span> (3 colours, the other exception).\n4. Switch to the 6-cycle, the cube and the grid: every method finds 2 colours, matching <span class=\"m\">\\chi = 2</span> for bipartite graphs, even though <span class=\"m\">\\Delta + 1</span> is 3, 4 and 5.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the readout always reports <span class=\"m\">\\Delta + 1</span> as an upper bound, and the actual number is often far below it. Whenever optimal is 3, look for the odd cycle; whenever it is 4 or more, look for a clique or a wheel-like structure forcing it.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\omega(G) \\le \\chi(G) \\le \\Delta(G) + 1, \\qquad \\chi(G) \\ge \\frac{n}{\\alpha(G)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\chi(K_n) = n, \\quad \\chi(C_{2k}) = 2, \\quad \\chi(C_{2k+1}) = 3, \\quad \\chi(\\text{tree}) = 2 \\;(n \\ge 2)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Brooks: } \\chi(G) \\le \\Delta(G) \\text{ unless } G \\text{ is complete or an odd cycle}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{planar} \\Rightarrow \\chi(G) \\le 4</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{number of proper } k\\text{-colourings: } P(K_n, k) = k(k-1)\\cdots(k-n+1), \\quad P(\\text{tree on } n, k) = k(k-1)^{n-1}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Greedy colouring bound</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every graph with maximum degree <span class=\"m\">\\Delta</span> has a proper colouring with at most <span class=\"m\">\\Delta + 1</span> colours, and the greedy algorithm finds one in any vertex order.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Process vertices in any order. When vertex <span class=\"m\">v</span> is coloured, at most <span class=\"m\">\\deg v \\le \\Delta</span> of its neighbours already have colours, so at most <span class=\"m\">\\Delta</span> of the colours <span class=\"m\">1, \\ldots, \\Delta + 1</span> are blocked. At least one is free; give <span class=\"m\">v</span> the smallest free colour. No edge ever gets two equal colours, because the later endpoint avoided the earlier one's colour.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Schedule exams for courses A–E with conflicts <span class=\"m\">AB, AC, BC, BD, CE, DE</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Lower bound: A, B, C form a triangle, so at least 3 slots.</li>\n<li>Greedy in order A, B, C, D, E: A 1, B 2, C 3 (adjacent to A and B), D 1 (adjacent only to B among coloured), E 2 (adjacent to C = 3 and D = 1).</li>\n<li>Check all six edges: 1–2, 1–3, 2–3, 2–1, 3–2, 1–2. All different.</li>\n<li>So <span class=\"m\">\\chi = 3</span>: slots {A, D}, {B, E}, {C}.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How many proper 3-colourings does a path on 4 vertices have?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>The first vertex can take any of 3 colours.</li>\n<li>Each next vertex must differ only from its predecessor: 2 choices each.</li>\n<li>Total <span class=\"m\">3 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 24</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">k(k-1)^{n-1}</span> with <span class=\"m\">k = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(a) What is <span class=\"m\">\\chi</span> of the cycle <span class=\"m\">C_7</span>, and of <span class=\"m\">K_{4,6}</span>? (b) A graph has maximum degree 4 and is neither complete nor an odd cycle. What upper bound on <span class=\"m\">\\chi</span> does Brooks' theorem give? (c) A graph on 12 vertices has largest independent set of size 3. Give a lower bound on <span class=\"m\">\\chi</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">C_7</span> is an odd cycle: <span class=\"m\">\\chi = 3</span>. <span class=\"m\">K_{4,6}</span> is bipartite with edges: <span class=\"m\">\\chi = 2</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">\\chi \\le \\Delta = 4</span>. (c) Each colour class has at most 3 vertices, so <span class=\"m\">\\chi \\ge 12/3 = 4</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Compilers perform register allocation by colouring an interference graph: variables are vertices, an edge means two variables are alive at the same moment, and colours are CPU registers. If the graph needs more colours than there are registers, some variables are \"spilled\" to memory. Chaitin's classic allocator is greedy colouring with a smart ordering.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Distributed training and parallel Gibbs sampling use colourings to decide which variables can be updated at the same time: vertices of the same colour have no edges between them, so they can be processed in parallel without conflicts. Sudoku and other constraint problems are solved by graph colouring search, a classic benchmark for constraint solvers.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Mobile phone networks assign frequencies to towers so that nearby towers never share one; this is colouring the graph where edges join towers whose signals overlap. Every printed political map can be coloured with just four colours, by the four colour theorem.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the chromatic number of <span class=\"m\">C_9</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Odd cycles need 3 colours.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A graph contains a clique on 5 vertices. Which must be true?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\chi \\ge 5</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\chi \\le 5</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\chi = 5</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The clique's vertices need distinct colours; other parts might need even more.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Greedy colouring on a graph with maximum degree 7 uses at most how many colours?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"8\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>At most <span class=\"m\">\\Delta + 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many proper 2-colourings does a tree on 6 vertices have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">k(k-1)^{n-1} = 2 \\times 1^5 = 2</span>: choose the root's colour, the rest is forced.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which graphs have <span class=\"m\">\\chi \\le 2</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">exactly the bipartite graphs</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">exactly the trees</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">exactly the planar graphs</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A 2-colouring is a bipartition.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the maximum number of colours ever needed for a planar graph?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The four colour theorem.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "directed-graphs": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"when-relationships-have-a-direction\" data-h=\"3\">When relationships have a direction</h3>\n<p>Many relationships only go one way. \"Course A is a prerequisite of course B\" does not mean B is a prerequisite of A. A web page can link to another without being linked back. Twitter-style \"follows\" are one-directional. Package managers know that library X depends on library Y. To model these, we give each edge a direction and draw it as an arrow.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Directed graph (digraph)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A directed graph has vertices and directed edges (arcs); each arc <span class=\"m\">(u, v)</span> goes from its tail <span class=\"m\">u</span> to its head <span class=\"m\">v</span>. The out-degree of <span class=\"m\">v</span> is the number of arcs leaving <span class=\"m\">v</span>, and the in-degree is the number arriving.</p></div></div>\n<p>Each arc adds one to exactly one out-degree and one in-degree, so the directed handshake lemma is <span class=\"m\">\\sum_v \\deg^+(v) = \\sum_v \\deg^-(v) = m</span>. Walks, paths and cycles are defined as before, except they must follow the arrows. So in a directed graph, <span class=\"m\">u</span> can reach <span class=\"m\">v</span> without <span class=\"m\">v</span> being able to reach <span class=\"m\">u</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"strong-connectivity\" data-h=\"3\">Strong connectivity</h3>\n<p>In a directed graph, \"connected\" splits into two ideas. A digraph is <strong>strongly connected</strong> if every vertex can reach every other vertex following arrows. It is <strong>weakly connected</strong> if it would be connected after ignoring directions. A one-way street system is usable only if it is strongly connected; otherwise some addresses can be entered but never left. Every digraph splits into <strong>strongly connected components</strong> (SCCs): maximal groups of vertices that can all reach each other. If you shrink each SCC to a single vertex, the result has no directed cycles, because any cycle between components would merge them into one. Tarjan's and Kosaraju's algorithms find all SCCs in <span class=\"m\">O(n + m)</span> time using DFS.</p>\n<h3 id=\"directed-acyclic-graphs\" data-h=\"3\">Directed acyclic graphs</h3>\n<p>A <strong>directed acyclic graph</strong> (DAG) is a digraph with no directed cycle. DAGs are the natural shape of dependencies: prerequisites, build steps, spreadsheets where each cell depends on others, the computation graph of a neural network. A cycle in a dependency graph would be a disaster: A needs B first, B needs C first, and C needs A first, so nothing can ever start.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Topological order</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A topological order of a digraph is a listing of all its vertices such that for every arc <span class=\"m\">(u, v)</span>, <span class=\"m\">u</span> comes before <span class=\"m\">v</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"imagine-getting-dressed\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine getting dressed</h3>\n<p>Imagine getting dressed in the morning. Undershorts must go on before trousers, trousers before shoes and before the belt, socks before shoes, the shirt before the tie and belt, and the tie and belt before the jacket. The watch can go on any time. Draw an arrow from each item to everything that must come after it, and you have a DAG. Any order in which you can actually get dressed is a topological order: for example undershorts, socks, shirt, watch, trousers, tie, shoes, belt, jacket. There can be many valid orders, and putting your shoes on before your socks is not one of them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"kahn-s-algorithm\" data-h=\"3\">Kahn's algorithm</h3>\n<p>How do we find a topological order? Some item must go first, and it must have no prerequisites, that is, <strong>in-degree 0</strong>. Kahn's algorithm repeatedly takes such a vertex:\n1. Compute every in-degree. Put all in-degree-0 vertices in a queue.\n2. Remove a vertex <span class=\"m\">u</span> from the queue and append it to the output.\n3. For each arc <span class=\"m\">(u, v)</span>, decrease <span class=\"m\">v</span>'s in-degree by 1, as if crossing off a satisfied prerequisite. If it becomes 0, add <span class=\"m\">v</span> to the queue.\n4. Repeat until the queue is empty.</p>\n<p>Small example: arcs <span class=\"m\">A \\to B</span>, <span class=\"m\">A \\to C</span>, <span class=\"m\">B \\to D</span>, <span class=\"m\">C \\to D</span>, <span class=\"m\">D \\to E</span>. In-degrees: A 0, B 1, C 1, D 2, E 1. Output A; B and C drop to 0. Output B; D drops to 1. Output C; D drops to 0. Output D; E drops to 0. Output E. Order: A, B, C, D, E. (A, C, B, D, E is the only other valid order.)</p>\n<p>If the queue empties before every vertex is output, the remaining vertices all still have positive in-degree, and that only happens when they contain a directed cycle. So Kahn's algorithm is also a <strong>cycle detector</strong>. This proves the key theorem: <strong>a digraph has a topological order if and only if it is a DAG</strong>. Each vertex and arc is handled once, so it runs in <span class=\"m\">O(n + m)</span>.</p>\n<p>The other classic method uses DFS: when DFS finishes a vertex (all its descendants are done), push it on a list. Reversing the finishing order gives a topological order, because when an arc <span class=\"m\">u \\to v</span> is explored, <span class=\"m\">v</span> always finishes before <span class=\"m\">u</span> in a DAG.</p>\n<h3 id=\"longest-paths-and-scheduling\" data-h=\"3\">Longest paths and scheduling</h3>\n<p>In a general graph, finding the longest path is hard, but in a DAG it is easy: process vertices in topological order and set <span class=\"m\">\\text{finish}(v) = \\text{duration}(v) + \\max</span> over prerequisites <span class=\"m\">u</span> of <span class=\"m\">\\text{finish}(u)</span>. The largest finish time is the length of the <strong>critical path</strong>, the minimum time to complete a project if unlimited workers can do independent tasks in parallel. Tasks on the critical path cannot be delayed without delaying the whole project.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g10-dag\" data-args='{&quot;dag&quot;: &quot;dressing&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Getting dressed as a DAG: each arrow points from an item to something that must come later.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>A topological order is usually not unique; do not mark an answer wrong just because it differs from yours. Check it arc by arc. Topological sorting is only defined for DAGs. Also be careful with arrow direction conventions: \"A depends on B\" is often drawn as <span class=\"m\">B \\to A</span> (B must come first), and mixing conventions reverses your order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Directed graphs have in- and out-degrees, and reachability is one-way. Strongly connected components group mutually reachable vertices, and shrinking them leaves a DAG. A digraph has a topological order exactly when it has no directed cycle. Kahn's algorithm (repeatedly remove an in-degree-0 vertex) and reversed DFS finishing times both find one in <span class=\"m\">O(n + m)</span>, and in a DAG longest paths give critical-path schedules.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-dag\" data-args='{&quot;dag&quot;: &quot;dressing&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Step through Kahn's algorithm, then add a cycle and watch it get stuck.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Press Step repeatedly. Yellow vertices are ready (in-degree 0); blue ones are already in the output with their position number.\n2. Watch in-degrees drop as prerequisites are output. Shoes become ready only after undershorts, socks and trousers are output.\n3. Read the final order and the longest chain (4 items: undershorts, trousers, belt, jacket), the critical path.\n4. Choose \"Add an edge that makes a cycle\" (jacket before shirt) and play again. Kahn's algorithm gets stuck after 5 of 9 items.\n5. Switch to the \"courses\" DAG and repeat. Its longest chain has 5 courses.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> a vertex becomes ready exactly when all its arrows-in have been crossed off, just like the article's in-degree argument. When there is a cycle, every vertex left has an incoming arrow from another vertex left, so none can ever start.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\sum_v \\deg^+(v) = \\sum_v \\deg^-(v) = m</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{topological order exists} \\iff \\text{no directed cycle}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{finish}(v) = d(v) + \\max_{(u,v)\\in E} \\text{finish}(u) \\quad (\\max \\text{ over nothing} = 0)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Kahn and DFS topological sort: } O(n + m)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">DAGs and topological orders</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A directed graph has a topological order if and only if it has no directed cycle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If there is a cycle <span class=\"m\">v_1 \\to v_2 \\to \\cdots \\to v_k \\to v_1</span>, a topological order would need <span class=\"m\">v_1</span> before <span class=\"m\">v_2</span> before ... before <span class=\"m\">v_k</span> before <span class=\"m\">v_1</span>, impossible. Conversely, a DAG always has a vertex of in-degree 0: otherwise, walking backwards along incoming arcs forever from any vertex must eventually repeat a vertex, giving a cycle. Put such a vertex first, delete it (the rest is still a DAG) and repeat; by induction this lists all vertices with every arc pointing forward.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Topologically sort: <span class=\"m\">5 \\to 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">5 \\to 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">4 \\to 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">4 \\to 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">2 \\to 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">3 \\to 1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>In-degrees: 0: 2, 1: 2, 2: 1, 3: 1, 4: 0, 5: 0. Queue [4, 5].</li>\n<li>Output 4: in-degree of 0 becomes 1, of 1 becomes 1. Output 5: 0 becomes 0, 2 becomes 0. Queue [0, 2].</li>\n<li>Output 0. Output 2: 3 becomes 0. Output 3: 1 becomes 0. Output 1.</li>\n<li>Order: 4, 5, 0, 2, 3, 1. Check each arc points forward: yes.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Critical path: tasks A (3 days), B (2), C (4), D (1), with <span class=\"m\">A \\to B</span>, <span class=\"m\">A \\to C</span>, <span class=\"m\">B \\to D</span>, <span class=\"m\">C \\to D</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Topological order A, B, C, D.</li>\n<li>finish(A) <span class=\"m\">= 3</span>; finish(B) <span class=\"m\">= 2 + 3 = 5</span>; finish(C) <span class=\"m\">= 4 + 3 = 7</span>.</li>\n<li>finish(D) <span class=\"m\">= 1 + \\max(5, 7) = 8</span>.</li>\n<li>The project takes 8 days; the critical path is A, C, D. B has 2 days of slack.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A digraph has arcs <span class=\"m\">a \\to b</span>, <span class=\"m\">b \\to c</span>, <span class=\"m\">c \\to a</span>, <span class=\"m\">c \\to d</span>, <span class=\"m\">d \\to e</span>, <span class=\"m\">e \\to d</span>. (a) List its strongly connected components. (b) Does it have a topological order? (c) Shrink each SCC to one vertex; what graph do you get, and what is its topological order?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">\\{a, b, c\\}</span> (they form a cycle) and <span class=\"m\">\\{d, e\\}</span> (they reach each other). (b) No, it has directed cycles. (c) Two vertices with one arc <span class=\"m\">\\{a,b,c\\} \\to \\{d,e\\}</span>; topological order: <span class=\"m\">\\{a,b,c\\}</span> then <span class=\"m\">\\{d,e\\}</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every neural network is a computation DAG. The forward pass evaluates nodes in topological order; backpropagation visits them in reverse topological order, applying the chain rule at each node. Frameworks like PyTorch and TensorFlow build this DAG and sort it automatically. Bayesian networks are DAGs too, where each arrow means \"directly influences\".</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Build systems (make, Bazel), package managers (apt, npm, pip) and spreadsheet recalculation all topologically sort dependency DAGs, and report an error when they find a cycle (\"circular dependency\"). Course schedulers, task runners and data pipelines (Airflow DAGs) do the same.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A recipe is a DAG: you must boil the water before adding pasta, and chop vegetables before frying them, but chopping and boiling can happen in parallel. The critical path tells you the earliest time dinner can be ready.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A digraph has 12 arcs. What is the sum of all in-degrees?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"12\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each arc adds 1 to exactly one in-degree.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which vertex can come first in a topological order?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a vertex with in-degree 0</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a vertex with out-degree 0</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">any vertex</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The first vertex cannot have any prerequisite.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Kahn's algorithm stops with 3 vertices never output. What does that mean?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the graph contains a directed cycle</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the graph is disconnected</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">there are 3 isolated vertices</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Remaining vertices all have positive in-degree from each other, which forces a cycle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many topological orders does the DAG with arcs <span class=\"m\">A \\to B</span>, <span class=\"m\">A \\to C</span> (and nothing else) have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A first, then B and C in either order.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Tasks X (2 h), Y (5 h), Z (1 h) with <span class=\"m\">X \\to Z</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y \\to Z</span>. What is the minimum completion time with parallel work?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>finish(Z) <span class=\"m\">= 1 + \\max(2, 5) = 6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In backpropagation, nodes of the computation graph are processed in:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">topological order</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">reverse topological order</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">BFS order from the input</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Gradients flow from the loss back to the inputs.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "network-flows": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"how-much-can-the-pipes-carry\" data-h=\"3\">How much can the pipes carry?</h3>\n<p>A city's water comes from a reservoir <span class=\"m\">s</span> and is used at a treatment plant <span class=\"m\">t</span>, connected by a network of pipes. Each pipe can carry at most a certain amount of water per second, its <strong>capacity</strong>, and water flows only one way in each pipe. What is the most water per second the network can deliver from <span class=\"m\">s</span> to <span class=\"m\">t</span>? The same question asks how many cars per hour a road network can carry from one side of a city to another, how much data per second can travel between two servers, or how many goods can be shipped from a factory to a port.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Flow network</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A flow network is a directed graph with a source <span class=\"m\">s</span>, a sink <span class=\"m\">t</span>, and a capacity <span class=\"m\">c(u,v) \\ge 0</span> on each arc.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Flow</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A flow assigns <span class=\"m\">f(u,v)</span> to each arc with <span class=\"m\">0 \\le f(u,v) \\le c(u,v)</span> (capacity constraint) and, at every vertex other than <span class=\"m\">s</span> and <span class=\"m\">t</span>, flow in equals flow out (conservation). Its value <span class=\"m\">|f|</span> is the net flow out of <span class=\"m\">s</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"a-first-attempt-push-along-paths\" data-h=\"3\">A first attempt: push along paths</h3>\n<p>The natural idea is: find a path from <span class=\"m\">s</span> to <span class=\"m\">t</span> where every arc still has spare capacity, push as much as the tightest arc allows (the <strong>bottleneck</strong>), and repeat. Take the network with arcs <span class=\"m\">s \\to a</span> (3), <span class=\"m\">s \\to b</span> (2), <span class=\"m\">a \\to b</span> (1), <span class=\"m\">a \\to t</span> (2), <span class=\"m\">b \\to t</span> (3). Push 2 along <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to t</span>. Push 2 along <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to t</span>. Now <span class=\"m\">s \\to a</span> has 1 spare, <span class=\"m\">a \\to b</span> has 1 and <span class=\"m\">b \\to t</span> has 1: push 1 along <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to b \\to t</span>. Total 5, and both arcs out of <span class=\"m\">s</span> are full, so 5 is clearly the maximum.</p>\n<p>But this naive method can get stuck. In the \"trap\" network where every arc has capacity 1 (<span class=\"m\">s \\to a</span>, <span class=\"m\">s \\to b</span>, <span class=\"m\">a \\to b</span>, <span class=\"m\">a \\to t</span>, <span class=\"m\">b \\to t</span>), suppose the first path chosen is <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to b \\to t</span>. Now <span class=\"m\">s \\to a</span>, <span class=\"m\">a \\to b</span> and <span class=\"m\">b \\to t</span> are full. The only spare arcs are <span class=\"m\">s \\to b</span> and <span class=\"m\">a \\to t</span>, and no forward path joins them. We are stuck at 1, but the true maximum is 2 (use <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to t</span> and <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to t</span>). The first choice was a mistake, and we need a way to undo it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"residual-graphs-allowing-undo\" data-h=\"3\">Residual graphs: allowing undo</h3>\n<p>The fix is to allow sending flow <em>backwards</em> along an arc that carries flow, which means cancelling some of it. The <strong>residual graph</strong> has, for each arc <span class=\"m\">(u,v)</span>, a forward residual arc with capacity <span class=\"m\">c(u,v) - f(u,v)</span> (room left) and a backward residual arc <span class=\"m\">(v,u)</span> with capacity <span class=\"m\">f(u,v)</span> (flow that could be cancelled). An <strong>augmenting path</strong> is any <span class=\"m\">s</span>–<span class=\"m\">t</span> path in the residual graph.</p>\n<p>In the trap, after the bad first path, the residual graph contains the backward arc <span class=\"m\">b \\to a</span> (capacity 1). Now <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to a \\to t</span> is an augmenting path: send 1 unit from <span class=\"m\">s</span> to <span class=\"m\">b</span>, \"cancel\" the unit that went <span class=\"m\">a \\to b</span>, and send it from <span class=\"m\">a</span> to <span class=\"m\">t</span> instead. The net result is flow on <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to t</span> and <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to t</span>: value 2.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ford-fulkerson-and-edmonds-karp\" data-h=\"3\">Ford–Fulkerson and Edmonds–Karp</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Ford–Fulkerson method</strong> is: start with zero flow; while the residual graph has an augmenting path, push the bottleneck amount along it. With integer capacities each augmentation adds at least 1, so it terminates, and the final flow is integer on every arc (the <strong>integrality theorem</strong>). But a bad choice of paths can take a very long time. <strong>Edmonds–Karp</strong> always picks a <em>shortest</em> augmenting path (fewest arcs) using BFS, and this guarantees at most <span class=\"m\">O(nm)</span> augmentations, <span class=\"m\">O(nm^2)</span> time overall.</p>\n<h3 id=\"cuts-and-why-the-answer-is-optimal\" data-h=\"3\">Cuts, and why the answer is optimal</h3>\n<p>An <strong><span class=\"m\">s</span>–<span class=\"m\">t</span> cut</strong> splits the vertices into a set <span class=\"m\">S</span> containing <span class=\"m\">s</span> and the rest <span class=\"m\">T</span> containing <span class=\"m\">t</span>. Its capacity is the total capacity of arcs going from <span class=\"m\">S</span> to <span class=\"m\">T</span>. Every unit of flow must cross from <span class=\"m\">S</span> to <span class=\"m\">T</span> at some point, so <strong>every flow is at most every cut</strong>: <span class=\"m\">|f| \\le c(S, T)</span>. In the small example, the cut <span class=\"m\">S = \\{s\\}</span> has capacity <span class=\"m\">3 + 2 = 5</span>, so no flow can exceed 5, and our flow of 5 is proved optimal.</p>\n<p>The <strong>max-flow min-cut theorem</strong> says this always works: the maximum flow value equals the minimum cut capacity. When Ford–Fulkerson stops, let <span class=\"m\">S</span> be the vertices reachable from <span class=\"m\">s</span> in the residual graph. Every arc from <span class=\"m\">S</span> to <span class=\"m\">T</span> must be full (otherwise its far end would be reachable), and every arc from <span class=\"m\">T</span> to <span class=\"m\">S</span> must be empty (otherwise its backward residual arc would make its tail reachable). So the flow across the cut equals its capacity exactly, and the flow is maximum.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g10-flow\" data-args='{&quot;net&quot;: &quot;small&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Labels are flow/capacity. The maximum flow 5 equals the capacity of the cut around s.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"matching-as-a-flow\" data-h=\"3\">Matching as a flow</h3>\n<p>Bipartite matching is a special case. Add a source joined to every left vertex and a sink joined from every right vertex, give every arc capacity 1, and direct the middle edges left to right. An integer flow of value <span class=\"m\">k</span> is exactly a matching of size <span class=\"m\">k</span>, and augmenting paths in the flow network are the alternating augmenting paths of the matching lesson. The min cut corresponds to König's minimum vertex cover.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Forgetting backward residual arcs is the most common error; without them the method can stop at a non-maximum flow. When computing a cut's capacity, count only arcs going from <span class=\"m\">S</span> to <span class=\"m\">T</span>, not arcs from <span class=\"m\">T</span> back to <span class=\"m\">S</span>. And the flow value is measured at the source (or equivalently at the sink), not by adding flows over all arcs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A flow respects capacities and conservation. Augmenting along <span class=\"m\">s</span>–<span class=\"m\">t</span> paths in the residual graph, where backward arcs allow undoing earlier choices, is the Ford–Fulkerson method; choosing shortest paths (Edmonds–Karp) gives <span class=\"m\">O(nm^2)</span> time. Every cut bounds every flow, and the max-flow min-cut theorem says the best flow meets the smallest cut. With integer capacities the maximum flow can be chosen integral, which makes matching and many assignment problems special cases.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-flow\" data-args='{&quot;net&quot;: &quot;classic&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Step through augmenting paths and watch the minimum cut appear at the end.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With \"Shortest path first (Edmonds–Karp)\", press Step. The augmenting paths are <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to c \\to t</span> (4), <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to d \\to t</span> (6), <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to d \\to t</span> (4) and <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to d \\to c \\to t</span> (5).\n2. After the last step the readout reports maximum flow 19 and colours the vertices still reachable from <span class=\"m\">s</span> in the residual graph. The red dashed arcs leaving them form the minimum cut: <span class=\"m\">s \\to a</span> (10) and <span class=\"m\">b \\to d</span> (9), capacity 19.\n3. Switch to \"First path found (DFS)\" and run again. Some paths use backward residual arcs (\"cancels earlier flow\"), and more augmentations are needed, but the final value is again 19.\n4. Choose the \"trap\" network with DFS: the first path <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to b \\to t</span> blocks everything, and the second path uses the backward arc <span class=\"m\">b \\to a</span> to fix it.\n5. Choose \"matching\": the flow computes a maximum matching of 4 workers to 4 jobs.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the final flow value always equals the capacity of the red cut, which is the max-flow min-cut theorem in action. Different path choices change the route, never the final value.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">0 \\le f(u,v) \\le c(u,v), \\qquad \\sum_{u} f(u,v) = \\sum_{w} f(v,w) \\;\\; (v \\ne s,t)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">|f| = \\sum_v f(s,v) - \\sum_v f(v,s)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">c_f(u,v) = c(u,v) - f(u,v), \\qquad c_f(v,u) = f(u,v) \\quad \\text{(residual capacities)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">c(S,T) = \\sum_{u \\in S,\\, v \\in T} c(u,v), \\qquad |f| \\le c(S,T)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\max_f |f| = \\min_{(S,T)} c(S,T); \\qquad \\text{Edmonds–Karp: } O(nm^2)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Max-flow min-cut theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In any flow network, the maximum value of an <span class=\"m\">s</span>–<span class=\"m\">t</span> flow equals the minimum capacity of an <span class=\"m\">s</span>–<span class=\"m\">t</span> cut.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any flow and cut, every unit leaving <span class=\"m\">s</span> must cross from <span class=\"m\">S</span> to <span class=\"m\">T</span>, and flow coming back from <span class=\"m\">T</span> to <span class=\"m\">S</span> only reduces the net, so <span class=\"m\">|f| \\le c(S,T)</span>. Now take a flow <span class=\"m\">f</span> with no augmenting path, and let <span class=\"m\">S</span> be the vertices reachable from <span class=\"m\">s</span> in the residual graph; <span class=\"m\">t \\notin S</span>. Every arc <span class=\"m\">u \\to v</span> with <span class=\"m\">u \\in S</span>, <span class=\"m\">v \\in T</span> has <span class=\"m\">f(u,v) = c(u,v)</span> (else <span class=\"m\">v</span> would be reachable), and every arc <span class=\"m\">v \\to u</span> from <span class=\"m\">T</span> to <span class=\"m\">S</span> has <span class=\"m\">f = 0</span> (else the backward arc <span class=\"m\">u \\to v</span> would exist). Hence <span class=\"m\">|f| = c(S,T)</span>, so this flow is maximum and this cut is minimum.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Max flow: <span class=\"m\">s \\to a</span> (4), <span class=\"m\">s \\to b</span> (3), <span class=\"m\">a \\to b</span> (2), <span class=\"m\">a \\to t</span> (2), <span class=\"m\">b \\to t</span> (5)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Augment <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to t</span> by 2 (<span class=\"m\">a \\to t</span> is now full).</li>\n<li>Augment <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to t</span> by 3 (<span class=\"m\">s \\to b</span> full; <span class=\"m\">b \\to t</span> has 2 left).</li>\n<li>Augment <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to b \\to t</span> by <span class=\"m\">\\min(2, 2, 2) = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>Total <span class=\"m\">2 + 3 + 2 = 7</span>. Cut <span class=\"m\">S = \\{s\\}</span>: <span class=\"m\">4 + 3 = 7</span>. Equal, so 7 is maximum.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute the capacity of the cut <span class=\"m\">S = \\{s, a\\}</span> in the same network</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Arcs from <span class=\"m\">S</span> to <span class=\"m\">T = \\{b, t\\}</span>: <span class=\"m\">s \\to b</span> (3), <span class=\"m\">a \\to b</span> (2), <span class=\"m\">a \\to t</span> (2).</li>\n<li>Capacity <span class=\"m\">3 + 2 + 2 = 7</span>.</li>\n<li>This is also a minimum cut: minimum cuts need not be unique.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A network has arcs <span class=\"m\">s \\to a</span> (5), <span class=\"m\">s \\to b</span> (4), <span class=\"m\">a \\to b</span> (3), <span class=\"m\">a \\to t</span> (3), <span class=\"m\">b \\to t</span> (6). (a) Find the maximum flow. (b) Give a minimum cut and its capacity. (c) If the capacity of <span class=\"m\">a \\to t</span> is increased to 10, what is the new maximum flow?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Push <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to t</span>: 3. Push <span class=\"m\">s \\to b \\to t</span>: 4. Push <span class=\"m\">s \\to a \\to b \\to t</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\min(2, 3, 2) = 2</span>. Total 9, with <span class=\"m\">s \\to a</span> (5) and <span class=\"m\">s \\to b</span> (4) full. (b) <span class=\"m\">S = \\{s\\}</span>, capacity <span class=\"m\">5 + 4 = 9</span>. (c) Still 9: the cut around <span class=\"m\">s</span> has capacity 9 regardless of <span class=\"m\">a \\to t</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Max flow solves bipartite matching, project selection, image segmentation (graph cuts separate foreground from background by a minimum cut), baseball elimination (can a team still finish first?) and network reliability (the minimum number of links whose failure disconnects two servers is a min cut with unit capacities).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Graph-cut methods were the standard for image segmentation and stereo vision before deep learning: pixel labels that minimise an energy are found exactly by a minimum cut. Optimal transport, used in generative models and domain adaptation, is a min-cost flow problem, and the Wasserstein distance between histograms is computed by min-cost flow algorithms.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Airlines and logistics companies use flow models to route aircraft, crews and freight. During the Cold War, the US Air Force studied the Soviet rail network as a flow problem; the min cut identified the smallest set of rail links whose removal would stop the most traffic, one of the original motivations for the theorem.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">An arc has capacity 7 and carries flow 4. What are its forward and backward residual capacities?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">3 forward, 4 backward</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">4 forward, 3 backward</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">7 forward, 0 backward</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Forward room is <span class=\"m\">7 - 4 = 3</span>; up to 4 units can be cancelled.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Every flow value is at most:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">every path's bottleneck</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">every cut's capacity</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the number of vertices</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>All flow from <span class=\"m\">s</span> to <span class=\"m\">t</span> must cross the cut.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">The arcs out of <span class=\"m\">s</span> have capacities 6, 4 and 5. What is the largest possible maximum flow?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"15\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The cut <span class=\"m\">\\{s\\}</span> has capacity 15, so the flow cannot exceed it.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why does Ford–Fulkerson need backward residual arcs?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">to undo earlier flow choices that block better routes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">to send flow from <span class=\"m\">t</span> back to <span class=\"m\">s</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">to make the graph undirected</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Without them the algorithm can get stuck at a non-maximum flow.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 5</div><div class=\"qtext\">With all capacities integers, the max flow:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">can be chosen with integer flow on every arc</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">is always the number of arcs</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">may need fractional flows</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The integrality theorem: each augmentation adds an integer amount.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "graphs-ml": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"data-that-is-made-of-connections\" data-h=\"3\">Data that is made of connections</h3>\n<p>Most ML methods start with a table: one row per example, independent of the others. But much of the world's data is about relationships. Molecules are atoms joined by bonds. Social networks are people joined by friendships. Road maps, citation networks, protein interaction networks, knowledge bases (\"Paris — capital of — France\") and recommendation data are all graphs. Even ordinary data becomes a graph when we connect each point to its nearest neighbours. This lesson shows how the graph theory you have learned turns into linear algebra that machine learning can use.</p>\n<h3 id=\"graphs-as-matrices\" data-h=\"3\">Graphs as matrices</h3>\n<p>Number the vertices <span class=\"m\">1</span> to <span class=\"m\">n</span>. The <strong>adjacency matrix</strong> <span class=\"m\">A</span> has <span class=\"m\">A_{ij} = 1</span> if <span class=\"m\">i</span> and <span class=\"m\">j</span> are adjacent (or the edge weight), and 0 otherwise. The <strong>degree matrix</strong> <span class=\"m\">D</span> is diagonal with <span class=\"m\">D_{ii} = \\deg i</span>. Two facts from earlier lessons now become matrix facts. Row sums of <span class=\"m\">A</span> are degrees. And <span class=\"m\">(A^k)_{ij}</span> counts walks of length <span class=\"m\">k</span> from <span class=\"m\">i</span> to <span class=\"m\">j</span>, so powers of <span class=\"m\">A</span> describe how information spreads <span class=\"m\">k</span> steps through the graph.</p>\n<p>For a path <span class=\"m\">1 - 2 - 3</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">A = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \\\\ 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 \\\\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad D = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\\\ 0 &amp; 2 &amp; 0 \\\\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 \\end{pmatrix}.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"building-a-graph-from-ordinary-data\" data-h=\"3\">Building a graph from ordinary data</h3>\n<p>Even when data arrives as a plain table, we can create a graph from it. The most common recipe is the <strong><span class=\"m\">k</span>-nearest-neighbour graph</strong>: make each data point a vertex and join it to the <span class=\"m\">k</span> points closest to it. Another is the <strong>similarity graph</strong>, where every pair of points <span class=\"m\">i, j</span> gets an edge with weight <span class=\"m\">w_{ij} = e^{-\\|x_i - x_j\\|^2 / (2\\sigma^2)}</span>, close to 1 for nearby points and close to 0 for distant ones. For example, with <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 1</span>, two points at distance 1 get weight <span class=\"m\">e^{-0.5} \\approx 0.61</span> and two points at distance 3 get <span class=\"m\">e^{-4.5} \\approx 0.011</span>. The graph keeps only local information, \"who is near whom\", and forgets the global coordinates. That is exactly what we want when the data lies on a curved surface, like points on a rolled-up sheet of paper, where straight-line distance is misleading but distances along the graph follow the sheet. Methods such as Isomap, UMAP and spectral clustering all start by building such a graph and then analyse it with the tools below.</p>\n<h3 id=\"random-walks-and-pagerank\" data-h=\"3\">Random walks and PageRank</h3>\n<p>Imagine a web surfer who, on each page, clicks a random link. The probability of moving from <span class=\"m\">i</span> to <span class=\"m\">j</span> is <span class=\"m\">A_{ij}/\\deg^+(i)</span>, so the transition matrix is <span class=\"m\">P = D^{-1}A</span>, a Markov chain on the graph. After many clicks, the fraction of time the surfer spends on each page settles down to a <strong>stationary distribution</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\pi</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\pi = \\pi P</span>. Pages that are linked to by many important pages are visited more, which is exactly what \"important\" should mean.</p>\n<p>Google's <strong>PageRank</strong> adds one twist: with probability <span class=\"m\">1 - d</span> (typically <span class=\"m\">d = 0.85</span>) the surfer gets bored and jumps to a uniformly random page. This guarantees the chain has a unique stationary distribution and handles dead ends. The update is\n<span class=\"mb\">PR(v) = \\frac{1 - d}{n} + d \\sum_{u \\to v} \\frac{PR(u)}{\\deg^+(u)}.</span>\nFor three pages with links <span class=\"m\">A \\to B</span>, <span class=\"m\">A \\to C</span>, <span class=\"m\">B \\to C</span>, <span class=\"m\">C \\to A</span> and starting scores <span class=\"m\">\\tfrac{1}{3}</span> each, one update gives <span class=\"m\">PR(A) = 0.05 + 0.85 \\cdot \\tfrac{1}{3} \\approx 0.333</span>, <span class=\"m\">PR(B) = 0.05 + 0.85 \\cdot \\tfrac{1}{6} \\approx 0.192</span>, <span class=\"m\">PR(C) = 0.05 + 0.85 \\cdot (\\tfrac{1}{6} + \\tfrac{1}{3}) = 0.475</span>. Page C, linked by both others, rises; repeated updates converge to the PageRank vector.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-graph-laplacian\" data-h=\"3\">The graph Laplacian</h3>\n<p>The single most important matrix in graph-based ML is the <strong>Laplacian</strong> <span class=\"m\">L = D - A</span>. Its magic is the quadratic form\n<span class=\"mb\">x^\\top L x = \\sum_{\\{i,j\\} \\in E} (x_i - x_j)^2.</span>\nIf <span class=\"m\">x</span> assigns a number to each vertex, <span class=\"m\">x^\\top L x</span> measures how much <span class=\"m\">x</span> changes across edges: it is small when neighbours have similar values, i.e. when <span class=\"m\">x</span> is <strong>smooth</strong> on the graph. For the path above with <span class=\"m\">x = (1, 0, -1)</span>, the edges contribute <span class=\"m\">(1 - 0)^2 + (0 - (-1))^2 = 2</span>.</p>\n<p>Consequences follow quickly. Every row of <span class=\"m\">L</span> sums to 0, so the all-ones vector has <span class=\"m\">L\\mathbf{1} = 0</span>: eigenvalue 0. Since <span class=\"m\">x^\\top L x \\ge 0</span>, all eigenvalues are non-negative. And <span class=\"m\">x^\\top L x = 0</span> exactly when <span class=\"m\">x</span> is constant on each connected component, so <strong>the number of zero eigenvalues of <span class=\"m\">L</span> equals the number of connected components</strong>. For the path <span class=\"m\">1 - 2 - 3</span> the eigenvalues are 0, 1 and 3, with eigenvectors <span class=\"m\">(1,1,1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(1,0,-1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1,-2,1)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"spectral-clustering\" data-h=\"3\">Spectral clustering</h3>\n<p>If a graph is <em>almost</em> disconnected, two clusters joined by a few edges, then <span class=\"m\">L</span> has one eigenvalue near 0 besides the true 0. Its eigenvector (the <strong>Fiedler vector</strong>, for the second-smallest eigenvalue) is nearly constant on each cluster with different values, so the sign of each entry tells you which cluster the vertex belongs to. <strong>Spectral clustering</strong> builds a similarity graph on the data, computes the first few eigenvectors of the Laplacian, and clusters the points using those eigenvector coordinates. It finds clusters of strange shapes, like two interlocking rings, that <span class=\"m\">k</span>-means on the raw coordinates cannot.</p>\n<h3 id=\"graph-neural-networks\" data-h=\"3\">Graph neural networks</h3>\n<p>A <strong>graph neural network</strong> gives every vertex a feature vector <span class=\"m\">h_v</span> (an atom's type, a user's profile) and updates it by <strong>message passing</strong>: each vertex collects its neighbours' vectors, aggregates them (sum, mean or max), and combines the result with its own vector through a learned layer:\n<span class=\"mb\">h_v^{(k+1)} = \\sigma\\Big(W^{(k)} \\cdot \\operatorname{mean}\\{h_u^{(k)} : u \\in N(v) \\cup \\{v\\}\\}\\Big).</span>\nAfter <span class=\"m\">k</span> layers, <span class=\"m\">h_v</span> depends on everything within distance <span class=\"m\">k</span>, the BFS ball of radius <span class=\"m\">k</span>, which is the same spreading that <span class=\"m\">A^k</span> describes. The popular GCN layer uses the normalised matrix <span class=\"m\">\\hat{A} = \\tilde{D}^{-1/2}(A + I)\\tilde{D}^{-1/2}</span>, where <span class=\"m\">\\tilde D</span> is the degree matrix of <span class=\"m\">A + I</span>, and computes <span class=\"m\">H^{(k+1)} = \\sigma(\\hat{A} H^{(k)} W^{(k)})</span>. Aggregation does not depend on how vertices are numbered, so GNNs respect the graph's structure: relabelling the vertices relabels the outputs the same way.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Do not feed an adjacency matrix row as an ordinary feature vector to a standard network: the result changes when vertices are renumbered. Remember that the Laplacian's smallest eigenvalue is always 0, so the useful clustering information is in the <em>next</em> eigenvectors. And stacking too many GNN layers makes all vertex features converge to the same value (<strong>over-smoothing</strong>), because repeated averaging is exactly the smoothing that the Laplacian measures.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Graphs become matrices: <span class=\"m\">A</span> counts walks, <span class=\"m\">D</span> holds degrees, <span class=\"m\">P = D^{-1}A</span> is a random walk, and PageRank is its damped stationary distribution. The Laplacian <span class=\"m\">L = D - A</span> measures smoothness, <span class=\"m\">x^\\top L x = \\sum (x_i - x_j)^2</span>; its zero eigenvalues count components and its next eigenvectors reveal clusters. GNNs pass messages along edges so that <span class=\"m\">k</span> layers see <span class=\"m\">k</span>-hop neighbourhoods.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;grid&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;walks&quot;, &quot;k&quot;: 2}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Powers of the adjacency matrix: how far information spreads in k steps.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With walk length <span class=\"m\">k = 2</span> and start vertex 0 (a corner of the grid), read the row of <span class=\"m\">A^2</span>: 2 walks back to 0, and 2 walks to the centre vertex 4.\n2. Increase <span class=\"m\">k</span> step by step. Note which vertices have non-zero counts: after <span class=\"m\">k</span> steps only vertices within distance <span class=\"m\">k</span> can be reached, and on this bipartite grid only those at the right parity.\n3. Click the centre vertex 4 to start there. Its counts grow fastest, because it has the highest degree.\n4. Think of each count as how strongly a GNN with <span class=\"m\">k</span> layers mixes that vertex's features into vertex 0's.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> <span class=\"m\">A^k</span> is the \"receptive field\" of a <span class=\"m\">k</span>-layer message-passing network, the same BFS ball from earlier lessons. High-degree vertices receive and send many more walks, which is why GCNs normalise by degree.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g10-graph-lab\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;disconnected&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;components&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Components and the Laplacian's zero eigenvalues.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Read the number of components: 3 (a triangle, a path of three vertices, and an isolated vertex).\n2. For each component, imagine the vector that is 1 on that component and 0 elsewhere. Every edge has both ends equal, so <span class=\"m\">x^\\top L x = 0</span>: three independent zero-eigenvectors.\n3. Turn on edit mode and add one edge between the triangle and the path. The count drops to 2, and so does the number of zero eigenvalues.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the multiplicity of eigenvalue 0 of <span class=\"m\">L</span> is exactly the number of components. Spectral clustering looks for eigenvalues that are <em>almost</em> 0, signalling components that are almost separate.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">L = D - A, \\qquad x^\\top L x = \\sum_{\\{i,j\\} \\in E} w_{ij}(x_i - x_j)^2 \\ge 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\{\\text{zero eigenvalues of } L\\} = \\#\\text{components}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P = D^{-1}A, \\qquad \\pi = \\pi P</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">PR(v) = \\frac{1-d}{n} + d \\sum_{u \\to v} \\frac{PR(u)}{\\deg^+(u)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H^{(k+1)} = \\sigma\\big(\\tilde{D}^{-1/2}(A + I)\\tilde{D}^{-1/2} H^{(k)} W^{(k)}\\big)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Laplacian quadratic form</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For an undirected graph with Laplacian <span class=\"m\">L = D - A</span>, <span class=\"m\">x^\\top L x = \\sum_{\\{i,j\\} \\in E}(x_i - x_j)^2</span> for every vector <span class=\"m\">x</span>. Hence <span class=\"m\">L</span> is positive semidefinite, and the dimension of its null space equals the number of connected components.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">x^\\top D x = \\sum_i \\deg(i)\\, x_i^2</span> and <span class=\"m\">x^\\top A x = \\sum_{i,j} A_{ij} x_i x_j = 2\\sum_{\\{i,j\\}\\in E} x_i x_j</span>. Each edge <span class=\"m\">\\{i,j\\}</span> contributes <span class=\"m\">x_i^2 + x_j^2</span> to the first sum, so <span class=\"m\">x^\\top L x = \\sum_{\\{i,j\\} \\in E}(x_i^2 + x_j^2 - 2x_i x_j) = \\sum_{\\{i,j\\}\\in E} (x_i - x_j)^2 \\ge 0</span>. This is 0 exactly when <span class=\"m\">x_i = x_j</span> across every edge, i.e. <span class=\"m\">x</span> is constant on each component. Those vectors form a space spanned by the component indicator vectors, of dimension equal to the number of components.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute the Laplacian of the path <span class=\"m\">1 - 2 - 3</span> and check an eigenvector</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">D = \\operatorname{diag}(1, 2, 1)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">L = D - A = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; -1 &amp; 0 \\\\ -1 &amp; 2 &amp; -1 \\\\ 0 &amp; -1 &amp; 1 \\end{pmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li>Row sums are <span class=\"m\">0, 0, 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">L\\mathbf{1} = 0</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L(1, 0, -1)^\\top = (1, 0, -1)^\\top</span>: eigenvalue 1, the Fiedler vector, separating vertex 1 from vertex 3.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L(1, -2, 1)^\\top = (3, -6, 3)^\\top</span>: eigenvalue 3. Check: <span class=\"m\">0 + 1 + 3 = 4 = \\operatorname{trace} L</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">One mean-aggregation GNN step on the path <span class=\"m\">1 - 2 - 3</span> with scalar features <span class=\"m\">h = (1, 0, 0)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Vertex 1 averages itself and vertex 2: <span class=\"m\">(1 + 0)/2 = 0.5</span>.</li>\n<li>Vertex 2 averages vertices 1, 2, 3: <span class=\"m\">(1 + 0 + 0)/3 = 1/3</span>.</li>\n<li>Vertex 3 averages itself and vertex 2: <span class=\"m\">(0 + 0)/2 = 0</span>.</li>\n<li>New features <span class=\"m\">(0.5, 0.333, 0)</span>: the signal at vertex 1 has spread one hop. After a second step it reaches vertex 3.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(a) Write the Laplacian of the triangle <span class=\"m\">K_3</span> and find <span class=\"m\">x^\\top L x</span> for <span class=\"m\">x = (1, 2, 4)</span>. (b) A graph's Laplacian has eigenvalues <span class=\"m\">0, 0, 0, 1.2, 3.5, 4.1</span>. How many connected components does it have? (c) With <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span> pages and <span class=\"m\">d = 0.85</span>, what is the PageRank of a page that no other page links to (after one update)?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">L = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 &amp; -1 &amp; -1 \\\\ -1 &amp; 2 &amp; -1 \\\\ -1 &amp; -1 &amp; 2\\end{pmatrix}</span>; <span class=\"m\">x^\\top L x = (1-2)^2 + (2-4)^2 + (1-4)^2 = 1 + 4 + 9 = 14</span>. (b) Three zero eigenvalues: 3 components. (c) Only the random-jump term remains: <span class=\"m\">(1 - 0.85)/4 = 0.0375</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>GNNs predict molecular properties for drug discovery, detect fraud in transaction networks, recommend content (PinSage at Pinterest), and forecast travel times (Google Maps uses GNNs on road segments). Knowledge-graph embeddings such as TransE learn vectors so that \"head + relation ≈ tail\". Label propagation and graph-regularised semi-supervised learning add the penalty <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\, f^\\top L f</span> so predictions are smooth across similar examples. Transformers can be viewed as GNNs on a complete graph, where attention weights are learned edge weights.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>PageRank and its relatives rank web pages, academic papers and influential accounts. Community detection in social networks uses spectral methods and modularity. Graph databases and query engines store knowledge graphs for search and question answering.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>When a map app estimates your arrival time, it treats roads as a graph whose edges carry live traffic speeds, and models predict how congestion on one road spreads to its neighbours, which is message passing on the road network.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What does every row of a graph Laplacian <span class=\"m\">L = D - A</span> sum to?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The diagonal entry is the degree and the row contains <span class=\"m\">-1</span> once per neighbour.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A graph's Laplacian has eigenvalue 0 with multiplicity 4. How many connected components?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The null space is spanned by the component indicator vectors.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For the path <span class=\"m\">1 - 2 - 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">x = (2, 2, 5)</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">x^\\top L x</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"9\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(2-2)^2 + (2-5)^2 = 9</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">After 3 message-passing layers, a vertex's representation can depend on vertices up to what distance?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each layer adds one hop.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In PageRank with <span class=\"m\">d = 0.85</span> and <span class=\"m\">n = 100</span> pages, what is the random-jump term <span class=\"m\">(1-d)/n</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.0015|3/2000\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">0.15/100 = 0.0015</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which eigenvector does spectral clustering use to split a graph into two parts?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the eigenvector for the largest eigenvalue of <span class=\"m\">L</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the eigenvector for the second-smallest eigenvalue of <span class=\"m\">L</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the all-ones vector</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The smallest eigenvalue's eigenvector is constant and carries no cluster information.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>"}, "formulas": [{"l": "graphs-intro", "t": "What is a graph?", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">G = (V, E), \\qquad n = |V|, \\qquad m = |E|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{simple graph: } 0 \\le m \\le \\binom n2 = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A_{ij} = \\begin{cases}1 &amp; ij \\in E\\\\ 0 &amp; \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}, \\qquad A = A^\\top \\text{ for undirected graphs}</span></p>"}, {"l": "degree-handshake", "t": "Degree and the handshake lemma", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\sum_{v\\in V}\\deg(v) = 2m \\qquad \\text{(handshake lemma)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\{v : \\deg(v) \\text{ odd}\\} \\text{ is even}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\delta(G) \\le \\frac{2m}{n} \\le \\Delta(G)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">k\\text{-regular: } m = \\frac{nk}{2}; \\quad K_n: m = \\frac{n(n-1)}2; \\quad C_n: m = n; \\quad P_n: m = n-1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{directed: } \\sum_v \\deg^+(v) = \\sum_v \\deg^-(v) = m</span></p>"}, {"l": "paths-connectivity", "t": "Walks, paths, cycles and connectivity", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{walk} \\supseteq \\text{trail} \\supseteq \\text{path}; \\qquad \\text{length} = \\text{number of edges}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">d(u,w) \\le d(u,v) + d(v,w)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{connected} \\Rightarrow m \\ge n - 1; \\qquad \\text{in general } m \\ge n - c</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(A^k)_{ij} = \\#\\{\\text{walks of length } k \\text{ from } i \\text{ to } j\\}, \\qquad (A^2)_{ii} = \\deg(i), \\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(A^3) = 6 \\cdot \\#\\text{triangles}</span></p>"}, {"l": "trees", "t": "Trees", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{tree on } n \\text{ vertices: } m = n - 1; \\qquad \\text{forest with } c \\text{ trees: } m = n - c</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{binary tree of height } h: \\; n \\le 2^{h+1} - 1 \\iff h \\ge \\log_2(n+1) - 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Cayley: } \\#\\{\\text{labelled trees on } n \\text{ vertices}\\} = n^{n-2}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{leaves in a tree: } \\#\\text{leaves} = 2 + \\sum_{\\deg v \\ge 3} (\\deg v - 2)</span></p>"}, {"l": "bfs-dfs", "t": "Breadth-first and depth-first search", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{BFS: } d(v) = d(\\text{parent}(v)) + 1, \\quad d(s) = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{time with adjacency lists: } O(n) + O\\Big(\\sum_v \\deg v\\Big) = O(n + 2m) = O(n + m)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{time with adjacency matrix: } O(n^2)</span></p>"}, {"l": "shortest-paths", "t": "Shortest paths with weights", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{relax}(u,v): \\; d(v) \\leftarrow \\min\\big(d(v),\\; d(u) + w(u,v)\\big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{dist}(s,v) = \\min_{u:\\,(u,v)\\in E}\\big(\\operatorname{dist}(s,u) + w(u,v)\\big) \\quad \\text{(Bellman equation)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Dijkstra (heap): } O((n+m)\\log n); \\quad \\text{Bellman–Ford: } O(nm); \\quad \\text{Floyd–Warshall: } O(n^3)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">d_k(i,j) = \\min\\big(d_{k-1}(i,j),\\, d_{k-1}(i,k) + d_{k-1}(k,j)\\big)</span></p>"}, {"l": "spanning-trees", "t": "Minimum spanning trees", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">|E(T)| = n - 1 \\text{ for any spanning tree } T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">w(T) = \\sum_{e \\in T} w(e)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{exchange: } w(T + e - f) = w(T) + w(e) - w(f)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Kruskal: } O(m \\log m), \\qquad \\text{Prim (heap): } O(m \\log n)</span></p>"}, {"l": "bipartite", "t": "Bipartite graphs", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">G \\text{ bipartite} \\iff G \\text{ has a proper 2-colouring} \\iff G \\text{ has no odd cycle}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">|E(K_{a,b})| = ab, \\qquad |E| \\le \\left\\lfloor \\tfrac{n^2}{4} \\right\\rfloor \\text{ for bipartite } G \\text{ on } n \\text{ vertices}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_{v \\in L} \\deg v = \\sum_{v \\in R} \\deg v = m</span></p>"}, {"l": "matching", "t": "Matchings, Hall's theorem and König's theorem", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">|M| \\le |C| \\text{ for any matching } M \\text{ and vertex cover } C</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{bipartite: } \\max |M| = \\min |C| \\quad \\text{(König)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Hall: } L \\text{ can be fully matched} \\iff |N(S)| \\ge |S| \\;\\; \\forall S \\subseteq L</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{augmenting path with } k \\text{ matched edges has } k+1 \\text{ unmatched edges: } |M \\oplus P| = |M| + 1</span></p>"}, {"l": "euler-hamilton", "t": "Euler and Hamilton: using every edge or every vertex", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Euler circuit} \\iff \\text{connected and every } \\deg v \\text{ even}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Euler trail} \\iff \\text{connected and exactly 0 or 2 vertices of odd degree}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Dirac: } n \\ge 3,\\; \\delta(G) \\ge \\tfrac{n}{2} \\Rightarrow \\text{Hamiltonian}; \\qquad \\text{Ore: } \\deg u + \\deg v \\ge n \\text{ for all non-adjacent } u,v</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\text{Hamilton cycles in } K_n = \\frac{(n-1)!}{2}</span></p>"}, {"l": "planarity", "t": "Planar graphs and Euler's formula", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">V - E + F = 2 \\quad \\text{(connected plane graph)}, \\qquad V - E + F = 1 + c \\quad (c \\text{ components})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_{\\text{faces}} \\deg(f) = 2E</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E \\le 3V - 6 \\;\\; (V \\ge 3), \\qquad E \\le 2V - 4 \\;\\; \\text{(no triangles)}, \\qquad E \\le \\tfrac{g}{g-2}(V - 2) \\;\\; \\text{(shortest cycle } g)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{planar} \\Rightarrow \\exists v: \\deg v \\le 5</span></p>"}, {"l": "colouring", "t": "Graph colouring", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\omega(G) \\le \\chi(G) \\le \\Delta(G) + 1, \\qquad \\chi(G) \\ge \\frac{n}{\\alpha(G)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\chi(K_n) = n, \\quad \\chi(C_{2k}) = 2, \\quad \\chi(C_{2k+1}) = 3, \\quad \\chi(\\text{tree}) = 2 \\;(n \\ge 2)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Brooks: } \\chi(G) \\le \\Delta(G) \\text{ unless } G \\text{ is complete or an odd cycle}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{planar} \\Rightarrow \\chi(G) \\le 4</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{number of proper } k\\text{-colourings: } P(K_n, k) = k(k-1)\\cdots(k-n+1), \\quad P(\\text{tree on } n, k) = k(k-1)^{n-1}</span></p>"}, {"l": "directed-graphs", "t": "Directed graphs, DAGs and topological sort", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\sum_v \\deg^+(v) = \\sum_v \\deg^-(v) = m</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{topological order exists} \\iff \\text{no directed cycle}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{finish}(v) = d(v) + \\max_{(u,v)\\in E} \\text{finish}(u) \\quad (\\max \\text{ over nothing} = 0)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Kahn and DFS topological sort: } O(n + m)</span></p>"}, {"l": "network-flows", "t": "Network flows and the max-flow min-cut theorem", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">0 \\le f(u,v) \\le c(u,v), \\qquad \\sum_{u} f(u,v) = \\sum_{w} f(v,w) \\;\\; (v \\ne s,t)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">|f| = \\sum_v f(s,v) - \\sum_v f(v,s)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">c_f(u,v) = c(u,v) - f(u,v), \\qquad c_f(v,u) = f(u,v) \\quad \\text{(residual capacities)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">c(S,T) = \\sum_{u \\in S,\\, v \\in T} c(u,v), \\qquad |f| \\le c(S,T)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\max_f |f| = \\min_{(S,T)} c(S,T); \\qquad \\text{Edmonds–Karp: } O(nm^2)</span></p>"}, {"l": "graphs-ml", "t": "Graphs in machine learning", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">L = D - A, \\qquad x^\\top L x = \\sum_{\\{i,j\\} \\in E} w_{ij}(x_i - x_j)^2 \\ge 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\{\\text{zero eigenvalues of } L\\} = \\#\\text{components}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P = D^{-1}A, \\qquad \\pi = \\pi P</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">PR(v) = \\frac{1-d}{n} + d \\sum_{u \\to v} \\frac{PR(u)}{\\deg^+(u)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H^{(k+1)} = \\sigma\\big(\\tilde{D}^{-1/2}(A + I)\\tilde{D}^{-1/2} H^{(k)} W^{(k)}\\big)</span></p>"}]});
